nfluenza when antiviral treatment would change management", "only_if": "influenza is circulating and the patient presents within the treatment window"}, {"test": "Nasopharyngeal swab for respiratory viruses (multiplex PCR)", "why": "Identifies a specific viral cause when the result would change management", "only_if": "severe illness, immunocompromised patient, or an outbreak setting"}, {"test": "Pertussis PCR or culture (nasopharyngeal swab)", "why": "Confirms whooping cough when paroxysmal cough, post-tussive vomiting or a prolonged cough suggests it", "only_if": "pertussis is suspected"}, {"test": "Sputum Gram stain and culture", "why": "Looks for a bacterial cause when bacterial infection is a real consideration", "only_if": "purulent sputum with a toxic appearance or a suspected bacterial pathogen"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "cbc", "why": "Rarely changes management in an uncomplicated cough illness"}, {"test": "bmp", "why": "Not needed unless the patient is dehydrated or severely ill"}, {"test": "crp", "why": "Not required for diagnosis; does not reliably separate viral bronchitis from pneumonia"}, {"test": "procalcitonin", "why": "Not routine; reserve for uncertain cases or antibiotic decisions"}, {"test": "Chest x-ray, posteroanterior and lateral", "why": "Not needed when the presentation is typical and pneumonia is not suspected"}, {"test": "CT chest without IV contrast", "why": "Not indicated for uncomplicated acute bronchitis"}, {"test": "Sputum Gram stain and culture", "why": "Not routine; most cases are viral"}, {"test": "Rapid influenza test", "why": "Not routine outside influenza season or when antivirals would not be used"}, {"test": "d_dimer", "why": "No role unless pulmonary embolism is being considered"}, {"test": "bnp", "why": "No role unless heart failure is being considered"}], "decision_points": ["The diagnosis is clinical; imaging is only needed when pneumonia or another process is suspected", "A normal chest x-ray in a patient with a compatible history supports acute bronchitis", "Antibiotics are not indicated for viral acute bronchitis; reserve them for a confirmed or strongly suspected bacterial cause such as pertussis", "Consider pertussis when the cough is paroxysmal, lasts more than 2 weeks, or is accompanied by post-tussive vomiting"], "criteria": [], "scores": []}, "differential": [{"name": "Community-acquired pneumonia", "slug": "community-acquired-pneumonia", "why_considered": "The single most important look-alike: cough, fever, sputum and chest discomfort overlap completely with acute bronchitis.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Fever above 100.4°F, tachypnea, hypoxemia, focal crackles, dullness to percussion or egophony", "discriminators": {"history": ["Higher fever with shaking chills", "Shortness of breath at rest or with minimal activity", "Pleuritic chest pain worse with deep breath", "Feeling systemically very unwell rather than just a bad cold"], "exam": ["Focal crackles over one area", "Dullness to percussion", "Increased tactile fremitus and egophony", "Tachypnea and use of accessory muscles"], "labs": ["Leukocytosis with neutrophilia", "Elevated procalcitonin (above 0.25–0.5 ng/mL)", "Hypoxemia on pulse oximetry or arterial blood gas"], "imaging": ["Lobar or segmental consolidation on chest x-ray", "Air bronchograms or pleural effusion"]}}, {"name": "Influenza", "slug": "influenza", "why_considered": "A leading viral cause of acute bronchitis; presents with cough plus systemic symptoms, especially in winter.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Abrupt onset over hours rather than days", "High fever, severe myalgias, headache and prostration", "Cough may be dry and non-productive early", "Known community outbreak or sick contacts"], "exam": ["High fever and toxic appearance", "Diffuse pharyngeal redness without exudate", "Lung exam often clear or with minimal findings"], "labs": ["Positive rapid influenza antigen or molecular test", "Leukopenia or lymphopenia"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray normal unless complicated by pneumonia"]}}, {"name": "Asthma exacerbation", "slug": "asthma", "why_considered": "Cough, wheeze and chest tightness are shared, and viral infections commonly trigger asthma attacks.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Recurrent episodes of wheeze and cough since childhood", "Known asthma diagnosis or prior inhaler use", "Symptoms triggered by allergens, exercise or cold air", "Nocturnal cough and wheeze"], "exam": ["Diffuse expiratory wheezing", "Prolonged expiratory phase", "Signs of atopy such as allergic rhinitis"], "labs": ["Peripheral eosinophilia", "Elevated total IgE"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray normal or hyperinflated"]}}, {"name": "COPD exacerbation", "slug": "copd", "why_considered": "Chronic productive cough with acute worsening, common in smokers and older adults.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Long-standing daily cough and sputum for years", "Progressive exertional dyspnea", "Heavy smoking history", "Prior COPD diagnosis or inhaler use"], "exam": ["Barrel chest, prolonged expiration", "Diffuse wheeze and distant breath sounds", "Use of accessory muscles at baseline"], "labs": ["Elevated CO2 on blood gas in advanced disease", "Polycythemia in chronic hypoxemia"], "imaging": ["Hyperinflation with flattened diaphragms", "Increased anteroposterior chest diameter"]}}, {"name": "Pertussis (whooping cough)", "slug": "pertussis", "why_considered": "Prolonged cough illness that can be mistaken for viral bronchitis, especially when the cough lingers for weeks.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Paroxysmal cough lasting more than 2 weeks, post-tussive vomiting or inspiratory whoop", "discriminators": {"history": ["Cough comes in violent paroxysms", "Post-tussive vomiting or whoop", "Cough lasting more than 2 weeks", "Close contact with a person who has a prolonged cough", "Incomplete or unknown vaccination"], "exam": ["Coughing spells during the visit", "Conjunctival hemorrhage or facial petechiae from coughing", "Lung exam often normal between spells"], "labs": ["Lymphocytosis with markedly elevated lymphocyte count", "Positive pertussis PCR or culture from nasopharyngeal swab"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray usually normal"]}}, {"name": "Tuberculosis", "slug": "tuberculosis", "why_considered": "Chronic cough with sputum can mimic a prolonged bronchitis, particularly in higher-risk patients.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Cough lasting more than 3 weeks with night sweats, weight loss, hemoptysis or a compatible exposure history", "discriminators": {"history": ["Cough for weeks to months", "Night sweats, fever and weight loss", "Hemoptysis", "Born in or travel to a high-prevalence country, incarceration, homelessness or HIV infection"], "exam": ["Cachexia and general wasting", "Upper-lobe crackles or amphoric breath sounds", "Lymphadenopathy"], "labs": ["Positive interferon-gamma release assay or tuberculin skin test", "Acid-fast bacilli on sputum smear", "Positive nucleic acid amplification test for Mycobacterium tuberculosis"], "imaging": ["Upper-lobe infiltrates, cavitation or hilar adenopathy on chest x-ray"]}}, {"name": "Gastroesophageal reflux disease", "slug": "gastroesophageal-reflux-disease", "why_considered": "Chronic cough can be caused by reflux, and patients may report chest burning that is attributed to bronchitis.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Heartburn, regurgitation or water brash", "Cough worse after meals or when lying down", "No preceding viral illness", "Symptoms improve with acid suppression"], "exam": ["Often normal chest exam", "No fever or systemic symptoms"], "labs": ["No specific laboratory abnormality"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray normal", "Ambulatory pH monitoring shows reflux if performed"]}}, {"name": "Postnasal drip syndrome (upper airway cough syndrome)", "slug": "upper-airway-cough-syndrome", "why_considered": "A common cause of persistent cough that can follow or accompany a cold.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Constant need to clear the throat", "Nasal congestion and discharge", "Cough worse at night or on waking", "No fever or chest pain"], "exam": ["Cobblestone appearance of the posterior pharynx", "Mucoid or purulent nasal discharge", "Normal lung exam"], "labs": ["No specific laboratory abnormality"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray normal", "Sinus imaging shows mucosal thickening if sinusitis is present"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Cough with clear or white sputum, no fever or low-grade fever, normal vital signs, no dyspnea at rest, able to carry out daily activities", "moderate": "More frequent cough with colored sputum, low-grade fever, mild tachypnea, chest tightness and wheeze, some disruption of sleep and work", "severe": "Persistent cough with significant chest discomfort, fever up to about 101.8°F, tachypnea, wheezing and reduced activity, but no hypoxemia or respiratory distress at rest", "critical": "Not a feature of uncomplicated acute bronchitis; respiratory failure, marked hypoxemia or toxic appearance should prompt reassessment for pneumonia, sepsis or another diagnosis"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Shortness of breath at rest or hypoxemia (SpO2 below 92%)", "meaning": "Suggests pneumonia, pulmonary embolism or another serious cause rather than simple acute bronchitis"}, {"finding": "High fever above 101.8°F with shaking chills", "meaning": "Points to pneumonia or another bacterial infection"}, {"finding": "Hemoptysis", "meaning": "Requires evaluation for tuberculosis, malignancy, bronchiectasis or pulmonary embolism"}, {"finding": "Cough lasting more than 8 weeks", "meaning": "No longer acute bronchitis; evaluate for pertussis, asthma, reflux, postnasal drip or other chronic causes"}, {"finding": "Night sweats and weight loss", "meaning": "Raises concern for tuberculosis or malignancy"}, {"finding": "Focal crackles, dullness to percussion or egophony", "meaning": "Suggests consolidation from pneumonia"}, {"finding": "Toxic appearance or altered mental status", "meaning": "Suggests sepsis or severe pneumonia; needs urgent evaluation"}], "complications": [{"name": "Secondary bacterial pneumonia", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Recurrent fever after initial improvement", "New focal crackles or dullness", "Worsening dyspnea", "New consolidation on chest x-ray"]}, {"name": "Post-tussive vomiting and rib pain", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Vomiting immediately after coughing spells", "Chest wall soreness reproducible on palpation"]}, {"name": "Rib fracture from severe coughing", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Focal chest wall pain worse with deep breath or movement", "Point tenderness over a rib", "Radiographic rib fracture"]}, {"name": "Exacerbation of underlying asthma or COPD", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Increasing wheeze and dyspnea", "Reduced response to rescue inhaler", "Decline in peak expiratory flow"]}, {"name": "Syncope with coughing", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Loss of consciousness immediately after a coughing paroxysm", "Rapid recovery without confusion"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Young children cannot describe sputum or chest tightness; they present with a lingering cough after a cold, sometimes with vomiting after coughing spells. Wheezing is more prominent and the cough may be mistaken for asthma or a foreign body."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Fever may be blunted or absent and the cough can be less forceful. Symptoms overlap more with pneumonia, heart failure and COPD, so the threshold for chest imaging is lower."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "The same symptoms can progress to pneumonia or be caused by unusual organisms; fever and systemic symptoms may be more prominent and the course more prolonged."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Presentation is similar, but cough and shortness of breath are more alarming because of the risk to the fetus; treatment choices are limited and pertussis should be considered."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "How long have you had the cough, and how did it start?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Gradual onset a few days after a cold; cough present for about 1–3 weeks, not more than 8 weeks"}, {"question": "Are you coughing anything up, and what does it look like?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Clear, white or colored sputum; purulent sputum alone does not mean bacterial infection"}, {"question": "Have you had a fever, and how high did it get?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Absent or low-grade fever; high fever with shaking chills suggests pneumonia"}, {"question": "Are you short of breath at rest or with minimal activity?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Absent; dyspnea at rest or hypoxemia points away from simple acute bronchitis"}, {"question": "Have you coughed up any blood?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Absent; hemoptysis requires evaluation for tuberculosis, malignancy, bronchiectasis or pulmonary embolism"}, {"question": "Does the chest pain get worse when you take a deep breath or lie flat?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Chest soreness from coughing rather than pleuritic pain; pleuritic pain suggests pneumonia or pulmonary embolism"}, {"question": "Have you had a runny or stuffy nose, sore throat or hoarse voice?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recent or current upper respiratory symptoms supporting a viral cause"}, {"question": "Have you been wheezing, or do you have a history of asthma or COPD?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Wheeze can occur with bronchitis; known asthma or COPD raises concern for an exacerbation instead"}, {"question": "Do you smoke, or are you around secondhand smoke, dust, fumes or vapors?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Smoking or irritant exposure as a risk factor and a reason for a prolonged cough"}, {"question": "Have you had night sweats, weight loss or a cough lasting more than 3 weeks?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Absent; present raises concern for tuberculosis or malignancy"}, {"question": "Has anyone around you had a similar cough, or has anyone been diagnosed with whooping cough?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Sick contacts; a contact with prolonged cough suggests pertussis"}, {"question": "Does the cough come in violent spells, and do you vomit or make a whooping sound afterward?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Paroxysmal cough with post-tussive vomiting or whoop suggests pertussis"}, {"question": "Have you been hospitalized recently or taken antibiotics?", "category": "medications", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Recent healthcare exposure or antibiotics, which would broaden the differential"}, {"question": "Do you have heartburn or a cough that is worse after meals or when lying down?", "category": "ros", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Reflux symptoms that would suggest gastroesophageal reflux as the cause of cough"}], "accepted_names": ["Acute bronchitis", "Bronchitis", "Chest cold", "Acute chest cold", "Viral bronchitis"], "reasoning_points": ["A cough that began gradually after a cold and has lasted about 1–3 weeks, often productive of clear, white or colored sputum", "Low-grade or no fever with sore throat, nasal congestion, hoarseness and fatigue supporting a viral upper respiratory illness", "Chest soreness or tightness from coughing rather than pleuritic pain, and no dyspnea at rest", "Lung exam with wheezing or rhonchi that clears with coughing, and no focal crackles, dullness to percussion or egophony", "Normal or near-normal vital signs without hypoxemia, tachypnea or high fever", "Chest x-ray not required when the presentation is typical; when obtained it is normal without consolidation", "Pneumonia excluded by the absence of high fever, focal lung findings, hypoxemia and systemic toxicity", "Pertussis considered and excluded when the cough is not paroxysmal and there is no post-tussive vomiting or whoop", "Asthma or COPD exacerbation considered and excluded by the absence of a compatible chronic history", "Antibiotics are not indicated for viral acute bronchitis; treatment is supportive"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Young or middle-aged adult with a cough for 1–2 weeks after a cold", "Clear or white sputum, low-grade or no fever, normal vital signs", "Diffuse wheeze or rhonchi that clears with coughing and no focal crackles", "No dyspnea at rest and no hypoxemia"], "medium": ["Patient with purulent yellow-green sputum and mild fever, raising the question of antibiotics", "Wheezing in a patient with no prior asthma history", "Cough persisting 3–6 weeks after other cold symptoms have resolved", "Older adult or smoker with overlapping COPD symptoms"], "hard": ["Older adult with blunted fever and a less forceful cough, where pneumonia must be excluded", "Patient with underlying asthma or COPD where an exacerbation is the more likely diagnosis", "Prolonged cough with post-tussive vomiting or whoop suggesting pertussis", "Cough lasting more than 8 weeks, which is no longer acute bronchitis", "Immunocompromised or pregnant patient with cough and dyspnea, where the threshold for imaging is lower"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
