depressants or metformin that can cause dry mouth"}, {"question": "Have you had any fever, facial swelling, trouble swallowing or loose teeth?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Signs of a deep dental or sinus infection or airway compromise that need urgent evaluation"}, {"question": "Do you have diabetes, kidney disease or liver disease?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Conditions that can produce a fruity, ammonia-like or musty breath odor"}, {"question": "Have you had any weight loss, excessive thirst or frequent urination?", "category": "ros", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Symptoms suggesting an underlying metabolic cause such as hyperglycemia"}], "accepted_names": ["Bad breath", "Halitosis", "Oral malodor", "Fetor oris"], "reasoning_points": ["Bad breath is diagnosed clinically from the history and a focused oral, nasal and pharyngeal examination; routine laboratory testing is not required.", "The most common causes are oral: poor oral hygiene, gingivitis or periodontal disease, dental caries, tongue coating and dry mouth.", "Nasal and sinus disease with postnasal drip is the next most common source when the oral examination is normal.", "Dietary intake of strong-smelling foods such as raw onion, garlic or cabbage and tobacco use are common contributors.", "A fruity or acetone-like odor suggests hyperglycemia or ketoacidosis; an ammonia-like or urine-like odor suggests advanced kidney disease; a musty or sweet odor suggests advanced liver disease.", "Fever, facial swelling, trismus or a toxic appearance suggests a deep dental or sinus infection and needs urgent imaging and treatment.", "Social and psychological distress is a common consequence even when the odor is mild."], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Adult with obvious poor oral hygiene, visible plaque and gingival inflammation", "Bad breath worse on waking and after strong-smelling meals", "Symptoms improve with brushing, flossing and 