ide waters or feels dry, drools slightly with drinking", "severe": "Ill-at-ease, unable to close the affected eye, obvious facial asymmetry at rest, may hold a tissue to the mouth", "critical": "Not typical for Bell's palsy; if the patient is toxic-appearing, febrile, or has altered mental status, consider another diagnosis"}, "findings": [{"id": "unilateral-facial-weakness", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Ask the patient to smile, show teeth, puff out the cheeks, and wrinkle the forehead", "finding": "Unilateral weakness of the entire half of the face, involving both the upper face (forehead, eyebrow) and lower face (mouth, cheek)", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "forehead-involvement", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Ask the patient to raise the eyebrows and wrinkle the forehead", "finding": "Inability to raise the eyebrow or wrinkle the forehead on the affected side (upper facial weakness)", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "incomplete-eye-closure", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Ask the patient to close both eyes tightly while you try to open them", "finding": "Inability to fully close the eye on the affected side; the eye rolls up (Bell's phenomenon) when closure is attempted", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "mouth-droop", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the face at rest and ask the patient to smile", "finding": "Drooping of the corner of the mouth on the affected side; the nasolabial fold is flattened", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "absent-limb-weakness", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Test strength in all four limbs and check gait", "finding": "Normal strength and gait; no arm or leg weakness", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "argues_against", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "normal-speech", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Listen to the patient's spontaneous speech and ask them to repeat a sentence", "finding": "Speech is clear and fluent; no dysarthria or aphasia", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "argues_against", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "normal-cranial-nerves-other", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Test extraocular movements, visual fields, pupils, and hearing", "finding": "Cranial nerves other than the facial nerve are intact; no diplopia, visual field cut, or hearing loss", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "argues_against", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "taste-impairment", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Test taste on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue on both sides (e.g., with sugar or salt)", "finding": "Decreased taste on the affected side of the tongue", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "hyperacusis", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Ask about sensitivity to sound or test with a tuning fork near the ear", "finding": "Increased sensitivity to sound on the affected side", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "supports", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "postauricular-pain", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Palpate behind the ear on the affected side", "finding": "Tenderness or pain behind the ear", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-vesicles", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the external ear canal, tympanic membrane, and oral mucosa", "finding": "No vesicles or rash in the ear canal or mouth", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "argues_against", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "no-neck-stiffness", "region": "neck", "maneuver": "Assess neck flexibility and perform Brudzinski and Kernig maneuvers if meningitis is a concern", "finding": "No neck stiffness or meningeal signs", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "argues_against", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-rash", "region": "skin", "maneuver": "Inspect the skin, especially for a targetoid rash or vesicles", "finding": "No rash, no erythema migrans, no vesicular lesions", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "argues_against", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "normal-sensation", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Test facial sensation to light touch and pinprick on both sides", "finding": "Sensation is intact; if the patient reports numbness, it does not follow a dermatomal or nerve distribution", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "normal-reflexes", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Test deep tendon reflexes in the arms and legs", "finding": "Reflexes are normal and symmetric", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}]}, "vitals": {}, "labs": [{"test_id": "glucose", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 100, "max": 125}, "moderate": {"min": 126, "max": 200}, "severe": {"min": 180, "max": 300}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Diabetes is a risk factor and may be newly found; hyperglycemia also worsens recovery. A normal glucose does not exclude the diagnosis."}, {"test_id": "a1c", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 5.7, "max": 6.4}, "moderate": {"min": 6.5, "max": 8.0}, "severe": {"min": 7.5, "max": 10.0}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Screens for undiagnosed diabetes, which is associated with Bell's palsy and poorer recovery."}, {"test_id": "hcg", "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Negative. Ordered in anyone who could be pregnant before starting corticosteroids or antivirals."}], "imaging": [{"study": "MRI brain and internal auditory canals with and without contrast", "modality": "mri", "preferred_when": "Atypical features, progressive or recurrent weakness, or concern for another cause", "findings": [{"text": "Enhancement and thickening of the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII) in the internal auditory canal or geniculate ganglion", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Normal brain parenchyma without mass, infarct, or demyelinating lesion", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "No mass lesion along the facial nerve or in the cerebellopontine angle", "frequency": "common"}]}, {"study": "CT head without contrast", "modality": "ct", "preferred_when": "Emergency setting when stroke is a concern and MRI is not immediately available", "findings": [{"text": "No acute intracranial hemorrhage or large territorial infarct", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Usually normal; may be unremarkable in Bell's palsy", "frequency": "common"}]}, {"study": "Chest x-ray", "modality": "xray", "findings": [{"text": "Usually normal; may show findings of sarcoidosis or malignancy when those are suspected", "frequency": "rare"}]}], "workup": {"initial": [{"test": "glucose", "why": "Diabetes is a risk factor and hyperglycemia affects management and recovery"}, {"test": "hcg", "why": "Rules out pregnancy before starting corticosteroids or antivirals", "only_if": "patient could be pregnant"}, {"test": "Lyme serology", "why": "Distinguishes Lyme disease from Bell's palsy", "only_if": "endemic area, tick exposure, or rash"}, {"test": "MRI brain and internal auditory canals with and without contrast", "why": "Evaluates for structural causes when features are atypical", "only_if": "atypical features, progressive or recurrent weakness, or concern for another cause"}], "confirmatory": [], "supporting": [{"test": "a1c", "why": "Screens for undiagnosed diabetes"}, {"test": "HIV", "why": "Consider in patients with risk factors or atypical features", "only_if": "risk factors or atypical presentation"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "cbc", "why": "Not routinely needed; Bell's palsy is a clinical diagnosis"}, {"test": "bmp", "why": "Not routinely needed unless dehydration or electrolyte concerns"}, {"test": "crp", "why": "Nonspecific and does not change management"}, {"test": "esr", "why": "Nonspecific and does not change management"}, {"test": "ct head without contrast", "why": "Not needed when the presentation is classic and there are no stroke features"}, {"test": "MRI brain and internal auditory canals with and without contrast", "why": "Not routine for classic Bell's palsy", "only_if": "atypical features, progressive or recurrent weakness, or concern for another cause"}, {"test": "Chest x-ray", "why": "Not indicated unless sarcoidosis or malignancy is suspected"}], "decision_points": ["Pregnancy test before starting corticosteroids or antivirals in anyone who could be pregnant", "Distinguish forehead-sparing weakness (stroke) from forehead-involving weakness (Bell's palsy) at the bedside", "Order MRI when weakness is progressive, recurrent, or accompanied by other cranial nerve deficits"], "criteria": [{"name": "Clinical diagnosis of Bell's palsy", "summary": "Acute unilateral facial paralysis of unknown cause that involves the upper and lower face, develops over 1–3 days, and is not explained by another condition.", "items": ["Sudden onset of unilateral facial weakness", "Involvement of the forehead and lower face", "Progression over 1–3 days", "No other cranial nerve deficits", "No limb weakness, speech disturbance, or other neurologic signs"]}], "scores": []}, "differential": [{"name": "Acute ischemic stroke", "slug": "ischemic-stroke", "why_considered": "Acute facial weakness is a common stroke presentation and the most important alternative to exclude.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Facial weakness that spares the forehead, or any limb weakness, speech disturbance, or other focal neurologic deficit.", "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden onset, often maximal at once rather than progressing over 1–3 days", "Weakness may involve the arm or leg", "Difficulty speaking or understanding speech", "Vascular risk factors such as hypertension, diabetes, smoking, atrial fibrillation"], "exam": ["Forehead sparing: the patient can still wrinkle the forehead on the affected side", "Weakness limited to the lower face", "Arm or leg weakness, drift, or sensory loss", "Dysarthria, aphasia, or neglect", "Gaze deviation or visual field cut"], "labs": ["Glucose and coagulation studies as part of stroke workup"], "imaging": ["Acute infarct on CT or MRI", "Large vessel occlusion or restricted diffusion on MRI"]}}, {"name": "Ramsay Hunt syndrome", "slug": "ramsay-hunt-syndrome", "why_considered": "Herpes zoster of the geniculate ganglion causes acute unilateral facial paralysis that closely mimics Bell's palsy.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Vesicles in the ear canal, on the pinna, or in the mouth, or severe ear pain with hearing loss.", "discriminators": {"history": ["Severe ear pain out of proportion to findings", "Vesicular rash in or around the ear or mouth", "Hearing loss, vertigo, or tinnitus", "May have had chickenpox"], "exam": ["Vesicles in the external auditory canal, on the pinna, or on the oral mucosa", "Sensorineural hearing loss on tuning fork testing", "Nystagmus or vertigo"], "labs": ["Varicella-zoster virus PCR from vesicle fluid if diagnosis is uncertain"], "imaging": ["MRI may show facial and vestibulocochlear nerve enhancement"]}}, {"name": "Lyme disease", "slug": "lyme-disease", "why_considered": "Lyme neuroborreliosis can cause unilateral or bilateral facial nerve palsy, especially in endemic areas.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Tick exposure, erythema migrans rash, bilateral facial weakness, or residence in an endemic area.", "discriminators": {"history": ["Tick bite or exposure in an endemic area", "Erythema migrans rash", "Arthralgia, headache, or neck stiffness", "Bilateral facial weakness is more common than in Bell's palsy"], "exam": ["Erythema migrans rash", "Joint swelling or tenderness", "Meningeal signs"], "labs": ["Positive Lyme serology (ELISA with Western blot confirmation)", "CSF lymphocytic pleocytosis if CNS involvement suspected"], "imaging": ["MRI may show meningeal or cranial nerve enhancement"]}}, {"name": "Otitis media with facial nerve involvement", "slug": "otitis-media", "why_considered": "Middle ear infection can inflame the facial nerve as it passes through the temporal bone.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Ear pain, fever, and recent upper respiratory infection", "Hearing loss or ear drainage"], "exam": ["Bulging, erythematous tympanic membrane", "Purulent ear drainage", "Conductive hearing loss"], "labs": ["Not routinely needed"], "imaging": ["CT temporal bone if complications such as mastoiditis are suspected"]}}, {"name": "Temporal bone fracture", "slug": "temporal-bone-fracture", "why_considered": "Trauma through the temporal bone can injure the facial nerve and cause acute facial paralysis.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Recent head trauma", "Loss of consciousness or ear bleeding"], "exam": ["Hemotympanum, Battle sign, or raccoon eyes", "Facial paralysis may be immediate or delayed", "Hearing loss"], "labs": ["Not routinely needed"], "imaging": ["Temporal bone fracture on CT"]}}, {"name": "Guillain-Barré syndrome", "slug": "guillain-barre-syndrome", "why_considered": "Can present with facial weakness, often bilateral, and ascending limb weakness.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Preceding diarrheal or respiratory illness", "Ascending weakness starting in the legs", "Paresthesias"], "exam": ["Symmetric limb weakness", "Reduced or absent deep tendon reflexes", "Often bilateral facial weakness"], "labs": ["CSF albuminocytologic dissociation (elevated protein with normal cell count)"], "imaging": ["MRI spine may show nerve root enhancement"]}}, {"name": "Sarcoidosis", "slug": "sarcoidosis", "why_considered": "Neurosarcoidosis can cause facial nerve palsy, sometimes bilateral or recurrent.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Constitutional symptoms such as fever, weight loss, night sweats", "Respiratory symptoms or skin lesions", "Recurrent or bilateral facial weakness"], "exam": ["Lymphadenopathy", "Skin plaques or nodules", "Uveitis"], "labs": ["Elevated ACE level", "Hypercalcemia"], "imaging": ["Hilar lymphadenopathy on chest x-ray or CT", "Cranial nerve enhancement on MRI"]}}, {"name": "Parotid gland tumor", "slug": "parotid-gland-tumor", "why_considered": "A mass in the parotid gland can compress the facial nerve and cause gradual facial weakness.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Gradual onset over weeks to months", "Painless or painful preauricular mass", "No improvement over time"], "exam": ["Palpable parotid mass", "Weakness may be progressive and involve only some branches of the facial nerve"], "labs": ["Not routinely needed"], "imaging": ["Parotid mass on ultrasound, CT, or MRI"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Incomplete weakness of the face; able to close the eye and smile with effort; no functional impairment.", "moderate": "Complete or near-complete weakness of one side of the face; unable to close the eye tightly; drooling and difficulty with eating or drinking.", "severe": "Complete paralysis with inability to close the eye, significant oral incompetence, and pain; risk of corneal injury.", "critical": "Not typical for Bell's palsy; if the patient is toxic-appearing, febrile, or has altered mental status, consider another diagnosis."}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Weakness that spares the forehead", "meaning": "Suggests a central cause such as stroke rather than Bell's palsy."}, {"finding": "Weakness of the arm or leg, or difficulty speaking", "meaning": "Suggests stroke or another central nervous system lesion."}, {"finding": "Fever, neck stiffness, or altered mental status", "meaning": "Suggests meningitis or encephalitis."}, {"finding": "Vesicles in the ear canal or mouth", "meaning": "Suggests Ramsay Hunt syndrome (herpes zoster oticus)."}, {"finding": "Bilateral facial weakness", "meaning": "Suggests Lyme disease, Guillain-Barré syndrome, sarcoidosis, or another systemic cause."}, {"finding": "Gradual onset over weeks or progressive weakness", "meaning": "Suggests a tumor or another structural cause."}, {"finding": "Recent tick bite or rash", "meaning": "Suggests Lyme disease."}], "complications": [{"name": "Corneal injury (exposure keratitis, ulceration)", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Inability to close the eye", "Eye redness, pain, or foreign body sensation", "Decreased vision"]}, {"name": "Incomplete recovery with residual facial weakness or synkinesis", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Weakness persisting beyond 3 months", "Involuntary facial movements with voluntary ones"]}, {"name": "Contracture of facial muscles", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Persistent tightness and asymmetry"]}, {"name": "Psychological distress", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Social withdrawal", "Anxiety or depression about appearance"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Bell's palsy is less common in children; when it occurs, it may follow an ear infection or viral illness and recovery is usually good. Other causes such as Lyme disease should be considered in endemic areas."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Stroke becomes a much more likely cause of acute facial weakness, so the distinction between forehead-sparing weakness (stroke) and forehead-involving weakness (Bell's palsy) is critical."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Bell's palsy is more common in pregnancy, especially in the third trimester and early postpartum period. Presentation is otherwise similar, but treatment decisions must account for pregnancy."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Facial weakness may be due to opportunistic infection or malignancy rather than Bell's palsy; onset may be more gradual and other cranial nerves may be involved."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Diabetes is a risk factor for Bell's palsy, and diabetic patients may have a higher risk of incomplete recovery."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "When did the weakness start, and how fast did it get to its worst?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Sudden onset reaching worst within 48–72 hours; gradual progression over weeks suggests a tumor or another structural cause"}, {"question": "Is the weakness on one side or both sides?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "One side; bilateral weakness suggests Lyme disease, Guillain-Barré syndrome, or sarcoidosis"}, {"question": "Can you raise your eyebrow and wrinkle your forehead on that side?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Inability to wrinkle the forehead (upper face involved); a spared forehead points to stroke"}, {"question": "Can you close that eye completely?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Incomplete eye closure, which risks corneal injury and drives eye care"}, {"question": "Do you have any weakness, numbness, or clumsiness in your arm or leg?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Absent; limb weakness suggests stroke or another central lesion"}, {"question": "Any trouble speaking, understanding speech, or swallowing?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Absent; present suggests stroke or brainstem involvement"}, {"question": "Did you have pain behind your ear before the weakness started?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Postauricular pain, which commonly precedes Bell's palsy"}, {"question": "Has your sense of taste changed, or does sound seem unusually loud on that side?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Altered taste on the front of the tongue or hyperacusis, supporting facial nerve involvement"}, {"question": "Any rash, blisters, or sores in or around your ear or mouth?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Vesicles suggest Ramsay Hunt syndrome (herpes zoster oticus)"}, {"question": "Any hearing loss, ringing in the ear, or spinning sensation?", "category": "ros", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Absent; hearing loss or vertigo suggests Ramsay Hunt syndrome or another cause"}, {"question": "Have you had a recent cold, flu, or other viral illness?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recent upper respiratory infection, a common trigger"}, {"question": "Have you had a tick bite, a rash, or been in an area where Lyme disease is common?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Tick exposure or erythema migrans, which points to Lyme disease"}, {"question": "Do you have diabetes, or are you pregnant or recently postpartum?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Diabetes or pregnancy, both risk factors for Bell's palsy and relevant to treatment"}, {"question": "Have you ever had facial weakness like this before?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recurrent episodes suggest an alternative diagnosis such as sarcoidosis or a structural lesion"}, {"question": "Any fever, neck stiffness, or severe headache?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Absent; present suggests meningitis or encephalitis"}, {"question": "Any recent head injury or trauma to the ear?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Absent; trauma suggests temporal bone fracture"}, {"question": "Does anyone in your family have Bell's palsy?", "category": "family", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Family history, a weak risk factor"}], "accepted_names": ["Bell's palsy", "Bells palsy", "Bell palsy", "Idiopathic facial nerve palsy", "Idiopathic peripheral facial palsy", "Facial nerve palsy (Bell's)"], "reasoning_points": ["Acute unilateral facial weakness that involves both the upper face (forehead, eyebrow) and lower face", "Onset is sudden and progresses over 1–3 days, then plateaus", "No limb weakness, no speech disturbance, and no other cranial nerve deficits", "Postauricular pain, altered taste on the anterior tongue, or hyperacusis may accompany the weakness", "No vesicles in the ear or mouth, ruling out Ramsay Hunt syndrome", "No tick exposure or rash, and no bilateral weakness, making Lyme disease less likely", "No fever, neck stiffness, or altered mental status, arguing against meningitis or encephalitis", "The presentation is not explained by stroke, tumor, trauma, or another structural cause"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Young adult with sudden one-sided facial droop noticed on waking", "Forehead and lower face both involved, with inability to close the eye", "Postauricular pain and altered taste on the affected side", "No limb weakness, speech difficulty, or other cranial nerve findings"], "medium": ["Older adult with vascular risk factors, requiring careful distinction from stroke", "Patient with diabetes or pregnancy, where risk is higher and treatment must be adjusted", "Mild weakness that is easy to overlook without testing forehead wrinkling and eye closure", "Recent viral illness with subtle associated symptoms such as hyperacusis"], "hard": ["Bilateral facial weakness suggesting Lyme disease, Guillain-Barré syndrome, or sarcoidosis", "Gradual or progressive weakness over weeks suggesting a parotid tumor or other structural cause", "Vesicles in the ear canal or mouth indicating Ramsay Hunt syndrome", "Recurrent episodes or additional cranial nerve deficits suggesting an alternative diagnosis", "Facial weakness after head trauma suggesting temporal bone fracture"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
