ng seams carefully and treat the whole household."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Itching may be milder or attributed to dry skin; chronic skin thickening and discoloration may be more prominent."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Scratch sores are more likely to become secondarily infected, and systemic louse-borne disease may be more severe."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Treatment should avoid agents with uncertain safety; permethrin is generally preferred, and hygiene measures are the mainstay."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "Where do you itch the most, and is it under your clothes?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Itching concentrated on the trunk, waist, groin, and upper thighs, in areas covered by clothing"}, {"question": "Have you noticed tiny bugs or eggs in the seams of your clothing, or on your body?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Live lice or nits found in clothing seams, collars, cuffs, or waistbands"}, {"question": "When did the itching start, and has it been getting worse?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Gradual onset days to weeks after infestation, slowly worsening; often present for weeks to months"}, {"question": "Does the itching get worse at night or when you wear or remove your clothes?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Continuous itching worse at night and with clothing use; relieved by bathing and washing clothes"}, {"question": "Have you had any sores, and are any of them painful, red, or draining?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Scratch marks and excoriations; purulence or spreading redness suggests secondary bacterial infection"}, {"question": "Have you had fever, chills, severe headache, or body aches?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Symptoms suggesting a louse-borne illness such as typhus, trench fever, or relapsing fever"}, {"question": "Have you been able to bathe and wash your clothes and bedding regularly?", "category": "social", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Limited access to showers, laundry, or clean clothing; poor hygiene is the main risk factor"}, {"question": "Where are you living right now — a shelter, encampment, or other crowded setting?", "category": "social", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Crowded living conditions, homelessness, unstable housing, or recent displacement"}, {"question": "Has anyone close to you had similar itching or bugs in their clothing?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Close contacts or household members with the same infestation, supporting person-to-person spread"}, {"question": "Have you shared clothing, bedding, or towels with anyone who has been itching?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Contact with clothing, bedding, or towels used by an infested person"}, {"question": "Do you have pets at home, and have they been itchy or scratching?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Pets with fleas or mites; body lice do not come from animals, so this argues against a zoonotic cause"}, {"question": "Have you traveled recently to an area where sandflies or other vector-borne skin diseases are common?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Travel that would raise concern for leishmaniasis or another vector-borne skin disease"}, {"question": "Do you have any condition or take any medication that weakens your immune system?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Immunocompromising condition or immunosuppressive medication, which raises the risk of secondary infection and severe louse-borne disease"}, {"question": "Have you had any trouble sleeping or felt embarrassed or anxious about this?", "category": "ros", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Sleep disturbance and distress; also screen for delusional parasitosis if the belief of infestation is fixed despite negative exams"}], "accepted_names": ["Body lice", "Body lice infestation", "Pediculus humanus corporis infestation", "Pediculus humanus humanus infestation", "Clothes lice", "Pediculosis corporis"], "reasoning_points": ["Intense itching concentrated on the trunk, waist, groin, and upper thighs in areas covered by clothing", "Live body lice or nits identified in the seams, collars, cuffs, or waistbands of the patient's clothing", "Risk factors of poor hygiene, inability to launder clothing, crowded living conditions, or homelessness", "Red bumps, excoriations, and scratch marks from bites and allergic reaction", "Lice and nits absent from the scalp hair and pubic hair, distinguishing body lice from head lice and pubic lice", "No burrows, tracks, or nodules in the finger webs, wrists, or genitalia, arguing against scabies", "Fever, headache, or body aches considered as possible louse-borne illness such as typhus, trench fever, or relapsing fever", "Secondary bacterial or fungal infection of scratch sores considered when there is purulence, spreading erythema, or fever"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Adult experiencing homelessness with intense itching under clothing", "Visible lice and nits in the seams of the clothing on inspection", "Excoriations and bite papules on the trunk, waist, and thighs", "Clear history of inability to bathe or wash clothing regularly"], "medium": ["Patient reports itching and rash but does not mention bugs; lice and nits found only on careful inspection of clothing seams with a magnifying lens", "Chronic infestation with thickened, darkened skin at the waist and groin and no obvious live lice", "Scratch sores with early secondary bacterial infection", "Itching attributed by the patient to dry skin or detergent"], "hard": ["Older adult with mild itching attributed to dry skin and no reported rash", "Child with