uency": "common"}, {"text": "It hurts a lot", "frequency": "common"}], "hpi": {"onset": "Sudden, at the moment of contact with the heat source, chemical, electricity or radiation", "duration": "Minutes to hours since injury at presentation; deeper burns may evolve over the first 24–48 hours", "progression": "Pain and redness are immediate; blisters form over minutes to hours; partial-thickness burns can deepen over the first day or two, especially if cooled poorly or infected", "location": "Depends on mechanism: hands, forearms, face and chest with flame; trunk, buttocks, perineum or extremities with scald; mouth and throat with caustic ingestion; often a patterned area matching the source", "radiation": "Usually none; pain is localized to the injured skin", "quality": "Burning, stinging or throbbing pain; deep full-thickness burns may be numb or painless", "severity": "Mild to severe; superficial burns are 2–4/10, partial-thickness burns often 7–10/10, full-thickness burns may be painless", "timing": "Constant, worse with air movement, dressing changes and cleaning", "aggravating": ["Air blowing across the burn", "Touching or pressing the area", "Dressing changes and cleaning", "Movement of the affected part"], "relieving": ["Cool running water applied soon after injury", "Covering the burn", "Acetaminophen or an opioid for pain"], "associated_symptoms": [{"text": "Redness and swelling of the burned skin", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "Blisters", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Peeling or sloughing skin", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Pain out of proportion to the visible injury", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Chills or feeling cold", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Nausea", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Hoarse voice, cough or singed nose hairs after smoke or flame exposure", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Dizziness or lightheadedness", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Numbness over the burned area (deep burn)", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Dark or decreased urine after a large burn", "frequency": "rare"}], "pertinent_negatives": ["Fever before the injury", "Rash or skin lesions before the injury", "Itching or new medicines before the injury", "Blistering in areas not exposed to the heat source", "Bleeding or drainage from the burn at the time of injury", "Loss of consciousness at the scene (unless electrical or inhalation injury)", "Shortness of breath at rest in a patient with only a small skin burn"]}, "ros": [{"system": "skin", "positives": [{"text": "Redness of the burned skin", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "Blisters", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Peeling or charred skin", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Pale, leathery or waxy skin in deep burns", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Rash elsewhere on the body", "Chronic ulcers", "Moles that have changed"], "argues_against": ["Widespread rash without a heat source", "Blistering in sun-exposed skin only"]}, {"system": "constitutional", "positives": [{"text": "Feeling cold or shivering", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Feeling weak or lightheaded", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Weight loss", "Night sweats", "Fever before the injury"], "argues_against": ["Fever and rash before any burn exposure"]}, {"system": "respiratory", "positives": [{"text": "Cough", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Hoarse voice", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Soot in the mouth or nose", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Shortness of breath", "frequency": "rare"}], "negatives": ["Chronic productive cough", "Wheezing at baseline"], "argues_against": ["Chronic obstructive lung disease as the cause of breathlessness"]}, {"system": "cardiovascular", "positives": [{"text": "Fast heartbeat", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Feeling faint on standing", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Chest pain", "Leg swelling", "Palpitations before the injury"], "argues_against": ["Primary cardiac cause of collapse"]}, {"system": "gastrointestinal", "positives": [{"text": "Nausea", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Vomiting", "frequency": "rare"}], "negatives": ["Blood in vomit", "Black stools", "Abdominal pain before the injury"], "argues_against": ["Gastrointestinal bleeding as the cause of shock"]}, {"system": "neurologic", "positives": [{"text": "Numbness over the burned area", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Dizziness", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Focal weakness", "Seizure before the injury", "Confusion at baseline"], "argues_against": ["Stroke or seizure as the primary cause"]}, {"system": "psychiatric", "positives": [{"text": "Anxiety about the injury", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Feeling overwhelmed", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Suicidal thoughts", "Intent to self-harm"], "argues_against": ["Deliberate self-inflicted burn without reported intent"]}], "atypical": [{"population": "children", "how_it_differs": "Scalds are the most common mechanism; children may not localize pain and present with crying, irritability and refusal to use the affected limb. Non-accidental burn patterns (glove-and-stocking, immersion lines, cigarette burns) should be considered.", "missing_classic_features": ["Clear description of the mechanism", "Accurate pain severity"]}, {"population": "infants", "how_it_differs": "Present with poor feeding, lethargy and irritability rather than pain complaints; even small burns can cause significant fluid loss and hypothermia.", "missing_classic_features": ["Verbal report of pain", "Localizing the burn"]}, {"population": "older_adults", "how_it_differs": "Thinner skin means deeper burns from the same exposure; impaired mobility or cognition may delay escape, and mortality is higher for the same burn size. Pain may be blunted by neuropathy.", "missing_classic_features": ["Severe pain", "Clear history of prolonged exposure"]}, {"population": "diabetes", "how_it_differs": "Peripheral neuropathy can blunt pain, and impaired healing and immune function increase the risk of infection and delayed presentation.", "missing_classic_features": ["Pain out of proportion to the injury"]}, {"population": "pregnancy", "how_it_differs": "Maternal hypovolemia and hypoxemia can compromise the fetus; burns are managed with attention to fetal monitoring and maternal resuscitation.", "missing_classic_features": ["Typical pain and fluid shifts without fetal concern"]}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "how_it_differs": "Higher risk of rapid wound infection and sepsis; fever may be absent or blunted.", "missing_classic_features": ["Fever with wound infection"]}]}, "exam": {"general": {"mild": "Comfortable, alert, no distress; focused on the burned area", "moderate": "In pain, grimacing, but alert and interactive", "severe": "Distressed, tachycardic, cool extremities, anxious or restless", "critical": "Toxic-appearing, pale, diaphoretic, lethargic or obtunded; may have soot around the mouth or singed facial hair"}, "findings": [{"id": "burn-depth-assessment", "region": "skin", "maneuver": "Inspect the burn and note color, blistering, capillary refill and sensation", "finding": "Superficial (first-degree) burns are red, dry and painful without blisters; partial-thickness (second-degree) burns are blistered, moist, red or mottled and very painful; full-thickness (third-degree) burns are white, waxy, leathery or charred, may be painless, and do not blanch", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "tbsa-estimate", "region": "skin", "maneuver": "Estimate the percentage of total body surface area burned using the rule of nines (or Lund-Browder in children)", "finding": "Extent of partial- and full-thickness burn expressed as % TBSA; superficial burns are not counted", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "airway-inspection", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the mouth, oropharynx and nose; listen to the voice", "finding": "Soot in the mouth or nose, singed nasal hairs, facial or oral burns, hoarse voice or stridor suggesting inhalation injury", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "circumferential-burn", "region": "extremities", "maneuver": "Inspect the burned extremity or chest for a full-thickness circumferential pattern", "finding": "Tight, unyielding eschar that may impair distal perfusion or chest expansion", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "severe", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "distal-perfusion", "region": "extremities", "maneuver": "Check capillary refill, radial or pedal pulses, and sensation distal to the burn", "finding": "Delayed capillary refill, weak or absent pulses, or numbness distal to a circumferential burn", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "severe", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "wound-infection-signs", "region": "skin", "maneuver": "Inspect the burn for purulent drainage, spreading erythema, foul odor or eschar separation", "finding": "Signs of wound infection or sepsis", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "normal-skin-elsewhere", "region": "skin", "maneuver": "Inspect skin not exposed to the heat source", "finding": "No rash, blisters or lesions in unburned areas", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "normal-lung-exam", "region": "lung", "maneuver": "Auscultate the lungs", "finding": "Clear breath sounds without wheeze or crackles in a patient without inhalation injury", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "normal-abdomen", "region": "abdomen", "maneuver": "Palpate the abdomen", "finding": "Soft, non-tender abdomen without guarding", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "normal-neuro", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Assess orientation and gross motor function", "finding": "Alert and oriented without focal deficit (unless electrical injury or hypoxia)", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}]}, "vitals": {"temp": {"mild": {"min": 97.8, "max": 99.0}, "moderate": {"min": 97.0, "max": 99.5}, "severe": {"min": 96.0, "max": 100.0}, "critical": {"min": 95.0, "max": 101.0}}, "hr": {"mild": {"min": 60, "max": 100}, "moderate": {"min": 90, "max": 120}, "severe": {"min": 110, "max": 140}, "critical": {"min": 130, "max": 170}}, "rr": {"mild": {"min": 12, "max": 20}, "moderate": {"min": 18, "max": 24}, "severe": {"min": 22, "max": 30}, "critical": {"min": 28, "max": 40}}, "sbp": {"mild": {"min": 100, "max": 130}, "moderate": {"min": 90, "max": 120}, "severe": {"min": 80, "max": 110}, "critical": {"min": 60, "max": 90}}, "dbp": {"mild": {"min": 60, "max": 80}, "moderate": {"min": 55, "max": 75}, "severe": {"min": 50, "max": 70}, "critical": {"min": 30, "max": 60}}, "spo2": {"mild": {"min": 95, "max": 100}, "moderate": {"min": 94, "max": 99}, "severe": {"min": 90, "max": 96}, "critical": {"min": 80, "max": 92}}}, "labs": [{"test_id": "hgb", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 10.0, "max": 13.0}, "severe": {"min": 8.0, "max": 11.0}, "critical": {"min": 6.0, "max": 9.0}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "May be normal early from hemoconcentration; a falling hemoglobin suggests blood loss or hemodilution from resuscitation."}, {"test_id": "hct", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 30, "max": 40}, "severe": {"min": 24, "max": 34}, "critical": {"min": 18, "max": 28}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Can be falsely elevated early from plasma loss before fluids are given."}, {"test_id": "wbc", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 11.0, "max": 18.0}, "severe": {"min": 14.0, "max": 25.0}, "critical": {"min": 18.0, "max": 35.0}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important", "caveat": "A stress leukocytosis is common in the first 24–48 hours and does not by itself mean infection; a rising count after that suggests wound infection or sepsis."}, {"test_id": "neut_pct", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 70, "max": 85}, "severe": {"min": 75, "max": 90}, "critical": {"min": 80, "max": 92}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "optional"}, {"test_id": "plt", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 100, "max": 150}, "critical": {"min": 50, "max": 120}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Thrombocytopenia can reflect dilution from massive resuscitation, consumption or early sepsis."}, {"test_id": "na", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 130, "max": 135}, "severe": {"min": 125, "max": 133}, "critical": {"min": 120, "max": 132}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Hyponatremia from fluid shifts and resuscitation; hypernatremia suggests inadequate free water."}, {"test_id": "k", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 3.2, "max": 3.5}, "severe": {"min": 2.8, "max": 3.4}, "critical": {"min": 2.5, "max": 3.2}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Hypokalemia is common with large-volume resuscitation; hyperkalemia raises concern for electrical injury, rhabdomyolysis or renal failure."}, {"test_id": "hco3", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 16, "max": 21}, "critical": {"min": 12, "max": 18}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Low bicarbonate suggests metabolic acidosis from shock, poor perfusion or inhalation injury."}, {"test_id": "bun", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 20, "max": 30}, "severe": {"min": 25, "max": 45}, "critical": {"min": 30, "max": 60}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Rises with dehydration and hypovolemia; a high BUN/creatinine ratio supports volume depletion."}, {"test_id": "cr", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 1.2, "max": 1.8}, "severe": {"min": 1.5, "max": 2.5}, "critical": {"min": 2.0, "max": 4.0}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important", "caveat": "A rise after a large burn suggests acute kidney injury from hypovolemia, myoglobinuria or sepsis."}, {"test_id": "glucose", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 100, "max": 160}, "severe": {"min": 140, "max": 220}, "critical": {"min": 160, "max": 300}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Stress hyperglycemia is common and worsens outcomes; tight control is avoided because of hypoglycemia risk."}, {"test_id": "albumin", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 2.0, "max": 3.2}, "critical": {"min": 1.5, "max": 2.8}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Falls with the systemic inflammatory response and capillary leak; low values worsen fluid resuscitation requirements."}, {"test_id": "ck", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 1000, "max": 10000}, "critical": {"min": 5000, "max": 50000}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Markedly elevated in electrical burns, crush injury and deep muscle burns; a very high CK with dark urine suggests rhabdomyolysis and myoglobinuric kidney injury."}, {"test_id": "lactate", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 2.0, "max": 3.0}, "severe": {"min": 3.0, "max": 5.0}, "critical": {"min": 4.0, "max": 8.0}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Elevated lactate indicates hypoperfusion or shock and guides resuscitation; it also rises with inhalation injury and carbon monoxide exposure."}, {"test_id": "ph", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 7.2, "max": 7.34}, "critical": {"min": 7.0, "max": 7.28}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Acidemia from shock, poor perfusion or inhalation injury; measured on arterial blood gas."}, {"test_id": "paco2", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 30, "max": 35}, "critical": {"min": 28, "max": 50}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Low from tachypnea and pain; a rising PaCO2 with a hoarse voice or stridor suggests upper airway obstruction from inhalation injury."}, {"test_id": "pao2", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 60, "max": 80}, "critical": {"min": 45, "max": 70}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Falls with inhalation injury, smoke inhalation or carbon monoxide poisoning; a normal PaO2 does not exclude carbon monoxide toxicity."}, {"test_id": "sao2", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 90, "max": 95}, "critical": {"min": 80, "max": 92}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Pulse oximetry can read falsely normal in carbon monoxide poisoning because it cannot distinguish carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin."}, {"test_id": "pt", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 14.0, "max": 18.0}, "critical": {"min": 16.0, "max": 24.0}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Prolonged with severe burns, disseminated intravascular coagulation or liver dysfunction."}, {"test_id": "inr", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 1.3, "max": 1.8}, "critical": {"min": 1.6, "max": 2.5}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "optional"}, {"test_id": "aptt", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 36, "max": 50}, "critical": {"min": 45, "max": 70}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "optional"}, {"test_id": "ua_blood", "qualitative": {"severe": "2+", "critical": "3+"}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Positive blood without red cells on microscopy suggests myoglobinuria from muscle injury, especially in electrical or deep burns."}, {"test_id": "ua_protein", "qualitative": {"severe": "1+", "critical": "2+"}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "optional"}, {"test_id": "ua_sg", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 1.02, "max": 1.03}, "severe": {"min": 1.025, "max": 1.035}, "critical": {"min": 1.03, "max": 1.04}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important", "caveat": "High specific gravity reflects dehydration and concentrated urine; urine output is the key resuscitation endpoint."}, {"test_id": "hcg", "frequency": "almost_always", "importance": "essential", "caveat": "Negative. Required in anyone who could be pregnant before imaging, medications and resuscitation decisions."}], "ecg": {"findings": [{"feature": "rate", "finding": "Sinus tachycardia", "frequency": "common", "min_severity": "moderate"}, {"feature": "rhythm", "finding": "Sinus rhythm", "frequency": "common", "min_severity": "mild"}, {"feature": "other", "finding": "Nonspecific ST-T changes from tachycardia, pain and electrolyte shifts", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate"}, {"feature": "qt", "finding": "Prolonged QT interval with hypokalemia, hypocalcemia or hypomagnesemia", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "severe"}, {"feature": "conduction", "finding": "Widened QRS with severe hyperkalemia from electrical injury or rhabdomyolysis", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "critical"}, {"feature": "st", "finding": "ST elevation or depression suggesting myocardial injury in electrical burns or in patients with underlying coronary disease", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "severe"}, {"feature": "rhythm", "finding": "Ventricular fibrillation or asystole in high-voltage electrical injury", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "critical"}]}, "imaging": [{"study": "Chest x-ray", "modality": "xray", "preferred_when": "Suspected inhalation injury, smoke exposure or respiratory symptoms", "findings": [{"text": "Normal in most patients with a skin burn alone", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Patchy alveolar or interstitial opacities from inhalation injury or pulmonary edema", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Soot in the trachea or main bronchi", "frequency": "rare"}, {"text": "Widened mediastinum or pneumothorax after blast or electrical injury", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "CT chest without contrast", "modality": "ct", "preferred_when": "Suspected inhalation injury with an unclear chest x-ray, or trauma from a blast or fall", "findings": [{"text": "Ground-glass and consolidative opacities from inhalation injury", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Airway wall thickening and mucus plugging", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Pneumothorax, rib fractures or pulmonary contusion from associated trauma", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "CT head without contrast", "modality": "ct", "preferred_when": "Electrical injury, loss of consciousness, fall or blast injury", "findings": [{"text": "Normal study in most patients with a skin burn alone", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Intracranial hemorrhage or cerebral edema after electrical injury or head trauma", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "CT cervical spine without contrast", "modality": "ct", "preferred_when": "Fall, blast injury or electrical injury with neck pain or altered mental status", "findings": [{"text": "Normal cervical spine in most patients", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Fracture or dislocation after a fall or blast", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast", "modality": "ct", "preferred_when": "Electrical injury, blunt trauma or unexplained shock", "findings": [{"text": "Normal study in most patients with a skin burn alone", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Solid organ injury, bowel injury or free fluid after blunt trauma", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "X-ray of the affected extremity", "modality": "xray", "preferred_when": "Electrical burn, deep burn over a joint, or suspected associated fracture", "findings": [{"text": "Normal bone in most thermal burns", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Fracture or dislocation after a fall or electrical injury", "frequency": "rare"}, {"text": "Soft tissue swelling or foreign material in the wound", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "Bronchoscopy", "modality": "endoscopy", "preferred_when": "Suspected inhalation injury with worsening oxygenation or unclear airway findings", "findings": [{"text": "Erythema, edema, soot or ulceration of the airway mucosa", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Carbonaceous material and mucus plugging in the tracheobronchial tree", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}], "workup": {"initial": [{"test": "cbc", "why": "Baseline hemoglobin, white count and platelets; guides resuscitation and later detects infection"}, {"test": "bmp", "why": "Electrolytes, renal function and glucose; guides fluid resuscitation and detects acute kidney injury"}, {"test": "abg", "why": "Assesses oxygenation, ventilation and acid-base status", "only_if": "suspected inhalation injury, smoke exposure or respiratory distress"}, {"test": "lactate", "why": "Detects hypoperfusion and guides resuscitation in large burns"}, {"test": "ua", "why": "Urine output and myoglobin screen in large or electrical burns"}, {"test": "hcg", "why": "Rules out pregnancy before imaging, medications and resuscitation decisions"}, {"test": "Chest x-ray", "why": "Looks for inhalation injury or associated trauma", "only_if": "suspected inhalation injury, smoke exposure or respiratory symptoms"}], "confirmatory": [{"test": "Bronchoscopy", "why": "Direct visualization confirms inhalation injury when the diagnosis is uncertain or the airway is worsening", "only_if": "suspected inhalation injury with unclear findings or worsening oxygenation"}], "supporting": [{"test": "ck", "why": "Detects muscle injury and rhabdomyolysis in electrical or deep burns"}, {"test": "coag", "why": "Detects coagulopathy in large burns or suspected disseminated intravascular coagulation"}, {"test": "Ct chest", "why": "Characterizes inhalation injury and associated trauma when the chest x-ray is unclear", "only_if": "suspected inhalation injury with an unclear chest x-ray or trauma"}, {"test": "CT head without contrast", "why": "Evaluates for intracranial injury after electrical injury, fall or blast", "only_if": "electrical injury, loss of consciousness, fall or blast injury"}, {"test": "CT cervical spine without contrast", "why": "Evaluates for cervical spine injury after a fall or blast", "only_if": "fall, blast injury or electrical injury with neck pain or altered mental status"}, {"test": "CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast", "why": "Evaluates for intra-abdominal injury after electrical or blunt trauma", "only_if": "electrical injury, blunt trauma or unexplained shock"}, {"test": "X-ray of the affected extremity", "why": "Evaluates for fracture or foreign body in electrical or deep burns", "only_if": "electrical burn, deep burn over a joint, or suspected associated fracture"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "lipase", "why": "No role in burn assessment unless abdominal pain suggests pancreatitis"}, {"test": "d_dimer", "why": "Not useful in burn assessment"}, {"test": "tsh", "why": "Not part of burn workup"}, {"test": "a1c", "why": "Not needed acutely unless diabetes management is relevant"}, {"test": "Ct chest", "why": "Not routine; reserved for suspected inhalation injury or trauma", "only_if": "suspected inhalation injury with an unclear chest x-ray or trauma"}, {"test": "CT head without contrast", "why": "Not routine; reserved for electrical injury, head trauma or altered mental status", "only_if": "electrical injury, loss of consciousness, fall or blast injury"}, {"test": "CT cervical spine without contrast", "why": "Not routine; reserved for trauma or electrical injury with neck symptoms", "only_if": "fall, blast injury or electrical injury with neck pain or altered mental status"}, {"test": "CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast", "why": "Not routine; reserved for electrical injury, blunt trauma or unexplained shock", "only_if": "electrical injury, blunt trauma or unexplained shock"}, {"test": "X-ray of the affected extremity", "why": "Not routine; reserved for electrical or deep burns with suspected fracture", "only_if": "electrical burn, deep burn over a joint, or suspected associated fracture"}], "decision_points": ["Assess airway early in any patient with facial burns, soot, hoarse voice or stridor; intubate before edema makes it difficult", "Estimate TBSA with the rule of nines in adults and Lund-Browder in children; superficial burns are not counted", "Start fluid resuscitation for burns over 20% TBSA in adults and over 10% in children using the Parkland or modified Brooke formula, then titrate to urine output", "Pregnancy test before imaging and medications in anyone who could be pregnant", "Suspect carbon monoxide or cyanide toxicity with smoke inhalation and a normal pulse oximetry reading", "Consider child abuse or neglect when the burn pattern does not match the reported mechanism"], "criteria": [], "scores": ["rule_of_nines", "lund_browder", "parkland_formula", "aba_burn_severity"]}, "differential": [{"name": "Cellulitis", "slug": "cellulitis", "why_considered": "Red, warm, swollen and tender skin with fever can mimic a partial-thickness burn, especially when the burn history is vague.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Spreading redness with fever and no clear heat or chemical exposure", "discriminators": {"history": ["No heat, chemical or electrical exposure", "Gradual onset over days rather than sudden at the moment of injury", "Often a break in the skin, insect bite or prior wound"], "exam": ["Poorly demarcated spreading erythema without blisters or a burn pattern", "Tender, warm skin with lymphangitic streaking", "No charred, waxy or leathery areas"], "labs": ["Leukocytosis with a left shift", "Elevated inflammatory markers"], "imaging": ["Ultrasound may show subcutaneous edema or abscess", "No findings of inhalation injury on chest imaging"]}}, {"name": "Toxic epidermal necrolysis", "slug": "toxic-epidermal-necrolysis", "why_considered": "Widespread skin sloughing and blistering can look like a large partial-thickness burn.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Widespread blistering and skin detachment without any heat or chemical exposure", "discriminators": {"history": ["Recent new medication such as an anticonvulsant, allopurinol or sulfonamide", "Preceding fever, malaise and mucosal pain", "No burn mechanism"], "exam": ["Positive Nikolsky sign with sheet-like epidermal detachment", "Painful erosions of the mouth, eyes or genitals", "Targetoid or dusky macules rather than a patterned burn"], "labs": ["Often normal or nonspecific; may show leukopenia or elevated liver enzymes"], "imaging": ["Not diagnostic; chest imaging only if respiratory involvement is suspected"]}}, {"name": "Stevens-Johnson syndrome", "slug": "stevens-johnson-syndrome", "why_considered": "Mucosal and skin blistering with systemic illness can resemble a burn.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Mucosal erosions with skin blistering after a new medication", "discriminators": {"history": ["New medication in the preceding weeks", "Prodromal fever and sore throat", "No exposure to heat, chemicals or electricity"], "exam": ["Hemorrhagic crusting of the lips and oral mucosa", "Conjunctivitis or genital erosions", "Detachment of less than 10% of body surface area"], "labs": ["Nonspecific; may show elevated liver enzymes or leukopenia"], "imaging": ["Not diagnostic"]}}, {"name": "Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome", "slug": "staphylococcal-scalded-skin-syndrome", "why_considered": "Widespread tender erythema with superficial peeling in a child can mimic a scald.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "A young child with diffuse tender erythema and peeling skin without a heat source", "discriminators": {"history": ["Recent conjunctivitis, otitis or nasopharyngeal infection", "No burn exposure", "Irritability and poor feeding"], "exam": ["Superficial, easily peeled skin with a positive Nikolsky sign", "No mucosal involvement", "Perioral crusting"], "labs": ["Leukocytosis; cultures from the primary site may grow Staphylococcus aureus"], "imaging": ["Not diagnostic"]}}, {"name": "Contact dermatitis", "slug": "contact-dermatitis", "why_considered": "Localized redness, swelling and blistering after chemical or plant exposure can look like a chemical burn.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Exposure to an allergen or irritant rather than a caustic", "Itching rather than burning pain", "Onset hours to days after exposure"], "exam": ["Erythematous, vesicular rash with sharp borders matching the contact area", "No charred or leathery skin"], "labs": ["Not needed"], "imaging": ["Not needed"]}}, {"name": "Frostbite", "slug": "frostbite", "why_considered": "Cold injury causes skin blistering, numbness and discoloration that can resemble a deep burn.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Prolonged exposure to cold, wind or wet conditions", "No heat, chemical or electrical exposure"], "exam": ["Affected areas are cold, pale or waxy and numb", "Distal extremities, nose or ears most affected"], "labs": ["Not diagnostic"], "imaging": ["Bone scan or angiography may show absent perfusion in severe cases"]}}, {"name": "Necrotizing fasciitis", "slug": "necrotizing-fasciitis", "why_considered": "Severe pain, skin discoloration and systemic toxicity can mimic a deep burn with sepsis.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Pain out of proportion to skin findings with rapid progression and systemic toxicity", "discriminators": {"history": ["Often follows a minor wound, surgery or varicella", "Rapidly worsening pain over hours", "No burn mechanism"], "exam": ["Pain out of proportion to visible skin changes", "Dusky or bullous skin with crepitus", "Woody induration and skin anesthesia"], "labs": ["Marked leukocytosis, elevated lactate and creatinine, hyponatremia"], "imaging": ["CT or MRI shows fascial thickening, gas or fluid tracking along fascia"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Superficial or small partial-thickness burn, less than 10% TBSA in adults, no airway or inhalation concern, hemodynamically stable, pain controlled with oral analgesia", "moderate": "Partial-thickness burn of 10–20% TBSA in adults, or any burn with significant pain, blisters or concern for deeper injury, but no airway compromise or shock", "severe": "Partial- or full-thickness burn over 20% TBSA in adults, or any full-thickness burn, circumferential burn, or burn with inhalation injury or hemodynamic instability", "critical": "Burn with airway compromise, inhalation injury with respiratory failure, shock, or burns over 40% TBSA; may require immediate intubation, aggressive fluid resuscitation and intensive care"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Hoarse voice, stridor, soot in the mouth or singed nasal hairs", "meaning": "Inhalation injury with impending airway obstruction; intubate before edema makes it impossible"}, {"finding": "Hypotension or heart rate over 120", "meaning": "Burn shock from massive fluid shifts; needs immediate IV fluid resuscitation"}, {"finding": "Circumferential full-thickness burn of the chest or extremity", "meaning": "Restrictive eschar can impair ventilation or distal perfusion; may need escharotomy"}, {"finding": "Dark or decreased urine output after a large burn", "meaning": "Hypovolemia or myoglobinuria with risk of acute kidney injury"}, {"finding": "Altered mental status or loss of consciousness at the scene", "meaning": "Carbon monoxide poisoning, hypoxia, head trauma or electrical injury"}, {"finding": "Burn pattern inconsistent with the reported mechanism", "meaning": "Concern for abuse or neglect, especially in children and older adults"}], "complications": [{"name": "Burn shock", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Burns over 20% TBSA", "Tachycardia and hypotension", "Decreased urine output"]}, {"name": "Inhalation injury with airway obstruction or respiratory failure", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Facial burns, soot or singed nasal hairs", "Hoarse voice or stridor", "Hypoxemia or rising PaCO2"]}, {"name": "Carbon monoxide or cyanide toxicity", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Enclosed-space fire", "Altered mental status", "Normal pulse oximetry despite severe hypoxia"]}, {"name": "Wound infection and sepsis", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Purulent drainage, foul odor or spreading erythema", "Fever, rising white count or hemodynamic instability", "Eschar separation"]}, {"name": "Acute kidney injury", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Dark urine or positive urine blood without red cells", "Elevated creatinine", "Inadequate fluid resuscitation"]}, {"name": "Hypertrophic scarring and contracture", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Deep partial- or full-thickness burns", "Burns crossing joints", "Delayed healing or infection"]}, {"name": "Heterotopic ossification", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Deep burns over joints", "Decreased range of motion months after injury"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Scalds are the most common mechanism; use the Lund-Browder chart for TBSA because the head is proportionally larger. Fluid resuscitation starts at 10% TBSA rather than 20%. Non-accidental burn patterns should be considered."}, {"population": "infants", "differences": "Present with poor feeding, lethargy and irritability rather than pain complaints. Even small burns can cause significant fluid loss and hypothermia; maintain warmth during assessment."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Thinner skin means deeper burns from the same exposure; impaired mobility or cognition may delay escape. Mortality is higher for the same burn size, and fluid resuscitation must be balanced against cardiac and renal comorbidity."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Maternal hypovolemia and hypoxemia can compromise the fetus. Resuscitate the mother aggressively with attention to fetal monitoring and left uterine displacement after 20 weeks."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Peripheral neuropathy can blunt pain and delay presentation; impaired healing and immune function increase the risk of wound infection. Glucose control is important but tight control is avoided because of hypoglycemia risk."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Higher risk of rapid wound infection and sepsis; fever may be absent or blunted, so a low threshold for cultures and imaging is needed."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "What caused the burn, and what was the source?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Scald, flame, hot surface, chemical, electrical, radiation or smoke; guides depth, workup and disposition"}, {"question": "When did the burn happen?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Minutes to hours ago; partial-thickness burns can deepen over the first 24–48 hours"}, {"question": "Was the burn cooled with running water, and was anything applied to it?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Early cool running water limits depth; butter, toothpaste or ice worsen injury"}, {"question": "Where on the body is the burn, and how much of the body does it cover?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Face, hands, feet, perineum, circumferential or large areas change disposition and fluid needs"}, {"question": "Did the burn happen in an enclosed space, or was there smoke, soot or hot gas?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Inhalation injury, carbon monoxide or cyanide exposure"}, {"question": "Is your voice hoarse, or do you have a cough, trouble breathing or throat tightness?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Airway edema from inhalation injury; intubate before obstruction"}, {"question": "Did you lose consciousness at the scene or fall?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Electrical injury, carbon monoxide poisoning, head or spine trauma"}, {"question": "How bad is the pain, and is any part of the burn numb?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Numbness suggests full-thickness burn; pain out of proportion suggests deeper injury"}, {"question": "Are you able to drink and keep fluids down?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Oral intake determines whether IV fluids are needed for smaller burns"}, {"question": "Have you had any fever, chills or worsening redness since the burn?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Wound infection or sepsis"}, {"question": "What medical problems do you have, such as diabetes, heart disease or nerve damage?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Comorbidities that worsen healing, blunt pain or complicate fluid resuscitation"}, {"question": "What medicines do you take?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Beta blockers or opioids that blunt tachycardia, and drugs affecting healing"}, {"question": "Do you have any medication allergies?", "category": "allergies", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Allergy to sulfa drugs, which affects topical antibiotic choice"}, {"question": "Could you be pregnant? When was your last period?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Pregnancy changes imaging, medications and resuscitation decisions"}, {"question": "How did the burn happen exactly — could it have been caused by someone else or by a lack of supervision?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Pattern inconsistent with the mechanism raises concern for abuse or neglect"}, {"question": "Do you have working smoke detectors at home, and do you smoke or use alcohol or drugs?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Risk factors for more severe burns and impaired escape"}], "accepted_names": ["Burns", "Burn injury", "Thermal burn", "Burn"], "reasoning_points": ["A clear mechanism of heat, chemical, electrical or radiation exposure with sudden onset of pain and skin changes at the moment of injury", "Depth assessed from color, blistering, capillary refill and sensation: superficial, partial-thickness or full-thickness", "Extent estimated as percent total body surface area using the rule of nines in adults and Lund-Browder in children, counting only partial- and full-thickness burns", "Airway and inhalation injury assessed from facial burns, soot, singed nasal hairs, hoarse voice or stridor", "Hemodynamic status and urine output used to recognize burn shock and guide fluid resuscitation", "Look-alikes such as cellulitis, toxic epidermal necrolysis, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, contact dermatitis, frostbite and necrotizing fasciitis excluded by history, exposure and examination", "Pregnancy excluded before imaging, medications and resuscitation decisions", "Burn pattern compared with the reported mechanism to screen for abuse or neglect"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Clear scald or flame mechanism with a patterned burn", "Obvious partial-thickness burn with blisters and severe pain", "Small burn under 10% TBSA in a healthy adult", "No airway, inhalation or hemodynamic concern"], "medium": ["Burn of 10–20% TBSA requiring fluid resuscitation and admission decisions", "Mixed-depth burn that is difficult to classify", "Facial burn with concern for inhalation injury but a normal chest x-ray", "Child with a scald and a mechanism that needs to be verified"], "hard": ["Full-thickness burn that is painless and mistaken for a less severe injury", "Circumferential chest or extremity burn with impaired ventilation or distal perfusion", "Electrical burn with deep muscle injury, rhabdomyolysis and cardiac arrhythmia", "Smoke inhalation with carbon monoxide toxicity and falsely normal pulse oximetry", "Older adult with a small burn but high mortality and comorbidity limiting resuscitation", "Burn pattern inconsistent with the reported mechanism suggesting abuse or neglect"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
