 "Symptoms often begin 6–48 hours after exposure", "Bloody diarrhea with high fever"], "exam": ["Diffuse abdominal tenderness", "Rose spots or splenomegaly suggest enteric fever rather than simple gastroenteritis"], "labs": ["Stool culture grows Salmonella", "Blood culture positive in bacteremia"], "imaging": ["Usually normal; CT may show ileocolitis"]}}, {"name": "Shigella infection", "slug": "shigellosis", "why_considered": "Bloody diarrhea with fever, cramps and tenesmus", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Very low-volume bloody stools with severe tenesmus and fever", "discriminators": {"history": ["Symptoms begin 1–3 days after exposure", "Tenesmus and small-volume bloody stools", "Person-to-person spread in daycare or crowded settings"], "exam": ["Lower abdominal tenderness", "Signs of dehydration"], "labs": ["Stool culture grows Shigella", "Fecal leukocytes present"], "imaging": ["Not routinely needed; CT may show colitis"]}}, {"name": "Escherichia coli O157:H7 infection", "slug": "e-coli-o157", "why_considered": "Bloody diarrhea after eating undercooked ground beef or contaminated produce", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Bloody diarrhea without high fever, especially after ground beef, with pallor or decreasing urine output", "discriminators": {"history": ["Exposure to undercooked ground beef, unpasteurized milk or contaminated produce", "Bloody diarrhea with minimal or no fever", "May progress to hemolytic uremic syndrome"], "exam": ["Abdominal tenderness", "Pallor, petechiae or edema suggest hemolytic uremic syndrome"], "labs": ["Stool culture on sorbitol-MacConkey agar or Shiga toxin testing", "Anemia, thrombocytopenia and elevated creatinine suggest hemolytic uremic syndrome"], "imaging": ["Not routinely needed"]}}, {"name": "Viral gastroenteritis", "slug": "viral-gastroenteritis", "why_considered": "Acute watery diarrhea, nausea, vomiting and cramps", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Vomiting often precedes diarrhea", "Symptom