ting or drinking; no systemic symptoms; heals in 7–14 days without scarring", "moderate": "Several ulcers or a larger ulcer causing significant pain that limits eating, drinking or talking; may have tender cervical lymph nodes; no dehydration or systemic illness", "severe": "Numerous or large (over 1 cm) ulcers, or continuous crops that interfere with oral intake; dehydration or weight loss may develop; consider major aphthous ulcers or an underlying systemic cause", "critical": "Not applicable; canker sores are not life-threatening, but severe cases can cause dehydration, especially in children, which requires urgent supportive care"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Ulcer persisting beyond 3 weeks or failing to heal", "meaning": "Needs biopsy to exclude malignancy or a blistering disease"}, {"finding": "Indurated, rolled or everted ulcer edges", "meaning": "Concerning for squamous cell carcinoma"}, {"finding": "Genital ulcers, eye redness or pain, or skin lesions", "meaning": "Suggests Behçet syndrome or another systemic inflammatory disease"}, {"finding": "Fever, weight loss, diarrhea or blood in stool", "meaning": "Suggests inflammatory bowel disease, celiac disease or infection"}, {"finding": "Inability to eat or drink, drooling, or signs of dehydration", "meaning": "Requires urgent supportive care, especially in children"}, {"finding": "New medication started before the ulcers appeared", "meaning": "Consider drug-induced ulceration and review the medication list"}], "complications": [{"name": "Dehydration and poor oral intake", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Refusal to eat or drink", "Drooling in children", "Dry mucous membranes", "Weight loss"]}, {"name": "Secondary bacterial infection of the ulcer", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Increasing pain, swelling or purulent discharge", "Fever", "Enlarging erythema"]}, {"name": "Scarring from major aphthous ulcers", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Ulcers larger than 1 cm", "Healing with a visible scar"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Young children cannot describe the sore and present with crying, refusing to eat or drink, drooling and irritability; dehydration is the main concern and may require urgent care."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Ulcers are larger, more numerous and slower to heal; consider herpes simplex, cytomegalovirus, fungal infection or drug-induced mucositis rather than simple recurrent aphthous ulcers."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "A new oral ulcer is less likely to be simple recurrent aphthous ulcers; evaluate for medication effects, nutritional deficiency and malignancy, and biopsy nonhealing lesions."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Hormonal changes can trigger or worsen recurrent aphthous ulcers; treatment is limited to topical agents and acetaminophen, avoiding systemic agents that are unsafe in pregnancy."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "Where in your mouth is the sore, and what does it look like?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Inside the lips, cheeks, tongue, floor of mouth or soft palate; a shallow round or oval ulcer with a gray-white center and a red rim on movable mucosa"}, {"question": "How long has this sore been there, and have you had sores like this before?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Recurrent crops over months to years, each lasting about 7–14 days and healing without scarring; a first or nonhealing ulcer raises concern for another cause"}, {"question": "Did you notice any tingling or burning before the sore appeared?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "A brief burning or tingling spot a day or two before the ulcer, typical of aphthous ulcers"}, {"question": "What makes the pain worse, and what helps?", "ca