and paresthesias in the hands", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Symptoms in multiple areas of the body over time", "Visual changes, bowel or bladder dysfunction, or gait difficulty", "Symptoms not confined to the median nerve distribution and not nocturnal"], "exam": ["Hyperreflexia, spasticity, or a sensory level", "Optic neuritis or internuclear ophthalmoplegia", "Normal Tinel and Phalen tests"], "imaging": ["Brain and spinal cord MRI showing demyelinating lesions"]}}, {"name": "Hypothyroidism", "slug": "hypothyroidism", "why_considered": "Hypothyroidism causes soft tissue swelling that can compress the median nerve and is a treatable contributor", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation", "Dry skin and hair loss"], "exam": ["Diffuse goiter or thyroid enlargement", "Delayed relaxation phase of deep tendon reflexes", "Bradycardia"], "labs": ["Elevated TSH with low free T4"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Intermittent nocturnal numbness and tingling in the median nerve distribution, no weakness, normal sensation on exam, symptoms relieved by shaking the hand or a night splint", "moderate": "More frequent symptoms including daytime paresthesias with activity, mild sensory loss in the median distribution, mild thumb abduction weakness, no thenar wasting", "severe": "Constant numbness, clear sensory loss, thenar muscle wasting, and significant thumb weakness with difficulty with fine motor tasks; nerve conduction studies show marked compression"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Rapidly progressive weakness or numbness over days to weeks", "meaning": "Suggests a more aggressive process such as a mass, infection, or inflammatory neuropathy rather than idiopathic carpal tunnel syndrome"}, {"finding": "Symptoms extending beyond the median nerve distribution or involving the lower extremities", "meaning": "Suggests polyneuropathy, radiculopathy, or a central nervous system process rather than isolated median nerve compression"}, {"finding": "Severe pain with focal bony tenderness or deformity after trauma", "meaning": "Suggests fracture, dislocation, or other structural injury requiring urgent imaging"}, {"finding": "Bilateral symptoms with systemic features such as fever, weight loss, or night sweats", "meaning": "Suggests an underlying systemic or inflammatory condition"}, {"finding": "Signs of acute arterial insufficiency (pallor, pulselessness, pain, paresthesias, paralysis)", "meaning": "Requires emergency evaluation for vascular compromise"}], "complications": [{"name": "Permanent median nerve sensory loss", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Constant numbness rather than intermittent tingling", "Symptoms present for months to years without treatment", "Abnormal two-point discrimination"]}, {"name": "Thenar muscle atrophy and permanent thumb weakness", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Visible flattening of the thenar eminence", "Weak thumb abduction and opposition", "Difficulty with pinching and fine motor tasks"]}, {"name": "Postoperative scar tenderness or pillar pain", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Pain at the base of the palm after carpal tunnel release", "Tenderness over the thenar or hypothenar eminence"]}, {"name": "Recurrence after carpal tunnel release", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Return of nocturnal paresthesias months to years after surgery", "Recurrent median nerve compression on nerve conduction studies"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Symptoms often begin in the second or third trimester, are frequently bilateral, and usually resolve after delivery; conservative treatment with night splints is preferred."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Carpal tunnel syndrome is more common and can be more severe; symptoms may be attributed to diabetic peripheral neuropathy, so careful median nerve distribution testing is important."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "May present with constant numbness and thenar wasting rather than intermittent tingling; pain may be less prominent and recovery after surgery may be slower."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Higher risk and often more severe symptoms; may be bilateral and less responsive to conservative treatment."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "Which fingers feel numb or tingly?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Thumb, index, middle, and radial half of the ring finger, with the little finger spared"}, {"question": "When do the symptoms happen — do they wake you at night?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Nocturnal symptoms that wake the patient from sleep, worse with the wrists bent"}, {"question": "What do you do when the symptoms wake you up?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Shaking or flicking the hand to relieve symptoms (flick sign)"}, {"question": "Is it one hand or both?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Often starts in the dominant hand but frequently becomes bilateral over time"}, {"question": "How long has this been going on, and is it getting worse?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Gradual onset over weeks to months; progression from intermittent night symptoms to daytime and constant numbness"}, {"question": "Have you noticed weakness, dropping things, or trouble with buttons or holding a cup?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Grip weakness, clumsiness, difficulty with fine motor tasks suggesting more advanced disease"}, {"question": "Does the numbness or pain spread up your arm or into your neck?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "May