"Usually asymptomatic and found on routine prenatal screening; untreated infection is linked to preterm birth, low birth weight, and neonatal conjunctivitis or pneumonia. Screening at the first prenatal visit is standard, and treatment with azithromycin or amoxicillin is recommended."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "Genital chlamydia in a prepubertal child raises concern for sexual abuse; infection can also present as neonatal conjunctivitis or afebrile pneumonia in infants born to infected mothers."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "People with HIV or other immunosuppression may have more persistent or severe symptoms and higher rates of repeat infection, and co-infection with gonorrhea is more likely."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Less often considered, so diagnosis is delayed; presentation is the same but clinicians may attribute discharge or dysuria to urinary tract infection or atrophy instead."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "Do you have any symptoms, or are you here because a partner tested positive or because of routine screening?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Many patients are asymptomatic; partner notification or screening is a common reason for presentation"}, {"question": "What symptoms have you noticed — discharge, burning with urination, pelvic or belly pain, bleeding between periods or after sex?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Thin discharge, dysuria, postcoital or intermenstrual bleeding, or pelvic pain"}, {"question": "When did your symptoms start?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Gradual onset about 1–3 weeks after exposure; often present for weeks before care is sought"}, {"question": "Have you had a new sex partner or more than one partner in the past year?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "New or multiple partners raises pretest probability and guides testing"}, {"question": "Do you use condoms? How consistently?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Inconsistent or no condom use"}, {"question": "Do you have sex with men, women, or both? Do you have vaginal, anal, or oral sex?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Determines which sites to test — urine or vaginal/cervical, rectal, and pharyngeal"}, {"question": "Has a current or past partner been diagnosed with an STI, or had discharge or burning with urination?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Partner with a known STI or urethral symptoms strongly supports the diagnosis"}, {"question": "Have you ever had an STI before, including chlamydia or gonorrhea?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prior STI increases risk of reinfection and complications"}, {"question": "Could you be pregnant? When was your first day of your last period?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Pregnancy changes treatment choices and, with pelvic pain, ectopic pregnancy must be excluded"}, {"question": "Do you have any lower belly pain, pain with sex, or fever?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Suggests ascending infection such as pelvic inflammatory disease"}, {"question": "Any pain or swelling in one testicle?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Epididymitis; sudden severe pain suggests torsion instead"}, {"question": "Any rectal pain, discharge, or bleeding? Any sore throat?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Rectal or pharyngeal infection based on exposure"}, {"question": "Any joint pain or swelling, eye redness, or rash?", "category": "ros", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Reactive arthritis or another STI such as secondary syphilis"}, {"question": "Are you taking any antibiotics or other medications right now?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recent antibiotics may affect test results; drug interactions matter for treatment choice"}, {"question": "Do you have any medication allergies, especially to antibiotics?", "category": "allergies", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Penicillin or cephalosporin allergy changes the regimen"}, {"question": "Do you have any concerns about safety at home, or has anyone pressured you into sex?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Intimate partner violence or coercion, which changes counseling and follow-up"}], "accepted_names": ["Chlamydia", "Chlamydia infection", "Chlamydial infection", "Chlamydia trachomatis infection", "CT infection", "Chlamydial urethritis", "Chlamydial cervicitis"], "reasoning_points": ["Sexual history identifies a new or multiple partners, inconsistent condom use, or a partner with a known STI", "Symptoms of urethritis or cervicitis — discharge, dysuria, postcoital bleeding — or the patient may be entirely asymptomatic", "Onset about 1–3 weeks after exposure fits the incubation period", "Exam may show mucopurulent cervical discharge, a friable cervix, or urethral discharge; cervical motion tenderness suggests pelvic inflammatory disease", "Sterile pyuria on urinalysis supports urethritis rather than a urinary tract infection", "Nucleic acid amplification testing of the appropriate sites confirms the diagnosis", "Gonorrhea co-infection, HIV, and syphilis should be tested at the same visit", "Pregnancy must be excluded before treatment and because of the risk of ectopic pregnancy with pelvic pain", "Partners must be treated and the patient should abstain from sex until therapy is completed"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Sexually active young adult with a new partner and no symptoms, presenting after a partner tested positive", "Thin urethral discharge with dysuria in a young man", "Mucopurulent cervical discharge with postcoital bleeding in a young woman"], "medium": ["Asymptomatic patient detected on routine screening with no complaints at all", "Dysuria with sterile pyuria and a negative urine culture", "Rectal infection presenting with rectal pain or discharge", "Patient with concurrent gonorrhea and chlamydia"], "hard": ["Pelvic inflammatory disease with fever and cervical motion tenderness where chlamydia is the underlying cause", "Epididymitis with unilateral testicular pain where torsion must first be excluded", "Pregnant patient found on routine prenatal screening with no symptoms", "Older adult whose discharge or dysuria is mistakenly attributed to urinary tract infection or atrophy", "Prepubertal child with genital chlamydia raising concern for sexual abuse"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
