ged bleeding after procedures"]}, {"name": "Portal vein thrombosis", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Sudden worsening ascites or abdominal pain", "Imaging showing filling defect in portal vein", "Variceal bleeding"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "older_adults", "differences": "May present with confusion, falls, or failure to thrive rather than classic stigmata; symptoms are often attributed to aging. Medication sensitivity is increased due to reduced hepatic metabolism."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Can be masked by normal pregnancy changes; may present with severe pruritus, jaundice, or liver test abnormalities overlapping with intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy or HELLP syndrome. Variceal bleeding risk increases with pregnancy."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Often asymptomatic and detected on routine labs; may present with unexplained hypoglycemia or worsening glycemic control due to impaired hepatic glucose handling. Metformin should be avoided in severe hepatic impairment."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Abdominal swelling may be attributed to central adiposity, delaying recognition of ascites; metabolic steatotic liver disease is the likely underlying cause. Weight loss and metabolic risk factor management are key."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Creatinine may underestimate renal impairment due to reduced muscle mass; avoid nephrotoxins and adjust medication doses. Hepatorenal syndrome is a feared complication."}, {"population": "hepatic_impairment", "differences": "Already known liver disease may present with decompensation such as encephalopathy, bleeding, or infection rather than new symptoms of cirrhosis itself. Medication dosing must be adjusted for hepatic impairment."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "How much alcohol have you drunk over your lifetime, and for how many years?", "category": "social", "importanc