V polymerase chain reaction (PCR) of lesion swab", "why": "Not routine for a classic recurrent cold sore"}, {"test": "csf", "why": "Only if meningitis or encephalitis is suspected, which is not part of uncomplicated cold sores"}, {"test": "MRI brain without and with contrast", "why": "Only if herpes encephalitis is suspected"}], "decision_points": ["A classic recurrent vesicle at the lip margin is diagnosed clinically; testing is reserved for atypical, severe, or immunocompromised presentations", "Consider referral or urgent evaluation if there is eye involvement, widespread skin lesions, or neurologic symptoms", "In children, assess hydration and ability to drink; dehydration may require supportive care"], "criteria": [], "scores": []}, "differential": [{"name": "Herpetic whitlow", "slug": "herpetic-whitlow", "why_considered": "HSV infection of the finger can produce a painful vesicular lesion that may be mistaken for a cold sore when the patient reports a hand lesion or when the history is unclear.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Lesion on a finger or thumb, often after contact with a person who has a cold sore", "Occupational exposure such as dental or health care work", "No lip or perioral involvement"], "exam": ["Tense, painful vesicles on an erythematous base on the finger", "Local tenderness and swelling of the digit"], "labs": ["HSV PCR of the lesion can confirm if needed"], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Herpes zoster ophthalmicus", "slug": "herpes-zoster", "why_considered": "A vesicular rash near the mouth or face could be confused with a cold sore, but zoster follows a dermatomal pattern and can involve the eye.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Vesicles in a dermatomal distribution, especially near the eye or forehead, or any eye redness or pain", "discriminators": {"history": ["Pain or tingling in a band-like distribution before the rash", "Rash on one side of the face, often forehead or cheek", "Eye redness, pain, or blurred vision if the ophthalmic branch is involved"], "exam": ["Vesicles grouped in a dermatomal pattern that does not cross the midline", "Conjunctival injection, corneal lesion, or decreased visual acuity if the eye is involved"], "labs": ["Varicella-zoster virus PCR of the lesion if the diagnosis is uncertain"], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Aphthous ulcers (canker sores)", "slug": "aphthous-ulcers", "why_considered": "Painful mouth ulcers are a common complaint and can be mistaken for cold sores, especially when they occur inside the mouth.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Ulcers inside the mouth, often on the tongue, buccal mucosa, or soft palate", "No tingling or burning before the ulcer appears", "No vesicle stage; ulcers start as a round, shallow sore with a yellow-white center and red rim", "Not contagious; no known viral trigger"], "exam": ["Single or few round, shallow ulcers with a gray-white base and erythematous halo", "No lip or perioral vesicles"], "labs": [], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Hand-foot-and-mouth disease", "slug": "hand-foot-and-mouth-disease", "why_considered": "Coxsackievirus infection can cause mouth sores and a rash, and in children it may present with fever and refusal to eat.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Fever, sore throat, and rash on hands and feet", "Recent contact with a child with similar symptoms", "Mouth sores are usually inside the mouth, not on the lip margin"], "exam": ["Vesicles or ulcers on the palms, soles, and sometimes buttocks", "Mouth ulcers on the tongue, gums, and buccal mucosa", "No lip margin vesicles"], "labs": ["Clinical diagnosis; viral PCR or serology rarely needed"], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Herpangina", "slug": "herpangina", "why_considered": "Another enteroviral illness that causes fever and painful mouth ulcers, often in children.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden fever, sore throat, and refusal to eat", "No lip tingling or cold sore history"], "exam": ["Small vesicles or ulcers on the soft palate, tonsils, and uvula", "No lip or perioral lesions"], "labs": [], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Herpes simplex keratitis", "slug": "herpes-simplex-keratitis", "why_considered": "HSV can infect the eye and cause a painful red eye with a dendritic corneal lesion; this can occur with or without a cold sore.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Eye redness, pain, tearing, or blurred vision in a patient with a cold sore or recent HSV infection", "discriminators": {"history": ["Eye pain, redness, tearing, or photophobia", "Blurred vision or foreign body sensation", "May have a history of cold sores"], "exam": ["Conjunctival injection, corneal haze, or a dendritic ulcer on fluorescein staining", "Decreased visual acuity"], "labs": ["HSV PCR of corneal scraping if needed, but usually clinical"], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Squamous cell carcinoma of the lip", "slug": "squamous-cell-carcinoma-lip", "why_considered": "A non-healing lip lesion can be mistaken for a cold sore, especially if it persists beyond the typical 2 weeks.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "A lip sore that does not heal within 3 weeks, is indurated, or bleeds easily", "discriminators": {"history": ["Lesion present for weeks to months and progressively enlarging", "No prodromal tingling or vesicle stage", "History of sun exposure, smoking, or alcohol use"], "exam": ["Indurated, ulcerated, or crusted lesion on the lip that may be painless", "May have palpable cervical lymph nodes"], "labs": [], "imaging": ["Biopsy is required for diagnosis; imaging of the neck may be considered if nodes are present"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Prodromal tingling or a small cluster of vesicles at the lip margin; no fever, no systemic symptoms, able to eat and drink normally.", "moderate": "Painful vesicles or ulcers with local tenderness, mild fever, tender submandibular lymph nodes, and discomfort with eating; no dehydration.", "severe": "First episode with widespread oral ulcers (gingivostomatitis), fever, significant pain, difficulty eating or drinking, and possible mild dehydration; or recurrent outbreaks that are unusually large or frequent.", "critical": "Disseminated HSV infection with widespread skin lesions, hepatitis, pneumonitis, or central nervous system involvement (encephalitis or meningitis); this is rare and occurs mainly in immunocompromised patients or neonates."}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Eye redness, pain, or blurred vision", "meaning": "Possible herpes simplex keratitis; urgent ophthalmology evaluation is needed to prevent corneal damage."}, {"finding": "Severe headache, confusion, neck stiffness, or seizures", "meaning": "Possible herpes encephalitis or meningitis; requires urgent evaluation and treatment."}, {"finding": "Widespread vesicles beyond the lip or mouth, especially in an immunocompromised patient", "meaning": "Possible disseminated HSV infection; needs urgent antiviral therapy."}, {"finding": "Inability to drink, drooling, or signs of dehydration in a child", "meaning": "Severe gingivostomatitis with dehydration; may require intravenous fluids and hospitalization."}, {"finding": "Lip sore that does not heal within 3 weeks or is indurated and bleeding", "meaning": "Possible squamous cell carcinoma; requires biopsy."}, {"finding": "Neonate with fever, poor feeding, lethargy, or vesicles", "meaning": "Possible neonatal HSV infection; a medical emergency requiring immediate evaluation and treatment."}], "complications": [{"name": "Herpes simplex keratitis", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Eye redness, pain, tearing, or blurred vision", "Dendritic corneal ulcer on fluorescein staining"]}, {"name": "Eczema herpeticum", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Widespread vesicular rash in a patient with atopic dermatitis", "Fever and malaise"]}, {"name": "Herpes encephalitis", "timing": "any", "seriousness":