n deficiency anemia with low ferritin", "Elevated CEA (not diagnostic, used for monitoring)"], "imaging": ["Colonoscopy shows an ulcerated, friable, or circumferential mass rather than a smooth mucosal protrusion", "Biopsy confirms invasive adenocarcinoma", "CT or MRI shows invasion beyond the bowel wall or nodal or distant disease"]}}, {"name": "Diverticular disease", "slug": "diverticular-disease", "why_considered": "A common cause of intermittent bright red rectal bleeding in older adults, the same symptom that can prompt evaluation for polyps.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Known diverticulosis", "Painless or mildly painful episodes of bleeding", "Left lower quadrant discomfort with fever if diverticulitis"], "exam": ["Left lower quadrant tenderness in diverticulitis", "Usually normal rectal exam"], "labs": ["Leukocytosis in acute diverticulitis", "Anemia possible with chronic bleeding"], "imaging": ["Colonoscopy shows diverticula (outpouchings) rather than protruding polyps", "CT shows colonic wall thickening and pericolonic fat stranding in diverticulitis"]}}, {"name": "Hemorrhoids", "slug": "hemorrhoids", "why_considered": "A very common cause of bright red blood on toilet paper after a bowel movement, which is also a presenting symptom of distal polyps.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Bleeding is typically painless and occurs after defecation", "Associated with straining, constipation, or prolonged sitting", "No change in bowel habit"], "exam": ["Visible external hemorrhoids or prolapsing internal hemorrhoids on inspection", "Digital rectal exam may be normal or reveal a tender perianal mass"], "labs": ["Usually normal; anemia is uncommon unless bleeding is chronic and heavy"], "imaging": ["Colonoscopy shows no polyps; hemorrhoids are seen on anoscopy or external inspection"]}}, {"name": "Inflammatory bowel disease", "slug": "inflammatory-bowel-disease", "why_considered": "Can cause diarrhea, mucus in the 