in", "Large or multiple polyps"]}, {"name": "Malignant transformation to colorectal cancer", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Advanced adenoma on histology", "Large polyp over 10 mm", "Villous histology or high-grade dysplasia"]}, {"name": "Post-polypectomy bleeding", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Rectal bleeding within days after colonoscopy", "Large or right-sided polyps removed", "Use of anticoagulants or antiplatelet agents"]}, {"name": "Colonic perforation", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Severe abdominal pain after colonoscopy", "Rigid abdomen, fever, hypotension"]}, {"name": "Colonic obstruction", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Abdominal distension, vomiting", "Inability to pass stool or gas", "Large distal polyp or mass"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "older_adults", "differences": "More likely to have larger, right-sided, or advanced polyps; anemia from occult blood loss may be the only clue. Screening decisions should account for overall health and life expectancy."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Higher risk of multiple and advanced polyps; screening may start earlier or be repeated more often."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Associated with a higher prevalence of adenomas and a modestly increased risk of progression, but presentation is still usually asymptomatic."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Colonoscopy is generally deferred to the second trimester if needed; sigmoidoscopy may be preferred for left-sided symptoms. Polyps are usually managed after delivery unless malignancy is suspected."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Higher risk of complications from colonoscopy and polypectomy; consider antibiotic prophylaxis and closer monitoring."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "When was your last colonoscopy, and what did it show?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Prior polyps or prior colorectal cancer; establishes personal history and surveillance interval"}, {"question": "Has anyone in your family had colon polyps or colon cancer? How old were they when it was found?", "category": "family", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "First-degree relative with colorectal cancer or polyps, especially before age 60; suggests hereditary risk and earlier screening"}, {"question": "Have you noticed any blood on the toilet paper, in the stool, or on your underwear?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Intermittent small-volume bright red bleeding, the most common symptom when polyps cause symptoms"}, {"question": "Have your bowel habits changed in the last few weeks?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Constipation or diarrhea lasting more than a week, or a persistent change from baseline"}, {"question": "Have you had any weight loss, fevers, or night sweats?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Constitutional symptoms that argue against a benign polyp and raise concern for malignancy"}, {"question": "Have you been told you are anemic, or have you felt unusually tired or short of breath?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Iron deficiency anemia from chronic occult blood loss"}, {"question": "Have you had a stool test for colon cancer screening, and was it positive?", "category": "ros", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Positive FIT or stool DNA test, which requires diagnostic colonoscopy"}, {"question": "Do you have any history of inflammatory bowel disease, such as ulcerative colitis or Crohn disease?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Long-standing colitis, which increases polyp and cancer risk"}, {"question": "Do you smoke, drink alcohol, or eat