": "Infants may present with inconsolable crying, vomiting, poor feeding, lethargy, or irritability. A history of trauma may be absent or unclear. Non-accidental trauma should be considered."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "What happened? Did you hit your head or have a blow to the body?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "A blunt head impact or a rapid acceleration-deceleration event such as a fall, collision, or whiplash"}, {"question": "Did you lose consciousness? If so, for how long?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Brief loss of consciousness (seconds to a minute) or none at all; longer than a few minutes suggests more severe injury"}, {"question": "Do you remember what happened right before and right after the injury?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Amnesia for events before or after the impact, which supports the diagnosis"}, {"question": "Were you confused, dazed, or 'not yourself' right after the hit?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Transient confusion or disorientation immediately after the injury"}, {"question": "When did your symptoms start?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Immediately after the injury, though some symptoms may not be noticed for hours"}, {"question": "What makes your symptoms worse?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Physical exertion, mental concentration, bright lights, loud noises, or rapid head movements"}, {"question": "Have you had a concussion or head injury before?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prior concussion, which increases risk and can prolong recovery"}, {"question": "Do you take any blood thinners or antiplatelet medicines?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Anticoagulants (warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban) or antiplatelet agents (aspirin, clopidogrel), which raise the risk of intracranial bleeding"}, {"question": "Have you vomited? How many times?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "One episode may occur with concussion; repeated vomiting is a red flag for increased intracranial pressure"}, {"question": "Do you have any weakness, numbness, trouble speaking, or trouble walking?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Focal neurologic deficits, which suggest a structural brain injury rather than simple concussion"}, {"question": "Is your headache getting worse over time, or is it the worst headache of your life?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "A progressively worsening or thunderclap headache, which raises concern for hemorrhage"}, {"question": "Have you had any seizures or convulsions?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Seizure activity, which indicates a more severe brain injury"}, {"question": "Is there any clear fluid or blood coming from your nose or ears?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "CSF leak or bleeding, suggesting a basilar skull fracture"}, {"question": "Do you have neck pain, especially in the middle of the back of your neck?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Midline cervical tenderness, which may indicate a cervical spine injury"}, {"question": "Are you having trouble with your vision, like blurriness or double vision?", "category": "ros", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Visual symptoms such as blurred or double vision, which are common after concussion"}, {"question": "How is your sleep? Are you sleeping more or less than usual?", "category": "ros", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Sleep disturbances, which are common after concussion"}, {"question": "Do you play any contact sports or have you had a recent fall?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Athletic participation or fall risk, which helps identify the mechanism and risk of recurrence"}], "accepted_names": ["Concussion", "Mild traumatic brain injury", "mTBI", "Mild TBI", "Brain concussion"], "reasoning_points": ["A blunt head trauma or acceleration-deceleration force occurred", "Transient confusion, disorientation, or impaired awareness was present after the injury", "Brief loss of consciousness or amnesia around the event may have occurred", "Symptoms such as headache, dizziness, nausea, balance problems, or visual changes are present", "Glasgow Coma Scale is 13-15 with no focal neurologic deficit", "No structural intracranial injury is seen on imaging when imaging is performed", "Red flags for more severe injury (repeated vomiting, worsening headache, focal deficits, seizure, prolonged loss of consciousness) are absent"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Adolescent athlete with a clear blow to the head during a game", "Brief confusion or dazed appearance witnessed by others", "Headache, dizziness, and nausea that worsen with exertion", "Normal neurologic exam and GCS 15"], "medium": ["Older adult who fell and now has confusion and balance problems without a clear headache", "Patient on an anticoagulant with a minor head strike and mild symptoms", "Symptoms that began hours after the injury rather than immediately", "Neck pain accompanying the head injury, requiring cervical spine assessment"], "hard": ["Young child with irritability, vomiting, and poor feeding but no clear history of trauma", "Patient with pre-existing migraine or ADHD whose symptoms are attributed to those conditions", "Older adult with subtle cognitive changes mistaken for dementia or delirium", "Patient with a normal head CT but persistent symptoms raising concern for another diagnosis"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
