n not limited to pressure points"], "exam": ["Well-demarcated erythematous plaques with silvery scale", "Involvement of palms and soles in a symmetric pattern", "Nail pitting or onycholysis", "Auspitz sign (pinpoint bleeding when scale is removed)"], "labs": [], "imaging": []}}, {"name": "Osteomyelitis", "slug": "osteomyelitis", "why_considered": "Deep foot pain and swelling in a patient with a foot lesion, especially with diabetes", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Fever, deep pain, or a non-healing ulcer over a bony prominence in a patient with diabetes or vascular disease", "discriminators": {"history": ["Fever, chills, or malaise", "Deep, constant pain that may be worse at night", "Non-healing ulcer or recent foot infection"], "exam": ["Fever", "Focal bone tenderness", "Overlying ulcer or sinus tract", "Surrounding cellulitis"], "labs": ["Elevated white blood cell count, CRP, and ESR", "Blood cultures may be positive"], "imaging": ["Foot x-ray showing bone destruction, periosteal reaction, or lucency", "MRI showing bone marrow edema and enhancement"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Small, painless or minimally tender area of thickened skin that does not limit activity", "moderate": "Thicker, painful lesion that causes discomfort with walking or manual work but no skin breakdown", "severe": "Large or multiple painful lesions that limit weight-bearing or hand use, or are associated with significant disability", "critical": "Not applicable; corns and calluses are not life-threatening"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Open wound, ulceration, or drainage", "meaning": "Suggests ulceration or infection; urgent evaluation and treatment are needed, especially in diabetes"}, {"finding": "Spreading redness, warmth, or fever", "meaning": "Suggests cellulitis or deeper infection; requires prompt medical evaluation"}, {"finding": "Loss of sensation in the feet", "meaning": "Suggests peripheral neuropathy; the patient may not feel the lesion and is at high risk for ulceration"}, {"finding": "Absent or diminished pedal pulses", "meaning": "Suggests peripheral arterial disease; poor healing and higher risk of infection"}, {"finding": "Rapidly growing, bleeding, or non-healing lesion", "meaning": "Raises concern for malignancy or infection; biopsy or further workup may be needed"}], "complications": [{"name": "Ulceration", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Break in the skin over the lesion", "Pain or drainage", "Diabetes or neuropathy"]}, {"name": "Cellulitis", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Spreading redness", "Warmth", "Fever"]}, {"name": "Osteomyelitis", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Deep pain", "Non-healing ulcer over a bony prominence", "Fever and elevated inflammatory markers"]}, {"name": "Chronic pain and gait changes", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Long-standing lesion", "Limping", "Avoidance of weight-bearing"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Neuropathy may mask pain, so lesions can go unnoticed until they ulcerate or become infected; any break in the skin needs urgent attention and offloading."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Thinning fat pads and foot deformities make calluses more likely and more painful; skin is thinner and more prone to breakdown."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "Often related to sports, new shoes, or going barefoot; lesions are usually less thick and less painful than in adults."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "Where exactly is the thickened skin, and how long has it been there?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "A focal area over a pressure point (tops or sides of toes, between toes, ball of the foot, heel, palms or fingers) present for weeks to months"}, {"question": "What kind of shoes do you wear, and have you changed footwear recently?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Tight, narrow, high-heeled, or poorly fitting shoes; new shoes or boots; identifies the friction source"}, {"question": "Do you stand or walk for long periods at work or during activities?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Occupational or recreational prolonged standing, walking, running, or repetitive manual work"}, {"question": "Does it hurt when you press directly on it, or when you squeeze it from the sides?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Pain with direct pressure favors a corn or callus; pain with side-to-side squeezing favors a plantar wart"}, {"question": "Have you noticed any break in the skin, drainage, or an open sore?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Any ulceration, fissure, or drainage, which suggests breakdown or infection rather than a simple pressure lesion"}, {"question": "Do you have diabetes, poor circulation, or numbness or tingling in your feet?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Diabetes, peripheral arterial disease, or peripheral neuropathy, which raise the risk of ulceration and infection"}, {"question": "Have you tried to cut, shave, or use medicated pads or acids on it?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Self-treatment that may have caused injury or infection; medicated corn plasters can damage skin"}, {"question": "Is the area red, warm, swollen, or spreading?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Signs of cellulitis or deeper infection"}, {"question": "Have you had a puncture wound, stepped on something, or had a foreign body in the foot or hand?", "category": "associated", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "A retained foreign body or granuloma that can mimic a focal callus"}, {"question": "Do you have any other skin conditions, such as psoriasis or athlete's foot?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Palmoplantar psoriasis or tinea pedis that can cause thickened, scaly skin"}, {"question": "Any fever, chills, or feeling unwell?", "category": "ros", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Systemic symptoms suggesting infection; usually absent in uncomplicated corns and calluses"}, {"question": "Is the lesion growing, bleeding, or failing to heal despite removing pressure?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Features concerning for malignancy or a non-healing ulcer, which require biopsy or urgent evaluation"}], "accepted_names": ["Corns and calluses", "Corn", "Callus", "Calluses", "Clavus", "Tyloma", "Heloma", "Hyperkeratosis due to pressure or friction"], "reasoning_points": ["A focal area of thickened, hardened skin at a site of repeated pressure or friction", "Location over a pressure point: tops or sides of toes and between toes for corns, soles and palms for calluses", "Gradual onset over weeks to months, worse with walking, standing, or manual work", "Preserved skin markings and no black dots, favoring a corn or callus over a wart", "Skin is intact with no ulceration, drainage, or surrounding cellulitis", "Sensation and pedal pulses are intact, making neuropathy and peripheral arterial disease less likely", "Symptoms improve with removal of pressure, better-fitting shoes, and non-medicated padding", "Diabetes, neuropathy, or poor circulation are checked for because they change management and raise the risk of ulceration"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Adult with a hard, thickened patch on the sole or a toe that hurts when walking", "Clear history of tight or poorly fitting shoes or prolonged standing", "Well-circumscribed hyperkeratosis with preserved skin markings and no ulceration", "Symptoms improve with offloading and better footwear"], "medium": ["Lesion between the toes that is hard to see and mimics tinea pedis", "Painful lesion on the ball of the foot with an underlying bony prominence or hammertoe", "Patient with diabetes and a callus that needs careful skin and sensation checks", "Lesion on the hands from repetitive manual work rather than the feet"], "hard": ["Callus over a bony prominence in a patient with neuropathy that is painless and could hide an ulcer", "Lesion that does not heal after pressure is removed, raising concern for malignancy or infection", "Plantar wart mistaken for a callus because of similar location and thickening", "Older adult with thin fat pads, foot deformity, and multiple painful calluses", "Chronic plaque on the sole that is actually palmoplantar psoriasis"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
