h as exercise, allergens, or cold air", "No barking cough or hoarse voice"], "exam": ["Diffuse expiratory wheezing", "Prolonged expiratory phase", "No inspiratory stridor"], "labs": ["Not routinely needed", "Allergy testing or spirometry may support the diagnosis later"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray usually normal; not routine"]}}, {"name": "Retropharyngeal abscess", "slug": "retropharyngeal-abscess", "why_considered": "Child with fever, neck stiffness, and noisy breathing", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Fever with neck stiffness, refusal to move the neck, or drooling", "discriminators": {"history": ["High fever and severe neck pain", "Refusal to move the neck or turn the head", "No barking cough"], "exam": ["Neck stiffness or torticollis", "Drooling or difficulty swallowing", "Muffled voice", "Tender neck or mass"], "labs": ["Marked leukocytosis", "Elevated inflammatory markers"], "imaging": ["Lateral neck x-ray shows widened retropharyngeal soft tissue space", "CT with contrast shows a rim-enhancing fluid collection"]}}, {"name": "Laryngomalacia", "slug": "laryngomalacia", "why_considered": "Infant with noisy breathing and stridor", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Stridor present since birth or the first weeks of life", "Worse when lying on the back, feeding, or crying", "No barking cough or fever"], "exam": ["High-pitched inspiratory stridor that worsens when supine", "No hoarse voice", "No respiratory distress at rest"], "labs": ["Not indicated"], "imaging": ["Flexible laryngoscopy shows omega-shaped epiglottis and prolapsing arytenoids"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Barking cough and hoarse voice with no stridor at rest; child is comfortable, playful, and feeding well", "moderate": "Inspiratory stridor at rest that eases when calm, mild retractions, and some irritability; child still interactive", "severe": "Stridor at rest with marked retractions, nasal flaring, and agitation or distress; child may be tiring", "critical": "Decreasing respiratory effort with lethargy or agitation, pallor or cyanosis, and quiet or absent stridor as obstruction worsens; impending respiratory failure"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Stridor at rest", "meaning": "Significant upper airway obstruction; needs immediate treatment and monitoring"}, {"finding": "Retractions, nasal flaring, or belly breathing", "meaning": "Increased work of breathing; moderate to severe croup"}, {"finding": "Drooling or difficulty swallowing", "meaning": "Suggests epiglottitis or another supraglottic process; do not examine the throat"}, {"finding": "High fever with toxic appearance", "meaning": "Suggests bacterial tracheitis or another bacterial infection"}, {"finding": "Cyanosis, pallor, or lethargy", "meaning": "Hypoxemia and impending respiratory failure"}, {"finding": "Stridor that becomes quieter or absent while the child worsens", "meaning": "Worsening obstruction with decreasing air movement; a late and dangerous sign"}, {"finding": "Sudden onset while eating or choking", "meaning": "Suggests foreign body aspiration"}], "complications": [{"name": "Upper airway obstruction with respiratory failure", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Stridor at rest that worsens", "Marked retractions and agitation", "Decreasing air movement or cyanosis"]}, {"name": "Hypoxemia", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Oxygen saturation below 92%", "Pallor or cyanosis", "Lethargy"]}, {"name": "Dehydration from poor oral intake", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Decreased wet diapers", "Dry mucous membranes", "Refusal to drink"]}, {"name": "Bacterial superinfection such as bacterial tracheitis or pneumonia", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["High fever", "Toxic appearance", "Worsening despite treatment", "Focal lung findings"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "infants", "differences": "Infants under 6 months may have a milder barking cough but can develop severe upper airway obstruction quickly; they may present with poor feeding, irritability, and apnea rather than classic stridor."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "Older children and adolescents can get croup but it is less common; symptoms may be milder and more like a bad cold with hoarseness."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Immunocompromised children may have a more prolonged or severe course and are at higher risk for bacterial superinfection or atypical pathogens."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "What does the cough sound like?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "A barking, seal-like cough rather than a wet or wheezing cough"}, {"question": "Do you hear a high-pitched noise when your child breathes in?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Inspiratory stridor, and whether it is present only with crying or also at rest"}, {"question": "How did this start and how has it changed over the last day or two?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Cold-like prodrome followed by barking cough and hoarseness over 1–2 days; rapid worsening over hours suggests another cause"}, {"question": "Is the noisy breathing worse at night or when your child is upset?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Nighttime and crying-related worsening, typical of croup"}, {"question": "Is your child drooling or having trouble swallowing?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Drooling or refusal to swallow suggests epiglottitis; do not examine the throat"}, {"question": "Did this start suddenly while your child was eating or playing, or was there a choking episode?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Abrupt onset or choking suggests foreign body aspiration"}, {"question": "How is your child acting between coughing spells — playful and interactive, or lethargic and struggling to breathe?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Comfortable and interactive suggests mild croup; lethargy, agitation, or continuous distress suggests severe obstruction"}, {"question": "Has your child been drinking and making normal amounts of urine?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Decreased intake or fewer wet diapers suggests dehydration"}, {"question": "Any fever, runny nose, or hoarse voice or cry?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Low-grade fever, congestion, and hoarseness supporting a viral cause"}, {"question": "Has your child had episodes like this before, and have they ever needed treatment in the emergency department?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recurrent croup raises concern for an underlying airway problem such as laryngomalacia or subglottic stenosis"}, {"question": "Is anyone else at home or daycare sick with a cold?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recent upper respiratory infection in household or daycare contacts"}, {"question": "Has your child been exposed to cigarette smoke?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Secondhand smoke exposure, a risk factor for croup"}, {"question": "Does your child have any allergies, or could this be a reaction to a food, medicine, or insect sting?", "category": "allergies", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Rapid onset after allergen exposure with hives or wheezing suggests anaphylaxis"}, {"question": "Any rash, vomiting, diarrhea, or neck stiffness?", "category": "ros", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Absent in typical croup; present findings point to an alternative diagnosis"}], "accepted_names": ["Croup", "Viral croup", "Spasmodic croup", "Laryngotracheobronchitis", "Acute laryngotracheitis"], "reasoning_points": ["Young child, typically 6 months to 3 years, with a recent cold-like illness", "Barking, seal-like cough with hoarse voice or cry", "High-pitched inspiratory stridor that worsens with crying, agitation, or nighttime", "Symptoms worse at night and over 3–5 days, often worst on nights 2–3", "Child is nontoxic and comfortable between coughing spells, with no drooling or difficulty swallowing", "No expiratory wheezing or crackles, arguing against bronchiolitis or asthma", "Diagnosis is clinical; imaging and labs are reserved for atypical or severe presentations", "Epiglottitis, bacterial tracheitis, foreign body aspiration, and anaphylaxis considered and excluded"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Toddler with a classic barking cough and hoarse voice after a few days of runny nose", "Stridor only when crying or upset, resolving when calm", "Low-grade fever and otherwise well-appearing child", "Symptoms clearly worse at night"], "medium": ["Stridor present at rest with mild retractions, requiring treatment and monitoring", "Infant under 6 months with poor feeding and irritability rather than a clear barking cough", "Older child with milder symptoms that resemble a bad cold with hoarseness", "Recurrent nighttime episodes with normal examination during the day"], "hard": ["Child with high fever and toxic appearance whose stridor does not improve after racemic epinephrine and steroids, suggesting bacterial tracheitis", "Sudden onset of stridor while eating or playing, suggesting foreign body aspiration", "Drooling, muffled voice, and refusal to swallow, suggesting epiglottitis", "Rapid onset of stridor with hives or wheezing after allergen exposure, suggesting anaphylaxis", "Child with recurrent croup raising concern for an underlying airway abnormality such as subglottic stenosis"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
