, "finding": "Clear lungs, no pleuritic chest pain or dyspnea", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "no-fever", "region": "general", "maneuver": "Measure temperature", "finding": "Afebrile", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-skin-break", "region": "skin", "maneuver": "Inspect the skin for wounds, ulcers, or portals of entry", "finding": "No open wound, draining ulcer, or interdigital fissure", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-joint-effusion", "region": "musculoskeletal", "maneuver": "Examine the knee and ankle joints", "finding": "No joint swelling, effusion, or instability", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "no-tenderness-achilles", "region": "musculoskeletal", "maneuver": "Palpate the Achilles tendon and gastrocnemius muscle bellies", "finding": "No focal tendon or muscle tenderness", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}]}, "vitals": {"temp": {"mild": {"min": 98.0, "max": 99.5}, "moderate": {"min": 98.0, "max": 100.0}, "severe": {"min": 98.0, "max": 100.4}, "critical": {"min": 97.0, "max": 101.0}}, "hr": {"mild": {"min": 60, "max": 100}, "moderate": {"min": 70, "max": 110}, "severe": {"min": 80, "max": 120}, "critical": {"min": 100, "max": 140}}, "rr": {"mild": {"min": 12, "max": 20}, "moderate": {"min": 12, "max": 22}, "severe": {"min": 14, "max": 24}, "critical": {"min": 20, "max": 32}}, "sbp": {"mild": {"min": 100, "max": 140}, "moderate": {"min": 100, "max": 140}, "severe": {"min": 90, "max": 140}, "critical": {"min": 70, "max": 100}}, "dbp": {"mild": {"min": 60, "max": 90}, "moderate": {"min": 60, "max": 90}, "severe": {"min": 50, "max": 90}, "critical": {"min": 40, "max": 65}}, "spo2": {"mild": {"min": 95, "max": 100}, "moderate": {"min": 95, "max": 100}, "severe": {"min": 92, "max": 100}, "critical": {"min": 85, "max": 95}}}, "labs": [{"test_id": "d_dimer", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 0.6, "max": 1.5}, "moderate": {"min": 1.0, "max": 3.0}, "severe": {"min": 2.0, "max": 6.0}, "critical": {"min": 3.0, "max": 10.0}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "essential", "caveat": "A negative D-dimer using a validated age-adjusted or highly sensitive assay makes DVT very unlikely and can rule it out in low-risk patients; a positive result is nonspecific and needs imaging. It is not useful when DVT is already highly likely."}, {"test_id": "inr", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 1.2, "max": 1.5}, "moderate": {"min": 1.4, "max": 2.0}, "severe": {"min": 1.8, "max": 3.0}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Baseline coagulation testing before starting anticoagulation; a prolonged INR may reflect liver disease, vitamin K deficiency, or a lupus anticoagulant rather than the clot itself."}, {"test_id": "aptt", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 36, "max": 45}, "moderate": {"min": 42, "max": 60}, "severe": {"min": 55, "max": 90}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Baseline before heparin; a prolonged aPTT can also be seen with a lupus anticoagulant or factor deficiency."}, {"test_id": "plt", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 100, "max": 149}, "moderate": {"min": 60, "max": 120}, "severe": {"min": 30, "max": 80}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Baseline platelet count before heparin; thrombocytopenia raises concern for heparin-induced thrombocytopenia or an underlying hematologic disorder."}, {"test_id": "cr", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 1.2, "max": 1.5}, "moderate": {"min": 1.4, "max": 2.0}, "severe": {"min": 1.8, "max": 3.5}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Renal function guides choice and dosing of anticoagulants, especially low-molecular-weight heparin and direct oral anticoagulants."}, {"test_id": "hgb", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 10.0, "max": 11.9}, "moderate": {"min": 8.0, "max": 10.5}, "severe": {"min": 6.5, "max": 9.0}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Anemia may be from an underlying malignancy, chronic disease, or occult blood loss and affects anticoagulation safety."}, {"test_id": "hcg", "frequency": "almost_always", "importance": "essential", "caveat": "Negative. Required in anyone who could be pregnant before imaging and anticoagulation, since pregnancy changes both the diagnostic strategy and treatment."}], "ecg": {"findings": [{"feature": "rate", "finding": "Sinus tachycardia", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "severe"}, {"feature": "rhythm", "finding": "Sinus rhythm", "frequency": "almost_always", "min_severity": "mild"}, {"feature": "other", "finding": "Right axis deviation or right ventricular strain pattern suggesting pulmonary embolism", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "critical"}]}, "imaging": [{"study": "Compression ultrasound of the lower extremity veins", "modality": "ultrasound", "preferred_when": "First-line test for suspected lower extremity DVT in all patients, including pregnancy", "findings": [{"text": "Noncompressible deep vein (failure to collapse with probe pressure)", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "Thrombus visible within the vein lumen", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Absent or reduced flow on color or spectral Doppler", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Vein distended and enlarged compared with the adjacent artery", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Nondiagnostic study because the deep veins are not adequately visualized", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "CT venography of the lower extremities with IV contrast", "modality": "ct", "preferred_when": "When ultrasound is nondiagnostic or when iliac or pelvic vein thrombosis is suspected", "findings": [{"text": "Filling defect within the deep vein lumen", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Venous enlargement with surrounding soft tissue edema", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "MR venography of the lower extremities", "modality": "mri", "preferred_when": "When ultrasound and CT are inconclusive or contrast is contraindicated", "findings": [{"text": "Filling defect or absent flow signal in the deep veins", "frequency": "common"}]}, {"study": "CT pulmonary angiography", "modality": "ct", "preferred_when": "When pulmonary embolism is suspected from symptoms or signs", "findings": [{"text": "Filling defect in the pulmonary arteries", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Right ventricular enlargement", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) scan", "modality": "nuclear", "preferred_when": "Pregnancy or contrast allergy when pulmonary embolism is suspected", "findings": [{"text": "Mismatched segmental perfusion defect", "frequency": "common"}]}], "workup": {"initial": [{"test": "Compression ultrasound of the lower extremity veins", "why": "First-line imaging to confirm or exclude DVT", "only_if": "First-line imaging to confirm or exclude DVT"}, {"test": "d_dimer", "why": "Rules out DVT in low-risk patients when negative", "only_if": "pretest probability is low and no high-risk features are present"}, {"test": "cbc", "why": "Baseline hemoglobin and platelet count before anticoagulation"}, {"test": "bmp", "why": "Baseline renal function and electrolytes before anticoagulation"}, {"test": "coag", "why": "Baseline PT/INR and aPTT before starting heparin or warfarin"}, {"test": "hcg", "why": "Rules out pregnancy before imaging and anticoagulation"}], "confirmatory": [], "supporting": [{"test": "CT venography of the lower extremities with IV contrast", "why": "Evaluates iliac or pelvic veins when ultrasound is nondiagnostic", "only_if": "ultrasound is nondiagnostic or iliac/pelvic vein thrombosis is suspected"}, {"test": "MR venography of the lower extremities", "why": "Alternative when ultrasound and CT are inconclusive or contrast is contraindicated", "only_if": "ultrasound and CT are inconclusive or contrast is contraindicated"}, {"test": "CT pulmonary angiography", "why": "Looks for concurrent pulmonary embolism", "only_if": "pulmonary embolism is suspected from dyspnea, chest pain, hypoxemia, or tachycardia"}, {"test": "Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) scan", "why": "Looks for pulmonary embolism without iodinated contrast", "only_if": "pulmonary embolism is suspected and the patient is pregnant or has a contrast allergy"}, {"test": "thrombophilia testing", "why": "May identify an inherited or acquired thrombophilia", "only_if": "unprovoked DVT in a young patient, strong family history, recurrent VTE, or unusual site"}, {"test": "Age appropriate cancer screening", "why": "Unprovoked DVT can be the first sign of occult malignancy", "only_if": "DVT is unprovoked and cancer screening is not up to date"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "d_dimer", "why": "Not useful when pretest probability is moderate or high; a positive result does not confirm DVT and a negative result does not exclude it in that setting"}, {"test": "Compression ultrasound of the lower extremity veins", "why": "Not needed when a negative highly sensitive D-dimer has already ruled out DVT in a low-risk patient"}, {"test": "thrombophilia testing", "why": "Not routine during the acute event; results rarely change initial management and testing is best deferred until after anticoagulation"}, {"test": "Age appropriate cancer screening", "why": "Not part of the acute DVT workup; reserved for unprovoked events after the acute phase"}, {"test": "Ct chest", "why": "Not indicated unless pulmonary embolism is suspected"}], "decision_points": ["Use a validated clinical probability score (Wells or Geneva) before ordering D-dimer or ultrasound", "In low-probability patients, a negative highly sensitive or age-adjusted D-dimer excludes DVT without imaging", "In moderate- or high-probability patients, go straight to compression ultrasound", "If the initial ultrasound is negative but suspicion remains high, repeat ultrasound in 5-7 days or check D-dimer", "Pregnancy test before CT or anticoagulation in anyone who could be pregnant", "Assess for pulmonary embolism symptoms and hemodynamic instability at every step"], "criteria": [{"name": "Wells score for DVT", "summary": "A clinical probability tool that assigns points for active cancer, paralysis or recent cast immobilization, bed rest or major surgery within 4 weeks, tenderness along deep veins, entire-leg swelling, calf swelling 3 cm or more greater than the other leg, pitting edema, collateral superficial veins, prior DVT, and an alternative diagnosis at least as likely. The total stratifies patients into low, moderate, or high probability and guides D-dimer and ultrasound use.", "items": ["Active cancer", "Paralysis, paresis, or recent plaster immobilization of the lower extremity", "Bedridden for more than 3 days or major surgery within 12 weeks", "Localized tenderness along the distribution of the deep venous system", "Entire leg swollen", "Calf swelling 3 cm or more compared with the asymptomatic leg", "Pitting edema confined to the symptomatic leg", "Collateral superficial veins (nonvaricose)", "Previously documented DVT", "Alternative diagnosis at least as likely as DVT"]}], "scores": ["wells_dvt"]}, "differential": [{"name": "Pulmonary embolism", "slug": "pulmonary-embolism", "why_considered": "DVT and pulmonary embolism are the same disease process; a clot can break loose and travel to the lungs, and patients with DVT may already have a silent or symptomatic PE.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Any new dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, hypoxemia, or syncope in a patient with leg swelling", "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden shortness of breath", "Pleuritic chest pain worse with inspiration", "Coughing up blood", "Lightheadedness or syncope"], "exam": ["Tachycardia out of proportion to the leg findings", "Tachypnea", "Hypoxemia on pulse oximetry", "Signs of right heart strain such as a loud P2 or elevated JVP"], "labs": ["Elevated D-dimer", "Elevated troponin or BNP suggesting right ventricular strain", "Respiratory alkalosis on blood gas"], "imaging": ["Filling defect in the pulmonary arteries on CT pulmonary angiography", "Mismatched segmental perfusion defect on V/Q scan", "Right ventricular enlargement on echocardiography or CT"], "ecg": ["Sinus tachycardia", "S1Q3T3 pattern", "Right axis deviation or right bundle branch block"]}}, {"name": "Cellulitis", "slug": "cellulitis", "why_considered": "Unilateral leg redness, warmth, swelling, and pain are the classic shared presentation of cellulitis and DVT.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Fever, chills, a clear portal of entry such as a wound or interdigital fissure, or rapidly spreading erythema", "discriminators": {"history": ["Fever and chills", "Rapidly spreading redness", "Recent skin break, insect bite, or fungal foot infection", "Pain is more superficial and burning"], "exam": ["Well-demarcated, spreading erythema with a leading edge", "Warm, tender, indurated skin", "Lymphangitic streaking", "Regional lymphadenopathy"], "labs": ["Leukocytosis with left shift", "Elevated inflammatory markers", "Blood cultures may be positive"], "imaging": ["Ultrasound shows subcutaneous edema and no deep vein thrombus", "MRI may show skin and subcutaneous inflammation without venous filling defect"]}}, {"name": "Baker cyst rupture", "slug": "baker-cyst-rupture", "why_considered": "Rupture of a popliteal cyst causes acute calf pain, swelling, and a positive Homans sign, closely mimicking DVT.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden onset of calf pain, often after activity", "Known knee osteoarthritis or meniscal tear", "Pain and swelling behind the knee", "No DVT risk factors"], "exam": ["Tenderness and fullness in the popliteal fossa", "Knee effusion or joint line tenderness", "Crescent-shaped ecchymosis near the ankle or medial malleolus"], "labs": ["D-dimer may be mildly elevated but is nonspecific"], "imaging": ["Ultrasound shows a popliteal cyst with fluid dissection into the calf and a compressible deep venous system", "MRI demonstrates the cyst and surrounding fluid without a venous filling defect"]}}, {"name": "Gastrocnemius or soleus muscle tear", "slug": "gastrocnemius-tear", "why_considered": "A calf muscle strain or tear produces acute calf pain, swelling, and tenderness that can be mistaken for DVT.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden onset during exercise or a specific movement", "Audible pop or snap at the time of injury", "Pain is focal and worse with muscle contraction", "No immobilization or thrombotic risk factors"], "exam": ["Focal tenderness over the medial gastrocnemius", "Pain reproduced by resisted plantar flexion or passive stretching", "Ecchymosis may appear after 24-48 hours"], "imaging": ["Ultrasound shows muscle fiber disruption, hematoma, or fluid collection with normal compressible deep veins", "MRI demonstrates muscle edema or tear without venous thrombus"]}}, {"name": "Chronic venous insufficiency", "slug": "chronic-venous-insufficiency", "why_considered": "Chronic venous disease causes leg swelling, aching, and skin discoloration, and can be mistaken for an acute DVT, especially in older adults.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Longstanding, slowly progressive leg heaviness and swelling", "Symptoms worse at the end of the day and with prolonged standing", "History of varicose veins or prior leg surgery", "No acute onset or thrombotic risk factors"], "exam": ["Bilateral or symmetric lower extremity edema", "Brown hemosiderin staining, lipodermatosclerosis, or stasis ulcers near the ankle", "Varicose veins", "No acute warmth or focal deep vein tenderness"], "imaging": ["Venous duplex shows venous reflux and a compressible deep venous system without acute thrombus"]}}, {"name": "Lymphedema", "slug": "lymphedema", "why_considered": "Lymphedema causes unilateral or asymmetric leg swelling that can be confused with DVT, particularly after cancer treatment or surgery.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Gradual, progressive swelling over weeks to months", "History of lymph node dissection, radiation, or filariasis", "Swelling is firm and nonpitting", "No acute pain or warmth"], "exam": ["Nonpitting, firm edema", "Positive Stemmer sign (inability to pinch the dorsal foot skin)", "Thickened skin with a peau d'orange appearance", "No erythema or deep vein tenderness"], "imaging": ["Venous duplex shows normal compressible deep veins", "Lymphoscintigraphy may show impaired lymphatic drainage"]}}, {"name": "Superficial thrombophlebitis", "slug": "superficial-thrombophlebitis", "why_considered": "Inflammation and thrombosis of a superficial vein cause a tender, red, warm cord that can be mistaken for DVT.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Tenderness and redness along a superficial vein", "Recent IV catheter or infusion", "Varicose veins", "Pain is superficial and follows a linear track"], "exam": ["Palpable, tender, cord-like superficial vein", "Erythema in a linear distribution", "No significant calf or thigh swelling"], "imaging": ["Ultrasound shows thrombus within a superficial vein with a normal deep venous system"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Distal (calf) DVT with minimal swelling, mild discomfort, no functional limitation, and no evidence of pulmonary embolism", "moderate": "Proximal (popliteal, femoral, or iliac) DVT with marked unilateral swelling, pain limiting ambulation, and no hemodynamic or respiratory compromise", "severe": "Extensive iliofemoral DVT with severe pain, marked swelling, risk of venous gangrene, or concurrent submassive pulmonary embolism with right ventricular strain but stable blood pressure", "critical": "Massive iliofemoral DVT with phlegmasia cerulea dolens or venous gangrene, or concurrent massive pulmonary embolism with hypotension, shock, or cardiac arrest"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Sudden shortness of breath, pleuritic chest pain, or hemoptysis", "meaning": "Suggests pulmonary embolism; needs urgent evaluation and anticoagulation"}, {"finding": "Hypotension, tachycardia, or syncope", "meaning": "Suggests massive pulmonary embolism or hemodynamic compromise; emergency intervention required"}, {"finding": "Hypoxemia on pulse oximetry", "meaning": "Suggests pulmonary embolism or right heart strain"}, {"finding": "Phlegmasia cerulea dolens (painful, blue, tense leg with absent pulses)", "meaning": "Impending venous gangrene; requires urgent vascular intervention"}, {"finding": "Rapidly progressive swelling with compartment syndrome signs", "meaning": "Severe venous hypertension and possible tissue necrosis"}, {"finding": "Fever with chills and spreading erythema", "meaning": "Suggests cellulitis or septic thrombophlebitis rather than uncomplicated DVT"}], "complications": [{"name": "Pulmonary embolism", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Sudden dyspnea", "Pleuritic chest pain", "Tachycardia", "Hypoxemia", "Hemoptysis"]}, {"name": "Post-thrombotic syndrome", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Chronic leg heaviness and swelling", "Hyperpigmentation and lipodermatosclerosis", "Venous ulcers near the ankle"]}, {"name": "Recurrent venous thromboembolism", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["New leg swelling or chest symptoms after stopping anticoagulation", "Unprovoked or cancer-associated DVT", "Inherited thrombophilia"]}, {"name": "Phlegmasia cerulea dolens and venous gangrene", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Massive iliofemoral thrombosis", "Painful, blue, tense leg", "Absent distal pulses", "Compartment syndrome"]}, {"name": "Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Platelet count drop by more than 50% after starting heparin", "New thrombosis despite anticoagulation", "Skin necrosis at heparin injection sites"]}, {"name": "Major bleeding from anticoagulation", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Melena or hematochezia", "Hematuria", "Large hematoma", "Falling hemoglobin or hemodynamic instability"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "DVT is more common in pregnancy and the postpartum period, especially in the left leg due to compression of the left iliac vein. Swelling and discomfort are common in normal pregnancy, so a high index of suspicion is needed. Low-molecular-weight heparin is the preferred anticoagulant; warfarin and direct oral anticoagulants are avoided. Compression ultrasound is first-line imaging."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "May present with vague leg discomfort or worsening edema attributed to chronic venous insufficiency or heart failure. Higher risk of bleeding on anticoagulation and more likely to have comorbid cancer or immobility."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Bilateral leg swelling and chronic skin changes are common, making asymmetry harder to detect. Weight-based dosing of low-molecular-weight heparin is challenging, and direct oral anticoagulants may be preferred."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "DVT is uncommon and usually associated with central venous catheters, malignancy, or inherited thrombophilia. Presentation may be pain and swelling around a line or an extremity. Anticoagulation is specialist-directed."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Low-molecular-weight heparin and direct oral anticoagulants require dose adjustment or avoidance depending on the degree of renal impairment. Unfractionated heparin may be preferred when renal function is severely reduced."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Higher risk of infection and septic thrombophlebitis; fever and systemic signs should prompt evaluation for an infectious source in addition to DVT."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "When did the leg swelling and pain start, and how has it changed since then?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Gradual onset over hours to a few days, building then plateauing; acute onset during exertion suggests a muscle tear"}, {"question": "Is the swelling in one leg or both?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Unilateral; bilateral swelling points to heart failure, venous insufficiency, or lymphedema"}, {"question": "Where exactly is the swelling and pain — calf, thigh, or the whole leg?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Calf or thigh; entire-leg or thigh involvement suggests proximal (iliofemoral) thrombosis"}, {"question": "What makes it better or worse?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Worse with standing, walking, or dependency and better with elevation and rest"}, {"question": "Have you had any chest pain, shortness of breath, or coughed up blood?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Any of these suggests concurrent pulmonary embolism and changes urgency"}, {"question": "Have you had surgery, been hospitalized, or been on bed rest in the last 3 months?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Major surgery (especially hip or knee replacement) or immobility within 12 weeks is a strong risk factor"}, {"question": "Have you had a blood clot in the leg or lung before?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Prior venous thromboembolism markedly raises the probability of recurrence"}, {"question": "Do you have cancer, or are you being treated for cancer?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Active malignancy or chemotherapy is a strong provoking factor"}, {"question": "Have you been on a long trip or sitting still for many hours recently?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Long-haul travel or prolonged sitting with the legs dependent"}, {"question": "Do you take birth control pills, estrogen, tamoxifen, or raloxifene?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Hormonal or selective estrogen receptor modulator therapy raises clot risk"}, {"question": "Could you be pregnant? When was your last menstrual period?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Pregnancy or the postpartum period increases risk and changes imaging and treatment choices"}, {"question": "Has anyone in your family had a blood clot, especially before age 50?", "category": "family", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Family history of venous thromboembolism or a known inherited thrombophilia"}, {"question": "Have you had recent leg trauma, a muscle injury, or a cast or brace on the leg?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Trauma or immobilization can cause a muscle tear or provoke a clot"}, {"question": "Do you have any wounds, ulcers, or fungal infection between the toes?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "A portal of entry suggests cellulitis rather than DVT"}, {"question": "Any fever or chills?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "High fever with chills favors cellulitis or septic thrombophlebitis"}, {"question": "Do you smoke?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Smoking is a weak additional risk factor"}], "accepted_names": ["Deep vein thrombosis", "Deep venous thrombosis", "DVT", "Lower extremity deep vein thrombosis", "Venous thrombosis"], "reasoning_points": ["Unilateral leg swelling with calf tenderness, warmth, and a measurable calf circumference difference", "Presence of provoking risk factors such as recent surgery, immobility, active cancer, prior VTE, pregnancy, or estrogen therapy", "Clinical probability assessed with a validated score (Wells or Geneva) before testing", "D-dimer used only to rule out DVT in low-probability patients; a negative highly sensitive or age-adjusted result excludes it", "Compression ultrasound is the first-line confirmatory test, showing a noncompressible deep vein or intraluminal thrombus", "Pregnancy excluded before imaging and anticoagulation in anyone who could be pregnant", "Pulmonary embolism considered and assessed at every step, since DVT and PE are the same disease process", "Alternative diagnoses such as cellulitis, Baker cyst rupture, muscle tear, chronic venous insufficiency, lymphedema, and superficial thrombophlebitis weighed and addressed"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Classic unilateral calf swelling and tenderness after recent surgery or prolonged immobility", "Clear calf circumference difference of 3 cm or more with warmth and erythema", "No chest symptoms and normal vital signs", "Compression ultrasound confirms a noncompressible popliteal or femoral vein"], "medium": ["Woman of reproductive age on combined oral contraceptives with unilateral leg swelling", "Older adult whose swelling is attributed to chronic venous insufficiency or heart failure", "Mildly elevated D-dimer in a low-probability patient requiring imaging to sort out", "Upper extremity DVT associated with a central venous catheter", "Concurrent cellulitis concern with fever and leg redness"], "hard": ["Pregnant patient with leg swelling that overlaps normal pregnancy changes", "Obesity with bilateral chronic edema masking asymmetry", "Baker cyst rupture or gastrocnemius tear mimicking DVT with a positive Homans sign", "Patient with concurrent pulmonary embolism presenting with dyspnea or syncope", "Phlegmasia cerulea dolens with a tense, blue, painful leg and absent pulses", "Renal impairment complicating anticoagulant selection and dosing"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
