s: no profile yet", "differential alcohol-withdrawal: no profile yet", "differential hepatic-encephalopathy: no profile yet", "differential hypoglycemia: no profile yet"]}, "review": {"status": "pending"}}, "patient": {"age": {"typical_min": 65, "typical_max": 95, "peak": "70–90", "possible_min": 0, "possible_max": 110}, "sex": "either", "demographics": ["Most common in hospitalized older adults, especially those with pre-existing cognitive impairment", "Occurs at any age in the setting of severe illness, surgery, or drug or alcohol withdrawal", "More common in nursing home residents and patients in intensive care"], "risk_factors": [{"text": "Older age", "strength": "strong"}, {"text": "Pre-existing dementia or mild cognitive impairment", "strength": "strong"}, {"text": "Hospitalization, especially ICU admission or recent surgery", "strength": "strong"}, {"text": "Infection such as urinary tract infection or pneumonia", "strength": "strong"}, {"text": "Use of psychoactive medications (sedatives, opioids, anticholinergics)", "strength": "strong"}, {"text": "Alcohol or drug intoxication or withdrawal", "strength": "moderate"}, {"text": "Metabolic derangement, dehydration, or electrolyte imbalance", "strength": "moderate"}, {"text": "Severe pain, sleep deprivation, or use of physical restraints", "strength": "moderate"}, {"text": "Sensory impairment (hearing or vision loss)", "strength": "weak"}, {"text": "Multiple chronic illnesses or organ failure", "strength": "moderate"}], "comorbidities": ["Dementia or other neurodegenerative disease", "Chronic kidney disease or liver failure", "Heart failure", "Diabetes mellitus", "Cancer, especially advanced disease", "Prior stroke or other structural brain lesion", "Alcohol use disorder", "Depression"], "family_history": [], "social_history": ["Lives in a nursing home or assisted living facility", "Recent hospitalization or surgery", "Heavy alcohol use", "Recent change in living situation or loss of a spouse"], "exposures": ["Recent surgery or anesthesia", "New or increased psychoactive medication", "Recent infection in the community or hospital", "Physical restraints or bladder catheterization"], "risk_medications": ["Diphenhydramine", "Oxycodone", "Morphine", "Lorazepam", "Diazepam", "Haloperidol", "Prednisone", "Amitriptyline", "Furosemide", "Cimetidine"]}, "presentation": {"chief_complaints": [{"text": "I don't know where I am or what day it is", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "My family says I've been confused and not making sense since yesterday", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "I keep seeing things that aren't there", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "I can't stay awake or focus on what people are saying", "frequency": "common"}], "hpi": {"onset": "Sudden, over hours to a few days", "duration": "Hours to days at presentation; may fluctuate and persist for weeks if the cause is not treated", "progression": "Symptoms come and go during the day, often worse at night; level of alertness and orientation change from hour to hour", "location": "", "radiation": "", "quality": "Fluctuating confusion with difficulty paying attention and organizing thoughts", "severity": "Moderate to severe; interferes with conversation, self-care and safety", "timing": "Worse in the evening and at night; may be more alert and oriented in the morning", "aggravating": ["Unfamiliar surroundings", "Loud or busy environment", "Being moved or restrained", "Sleep deprivation"], "relieving": ["Familiar people and surroundings", "Quiet, well-lit room", "Glasses and hearing aids", "Treating the underlying cause"], "associated_symptoms": [{"text": "Trouble concentrating or following a conversation", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "Disorientation to time, place or person", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "Short-term memory problems", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Disrupted sleep-wake cycle with daytime sleepiness and nighttime wakefulness", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Agitation, restlessness or irritability", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Withdrawal or reduced responsiveness", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Visual hallucinations", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Delusions or suspiciousness", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Emotional lability, fear or anger", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Tremor, sweating or rapid heartbeat in withdrawal states", "frequency": "rare"}], "pertinent_negatives": ["Gradual memory decline over months to years before this episode", "Focal weakness, numbness or difficulty speaking", "New severe headache or head trauma", "Loss of consciousness or seizure before the confusion started", "Fever with stiff neck or rash", "Intentional overdose or recent suicide attempt"]}, "ros": [{"system": "constitutional", "positives": [{"text": "Feeling tired or sleepy during the day", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Poor appetite", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Fever or chills if infection is the cause", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Weight loss", "Night sweats"]}, {"system": "neurologic", "positives": [{"text": "Confusion and disorientation", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "Difficulty paying attention", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "Memory problems", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Visual hallucinations", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Tremor or unsteady movements", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Focal weakness", "Numbness", "Slurred speech", "New severe headache", "Seizure"], "argues_against": ["Focal neurologic deficit suggesting stroke", "Head trauma with loss of consciousness"]}, {"system": "psychiatric", "positives": [{"text": "Agitation or restlessness", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Withdrawal or reduced interaction", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Suspiciousness or delusions", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Emotional swings", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Long-standing depressed mood before this episode", "Prior manic episodes", "Chronic psychotic illness"], "argues_against": ["Primary psychiatric disorder with stable course", "Depression as the sole explanation"]}, {"system": "gastrointestinal", "positives": [{"text": "Nausea or vomiting", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Constipation", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Blood in stool", "Jaundice"]}, {"system": "genitourinary", "positives": [{"text": "Burning with urination or frequent urination", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Decreased urine output", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Flank pain", "Blood in urine"]}, {"system": "cardiovascular", "positives": [{"text": "Rapid heartbeat", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Low blood pressure or dizziness on standing", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Chest pain", "Leg swelling"]}, {"system": "respiratory", "positives": [{"text": "Cough or shortness of breath if pneumonia is the cause", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Coughing up blood", "Sudden severe shortness of breath"]}], "atypical": [{"population": "older_adults", "how_it_differs": "Hypoactive delirium is most common in this group and is often mistaken for depression, fatigue or dementia; patients may be quiet, withdrawn and sleepy rather than agitated.", "missing_classic_features": ["Agitation", "Hallucinations", "Obvious confusion reported by the patient"]}, {"population": "children", "how_it_differs": "Children may present with irritability, inconsolable crying, refusal to eat, or regression of behavior rather than clear disorientation; they may not report hallucinations.", "missing_classic_features": ["Disorientation to time and place", "Clear description of confusion"]}, {"population": "infants", "how_it_differs": "Infants show poor feeding, lethargy or inconsolable fussiness; the diagnosis is usually made when an underlying illness or medication effect is identified.", "missing_classic_features": ["Verbal reports of confusion", "Fluctuating attention noted by the patient"]}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "how_it_differs": "Infection may be the trigger with minimal or no fever, so delirium can be the only sign of a serious infection.", "missing_classic_features": ["Fever", "Localizing infectious symptoms"]}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "how_it_differs": "Accumulation of medications and metabolic waste can cause or worsen delirium; symptoms may persist until dialysis or dose adjustment.", "missing_classic_features": ["Rapid improvement after treating a single obvious cause"]}, {"population": "hepatic_impairment", "how_it_differs": "Hepatic encephalopathy can present with fluctuating confusion, sleep inversion and asterixis; it may be mistaken for primary delirium from another cause.", "missing_classic_features": ["Clear non-hepatic trigger"]}, {"population": "pregnancy", "how_it_differs": "Delirium in pregnancy is uncommon and usually signals a severe underlying condition such as eclampsia, sepsis, or a medication reaction; presentation may be dominated by obstetric symptoms.", "missing_classic_features": ["Typical geriatric risk profile"]}]}, "exam": {"general": {"mild": "Awake but easily distracted; may appear tired or mildly restless; conversation drifts off topic", "moderate": "Clearly inattentive and disoriented; either drowsy and withdrawn or restless and picking at lines or clothing", "severe": "Markedly confused; may be agitated and attempting to climb out of bed, or somnolent and only briefly rousable", "critical": "Fluctuating between agitation and unresponsiveness; may be combative or barely arousable; autonomic overactivity if withdrawal or severe systemic illness"}, "findings": [{"id": "inattention", "region": "mental_status", "maneuver": "Ask the patient to recite the months backward or spell 'world' backward", "finding": "Cannot sustain attention; makes errors or loses track of the task", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "disorientation", "region": "mental_status", "maneuver": "Ask the patient their name, where they are, and the date or day of the week", "finding": "Disoriented to time, place, or person", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "fluctuating-course", "region": "mental_status", "maneuver": "Observe and re-examine the patient over minutes to hours, or compare with nursing reports from earlier in the day", "finding": "Level of alertness and orientation changes during the day, often worse at night", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "altered-consciousness", "region": "mental_status", "maneuver": "Observe the patient's level of arousal and response to voice or touch", "finding": "Reduced or fluctuating level of consciousness (drowsy, hypervigilant, or difficult to arouse)", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "disorganized-speech", "region": "mental_status", "maneuver": "Listen to the patient's spontaneous speech and ask open-ended questions", "finding": "Rambling, tangential, or incoherent speech", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "memory-impairment", "region": "mental_status", "maneuver": "Give three words and ask the patient to repeat them after a few minutes", "finding": "Impaired short-term recall", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "hallucinations", "region": "mental_status", "maneuver": "Ask whether the patient sees or hears things others do not", "finding": "Visual or auditory hallucinations, often frightening", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "delusions", "region": "mental_status", "maneuver": "Ask about suspicious or fixed false beliefs", "finding": "Paranoid or persecutory delusions", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "supports", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "agitation", "region": "mental_status", "maneuver": "Observe behavior during the interview", "finding": "Restlessness, pulling at lines or clothing, attempting to get out of bed", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "hypoactivity", "region": "mental_status", "maneuver": "Observe the patient's spontaneous activity and interaction", "finding": "Withdrawn, sleepy, or slow to respond; easily mistaken for depression or fatigue", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "asterixis", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Ask the patient to extend both arms with wrists dorsiflexed and observe for a few seconds", "finding": "Brief, irregular flapping of the hands (suggests hepatic encephalopathy or metabolic cause)", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "tremor", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Observe the outstretched hands and ask the patient to hold a steady posture", "finding": "Postural or intention tremor (suggests alcohol or sedative withdrawal, or medication effect)", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-focal-deficit", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Test strength, sensation, and cranial nerves, and ask the patient to smile and raise both arms", "finding": "No focal weakness, sensory loss, or facial droop", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "no-meningismus", "region": "neck", "maneuver": "Flex the neck and check for resistance or pain", "finding": "No neck stiffness or meningismus", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-head-trauma", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the scalp and ask about recent falls or head injury", "finding": "No scalp laceration, hematoma, or signs of trauma", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-rash", "region": "skin", "maneuver": "Inspect the skin, including the back and pressure areas", "finding": "No petechial or purpuric rash", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "no-fever-source", "region": "lung", "maneuver": "Auscultate the lungs and inspect for a source of infection", "finding": "No focal crackles or consolidation to suggest pneumonia", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}]}, "vitals": {"temp": {"mild": {"min": 98.0, "max": 99.5}, "moderate": {"min": 99.0, "max": 101.0}, "severe": {"min": 100.4, "max": 103.0}, "critical": {"min": 101.0, "max": 104.0}}, "hr": {"mild": {"min": 70, "max": 100}, "moderate": {"min": 90, "max": 115}, "severe": {"min": 100, "max": 130}, "critical": {"min": 110, "max": 150}}, "rr": {"moderate": {"min": 16, "max": 22}, "severe": {"min": 18, "max": 26}, "critical": {"min": 22, "max": 32}}, "sbp": {"moderate": {"min": 100, "max": 140}, "severe": {"min": 90, "max": 130}, "critical": {"min": 80, "max": 110}}, "dbp": {"moderate": {"min": 60, "max": 85}, "severe": {"min": 55, "max": 80}, "critical": {"min": 45, "max": 70}}, "spo2": {"severe": {"min": 90, "max": 96}, "critical": {"min": 85, "max": 93}}}, "labs": [{"test_id": "na", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 130, "max": 134}, "moderate": {"min": 125, "max": 130}, "severe": {"min": 118, "max": 125}, "critical": {"min": 110, "max": 118}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "essential", "caveat": "Hyponatremia is a common reversible cause; correct slowly to avoid osmotic demyelination."}, {"test_id": "na", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 146, "max": 150}, "moderate": {"min": 150, "max": 155}, "severe": {"min": 155, "max": 165}, "critical": {"min": 160, "max": 175}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Hypernatremia from dehydration or poor access to water, especially in nursing home residents."}, {"test_id": "ca", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 10.3, "max": 11.0}, "moderate": {"min": 11.0, "max": 12.5}, "severe": {"min": 12.5, "max": 14.0}, "critical": {"min": 13.5, "max": 16.0}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Hypercalcemia causes confusion and lethargy; check ionized calcium if albumin is low."}, {"test_id": "glucose", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 55, "max": 69}, "moderate": {"min": 40, "max": 55}, "severe": {"min": 25, "max": 40}, "critical": {"min": 15, "max": 30}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "essential", "caveat": "Hypoglycemia is a rapidly reversible cause; check a bedside glucose immediately in any confused patient."}, {"test_id": "glucose", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 180, "max": 250}, "moderate": {"min": 250, "max": 400}, "severe": {"min": 400, "max": 600}, "critical": {"min": 500, "max": 900}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Severe hyperglycemia suggests diabetic ketoacidosis or hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state."}, {"test_id": "bun", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 25, "max": 45}, "severe": {"min": 40, "max": 80}, "critical": {"min": 60, "max": 120}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Uremia from renal failure or dehydration contributes to encephalopathy."}, {"test_id": "cr", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 1.4, "max": 2.5}, "severe": {"min": 2.5, "max": 5.0}, "critical": {"min": 4.0, "max": 10.0}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Also guides safe dosing of sedatives and antipsychotics."}, {"test_id": "ammonia", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 60, "max": 120}, "severe": {"min": 100, "max": 200}, "critical": {"min": 150, "max": 300}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Elevated in hepatic encephalopathy; a normal level does not exclude it."}, {"test_id": "tsh", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 4.5, "max": 10.0}, "moderate": {"min": 10.0, "max": 30.0}, "severe": {"min": 20.0, "max": 60.0}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Severe hypothyroidism can present with confusion and hypothermia; hyperthyroidism can mimic agitated delirium."}, {"test_id": "wbc", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 11.5, "max": 15.0}, "moderate": {"min": 15.0, "max": 20.0}, "severe": {"min": 18.0, "max": 28.0}, "critical": {"min": 22.0, "max": 35.0}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Leukocytosis supports infection as the trigger; a normal count does not exclude it, especially in older adults."}, {"test_id": "ua_le", "qualitative": {"mild": "Trace", "moderate": "1+", "severe": "2+"}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Urinary tract infection is a frequent precipitant; asymptomatic bacteriuria is common in older adults and may not be the true cause."}, {"test_id": "ua_nitrite", "qualitative": {"moderate": "Positive"}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important"}, {"test_id": "ua_wbc", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 6, "max": 15}, "moderate": {"min": 15, "max": 40}, "severe": {"min": 30, "max": 100}}, "frequency": "common", "importance": "important"}, {"test_id": "lactate", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 2.2, "max": 4.0}, "severe": {"min": 4.0, "max": 8.0}, "critical": {"min": 6.0, "max": 12.0}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Elevation suggests sepsis, hypoperfusion, or alcohol-related thiamine deficiency."}, {"test_id": "csf_wbc", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 10, "max": 100}, "severe": {"min": 100, "max": 1000}, "critical": {"min": 500, "max": 5000}}, "frequency": "rare", "importance": "essential", "caveat": "Only when meningitis or encephalitis is suspected; a normal CSF cell count does not exclude early infection."}, {"test_id": "csf_protein", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 50, "max": 100}, "severe": {"min": 100, "max": 200}, "critical": {"min": 150, "max": 400}}, "frequency": "rare", "importance": "important"}, {"test_id": "csf_glucose", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 30, "max": 40}, "severe": {"min": 20, "max": 35}, "critical": {"min": 10, "max": 25}}, "frequency": "rare", "importance": "important", "caveat": "Low CSF glucose relative to serum suggests bacterial meningitis."}], "ecg": {"findings": [{"feature": "rate", "finding": "Sinus tachycardia from infection, dehydration, pain, or alcohol or sedative withdrawal", "frequency": "common", "min_severity": "moderate"}, {"feature": "rhythm", "finding": "Atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response, a common precipitant of delirium in older adults", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate"}, {"feature": "qt", "finding": "Prolonged QTc from electrolyte derangement or QT-prolonging medications such as haloperidol", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate"}, {"feature": "st", "finding": "ST depression or elevation suggesting myocardial ischemia or infarction as the trigger", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "severe"}, {"feature": "other", "finding": "Peaked T waves with widened QRS in hyperkalemia, or flattened T waves and U waves in hypokalemia", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate"}]}, "imaging": [{"study": "CT head without IV contrast", "modality": "ct", "preferred_when": "New focal neurologic deficit, head trauma, anticoagulation, or no clear cause identified", "findings": [{"text": "No acute intracranial abnormality", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Acute or chronic subdural hematoma", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Acute ischemic or hemorrhagic stroke", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Cerebral atrophy consistent with underlying dementia", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Old lacunar or cortical infarcts", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "MRI brain with and without IV contrast", "modality": "mri", "preferred_when": "Suspected encephalitis, abscess, or when CT is nondiagnostic", "findings": [{"text": "Temporal lobe signal abnormality suggesting herpes simplex encephalitis", "frequency": "rare"}, {"text": "Ring-enhancing lesion suggesting abscess or tumor", "frequency": "rare"}, {"text": "Diffuse white matter changes consistent with small vessel disease", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "Chest x-ray", "modality": "xray", "preferred_when": "Fever, cough, hypoxia, or suspected pneumonia", "findings": [{"text": "Lobar consolidation or infiltrate suggesting pneumonia", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Clear lungs", "frequency": "common"}]}, {"study": "CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast", "modality": "ct", "preferred_when": "Abdominal pain, fever without a source, or suspected intra-abdominal infection", "findings": [{"text": "No acute intra-abdominal process", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Bowel obstruction, perforation, or abscess", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "Electroencephalogram (EEG)", "modality": "other", "preferred_when": "Suspected nonconvulsive status epilepticus or unexplained persistent unresponsiveness", "findings": [{"text": "Diffuse slowing consistent with metabolic encephalopathy", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Triphasic waves suggesting hepatic or metabolic encephalopathy", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Epileptiform discharges or nonconvulsive status epilepticus", "frequency": "rare"}]}], "workup": {"initial": [{"test": "bmp", "why": "Detects hyponatremia, hypercalcemia, uremia, and acid-base disturbance, all common reversible causes"}, {"test": "cbc", "why": "Looks for infection or anemia as a trigger"}, {"test": "glucose", "why": "Bedside glucose is mandatory; hypoglycemia is immediately reversible"}, {"test": "ua", "why": "Urinary tract infection is a frequent precipitant in older adults"}, {"test": "tsh", "why": "Thyroid dysfunction can present with confusion"}, {"test": "b12", "why": "Deficiency is a reversible cause of cognitive impairment"}, {"test": "ECG", "why": "Detects arrhythmia, ischemia, or QT prolongation relevant to treatment"}, {"test": "Chest x-ray", "why": "Looks for pneumonia", "only_if": "fever, cough, hypoxia, or suspected pneumonia"}, {"test": "CT head without IV contrast", "why": "Rules out structural cause", "only_if": "new focal deficit, head trauma, anticoagulation, or no cause found"}, {"test": "lumbar puncture", "why": "Evaluates for meningitis or encephalitis", "only_if": "fever with headache, neck stiffness, or rash, or unexplained delirium with no other source"}], "confirmatory": [{"test": "Confusion Assessment Method (CAM)", "why": "Bedside structured assessment confirming acute onset, fluctuating course, inattention, and disorganized thinking or altered consciousness"}], "supporting": [{"test": "ammonia", "why": "Supports hepatic encephalopathy when liver disease is suspected"}, {"test": "lactate", "why": "Suggests sepsis or hypoperfusion"}, {"test": "crp", "why": "Adds evidence of infection or inflammation"}, {"test": "procalcitonin", "why": "Supports bacterial infection when the source is unclear"}, {"test": "mg", "why": "Hypomagnesemia can cause confusion and arrhythmia"}, {"test": "phos", "why": "Low phosphate contributes to weakness and confusion"}, {"test": "ica", "why": "Ionized calcium when albumin is low or calcium is borderline"}, {"test": "cortisol_am", "why": "Adrenal insufficiency can present with confusion and hypotension"}, {"test": "hcg", "why": "Pregnancy alters the differential and management", "only_if": "patient is a woman of childbearing age"}, {"test": "MRI brain with and without IV contrast", "why": "Evaluates for encephalitis or abscess", "only_if": "suspected encephalitis, abscess, or nondiagnostic CT"}, {"test": "Electroencephalogram (EEG)", "why": "Detects nonconvulsive status epilepticus", "only_if": "persistent unexplained unresponsiveness or suspected seizures"}, {"test": "CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast", "why": "Looks for intra-abdominal infection", "only_if": "abdominal pain or fever without a source"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "CT head without IV contrast", "why": "Not routine when the cause is clearly metabolic or infectious and there is no focal deficit or trauma"}, {"test": "MRI brain with and without IV contrast", "why": "Not first-line; reserved for suspected encephalitis or abscess"}, {"test": "CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast", "why": "Not routine without abdominal findings or fever of unknown source"}, {"test": "lumbar puncture", "why": "Not routine without signs of CNS infection"}, {"test": "Electroencephalogram (EEG)", "why": "Not routine unless seizures or nonconvulsive status are suspected"}, {"test": "Chest x-ray", "why": "Not routine without respiratory symptoms or fever"}, {"test": "hcg", "why": "Not needed in men or women who cannot be pregnant"}], "decision_points": ["Check bedside glucose immediately in any patient with acute confusion", "Review the full medication list and stop or reduce deliriogenic drugs", "Look for and treat infection, dehydration, and electrolyte disturbance before attributing symptoms to dementia or a psychiatric disorder", "Image the brain when there is focal deficit, trauma, anticoagulation, or no identified cause", "Perform lumbar puncture when CNS infection is suspected, after imaging if there are focal signs or immunocompromise", "Use the CAM or similar structured tool to confirm the diagnosis at the bedside"], "criteria": [{"name": "Confusion Assessment Method (CAM)", "summary": "Bedside tool that identifies delirium when there is an acute change with fluctuating course plus inattention, and either disorganized thinking or an altered level of consciousness.", "items": ["Acute onset with fluctuating course", "Inattention", "Disorganized thinking", "Altered level of consciousness"]}], "scores": ["cam_icu", "drs_r98", "mdas"]}, "differential": [{"name": "Dementia", "slug": "dementia", "why_considered": "Both cause confusion and disorientation in older adults, and the two frequently coexist; dementia is the strongest risk factor for delirium.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Gradual decline in memory and function over months to years", "Stable, slowly progressive course rather than hour-to-hour fluctuation", "Attention relatively preserved until late stages", "No acute medical trigger"], "exam": ["Alert and attentive at baseline; can engage in conversation", "Memory loss predominates over inattention", "No fluctuating level of consciousness"], "labs": ["Workup for reversible causes is unrevealing", "B12, TSH and metabolic panel normal or showing chronic stable abnormalities"], "imaging": ["CT or MRI shows cerebral atrophy or chronic vascular changes without an acute lesion"]}}, {"name": "Depression", "slug": "depression", "why_considered": "Hypoactive delirium is easily mistaken for depression because both cause withdrawal, reduced interaction, poor appetite and slowed responses.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Weeks to months of low mood, anhedonia and hopelessness", "No acute onset or fluctuation during the day", "Intact attention and orientation", "No acute medical illness or new medication"], "exam": ["Alert, oriented and attentive", "Psychomotor slowing without disorientation", "No fluctuating consciousness"], "labs": ["Metabolic, infectious and toxic workup unrevealing"]}}, {"name": "Psychosis (primary psychiatric disorder)", "slug": "psychosis", "why_considered": "Hyperactive delirium with hallucinations, delusions and agitation can resemble a primary psychotic disorder such as schizophrenia.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Long-standing or recurrent psychotic symptoms before this episode", "Clear sensorium and orientation between episodes", "No acute medical trigger", "Auditory hallucinations more typical than visual"], "exam": ["Alert, oriented and attentive", "No fluctuating level of consciousness", "No autonomic instability or tremor"], "labs": ["Toxicology and metabolic workup negative"]}}, {"name": "Nonconvulsive status epilepticus", "slug": "nonconvulsive-status-epilepticus", "why_considered": "Can present with fluctuating confusion, altered awareness and subtle motor signs, mimicking delirium.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Persistent unexplained altered mental status that does not improve after correcting obvious causes", "discriminators": {"history": ["Known epilepsy or prior seizures", "Prolonged unresponsiveness or staring spells", "Subtle twitching of face or limbs"], "exam": ["Fluctuating or persistently reduced consciousness", "Subtle myoclonic jerks or eye deviation"], "labs": ["No metabolic cause identified"], "imaging": ["EEG shows continuous or repetitive epileptiform discharges"]}}, {"name": "Meningitis or encephalitis", "slug": "meningitis", "why_considered": "CNS infection can present with acute confusion, fever and altered consciousness, and is a life-threatening cause of delirium.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Fever with headache, neck stiffness, rash, or delirium without another source", "discriminators": {"history": ["Fever, headache, photophobia", "Neck stiffness", "Recent viral illness or rash"], "exam": ["Meningismus, positive Kernig or Brudzinski sign", "Focal neurologic deficits or seizures", "Petechial or purpuric rash"], "labs": ["CSF pleocytosis, elevated protein, low glucose", "Positive CSF culture or PCR"], "imaging": ["MRI temporal lobe signal abnormality in herpes simplex encephalitis", "CT may be normal or show complications"]}}, {"name": "Alcohol or sedative withdrawal (delirium tremens)", "slug": "alcohol-withdrawal", "why_considered": "Withdrawal causes agitation, confusion, hallucinations and autonomic overactivity, closely resembling hyperactive delirium.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Recent cessation or reduction of heavy alcohol or sedative use with tremor, sweating and tachycardia", "discriminators": {"history": ["Heavy chronic alcohol or benzodiazepine use", "Last drink or dose 1–5 days ago", "Prior withdrawal seizures"], "exam": ["Tremor, diaphoresis, tachycardia, hypertension", "Agitation, visual hallucinations", "Autonomic overactivity"], "labs": ["Elevated AST/ALT, macrocytosis, low platelets", "Elevated lactate, electrolyte derangements"]}}, {"name": "Hepatic encephalopathy", "slug": "hepatic-encephalopathy", "why_considered": "Fluctuating confusion, sleep inversion and asterixis can mimic delirium and often coexist with it.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Known cirrhosis or liver disease", "Precipitant such as GI bleed, infection, constipation or dehydration", "Sleep-wake inversion"], "exam": ["Asterixis", "Jaundice, ascites, spider angiomata", "Fetor hepaticus"], "labs": ["Elevated ammonia", "Thrombocytopenia, elevated bilirubin, prolonged INR"]}}, {"name": "Hypoglycemia", "slug": "hypoglycemia", "why_considered": "Low blood glucose causes acute confusion, agitation and altered consciousness that resolves rapidly with treatment.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Any patient with acute confusion; check bedside glucose immediately", "discriminators": {"history": ["Diabetes treated with insulin or sulfonylureas", "Missed meal, increased activity, or recent dose change", "Diaphoresis, palpitations, hunger"], "exam": ["Tremor, sweating, tachycardia", "Confusion or unresponsiveness"], "labs": ["Low bedside glucose that corrects with dextrose"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Early or subtle: mild inattention and disorientation that fluctuate; patient can still converse and follow simple commands; often recognized only by family or nursing staff.", "moderate": "Established: clear inattention, disorientation and either agitation or withdrawal; interferes with self-care and safety; requires close supervision.", "severe": "Marked confusion with agitation or somnolence; may attempt to climb out of bed or be difficult to arouse; autonomic instability if withdrawal or severe systemic illness; high risk of falls and injury.", "critical": "Life-threatening: fluctuating between combativeness and unresponsiveness; autonomic overactivity with fever, tachycardia and hypertension in severe withdrawal; or delirium as the presenting sign of sepsis, hypoxia or intracranial catastrophe."}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Fever with neck stiffness, headache or rash", "meaning": "Possible meningitis or encephalitis; needs urgent lumbar puncture and empiric treatment"}, {"finding": "New focal neurologic deficit, seizure or head trauma", "meaning": "Structural brain lesion such as stroke, hemorrhage or subdural hematoma; needs urgent imaging"}, {"finding": "Hypotension, tachycardia, tachypnea or elevated lactate", "meaning": "Sepsis or hypoperfusion; delirium may be the only early sign in older adults"}, {"finding": "Low oxygen saturation or respiratory distress", "meaning": "Hypoxia or pneumonia as the trigger; needs immediate oxygen and workup"}, {"finding": "Severe agitation with autonomic overactivity after alcohol or sedative cessation", "meaning": "Delirium tremens; risk of seizures, arrhythmia and death without treatment"}, {"finding": "Bedside glucose below 70 mg/dL", "meaning": "Hypoglycemia; immediately reversible with dextrose"}, {"finding": "Persistent unexplained unresponsiveness despite correcting obvious causes", "meaning": "Possible nonconvulsive status epilepticus; needs EEG"}], "complications": [{"name": "Falls and fractures", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Agitation or attempts to climb out of bed", "Unsteady gait", "Use of restraints or sedatives"]}, {"name": "Aspiration pneumonia", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Dysphagia", "Coughing during meals", "Reduced level of consciousness"]}, {"name": "Pressure ulcers", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Prolonged immobility", "Incontinence", "Malnutrition"]}, {"name": "Functional decline and loss of independence", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Prolonged delirium", "Need for nursing home placement", "New dependence in activities of daily living"]}, {"name": "Increased mortality", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Delirium superimposed on severe illness", "Older age", "Underlying dementia or organ failure"]}, {"name": "Persistent cognitive impairment after recovery", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Longer duration of delirium", "Pre-existing cognitive impairment", "Older age"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Hypoactive delirium is most common and is often mistaken for depression, fatigue or dementia; symptoms may be subtle and recognized only by family or nursing staff. Higher risk of falls, functional decline and mortality."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "May present with irritability, inconsolable crying, refusal to eat or behavioral regression rather than clear disorientation; they may not report hallucinations. Diagnosis relies on observation and identifying an underlying cause."}, {"population": "infants", "differences": "Show poor feeding, lethargy or inconsolable fussiness; the diagnosis is usually made when an underlying illness or medication effect is identified."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Delirium in pregnancy is uncommon and usually signals a severe underlying condition such as eclampsia, sepsis or a medication reaction; presentation may be dominated by obstetric symptoms."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Infection may be the trigger with minimal or no fever, so delirium can be the only sign of a serious infection; a lower threshold for imaging and lumbar puncture is warranted."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Accumulation of medications and metabolic waste can cause or worsen delirium; symptoms may persist until dialysis or dose adjustment. Careful dosing of sedatives and antipsychotics is essential."}, {"population": "hepatic_impairment", "differences": "Hepatic encephalopathy can present with fluctuating confusion, sleep inversion and asterixis; it may be mistaken for primary delirium from another cause. Ammonia levels and clinical response to lactulose help distinguish."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "When did the confusion start, and has it changed since then?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Sudden onset over hours to a few days with fluctuation during the day; a gradual decline over months points to dementia instead"}, {"question": "Is the patient more confused at certain times of day, especially at night?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Worse in the evening and at night with clearer periods in the morning; fluctuation is a core feature"}, {"question": "Can the patient hold attention, follow a conversation, or does their mind wander?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Inattention and difficulty tracking conversation; attention is the key deficit in delirium"}, {"question": "Has the patient been sleepy and withdrawn, or restless and agitated?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Hypoactive, hyperactive or mixed pattern; hypoactive is most common in older adults and easily missed"}, {"question": "Any fever, cough, burning with urination, or other signs of infection?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Urinary tract infection, pneumonia or another infection as the trigger"}, {"question": "Has the patient had any falls, head injury, or new weakness, numbness or trouble speaking?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Head trauma, subdural hematoma, or stroke; focal deficits or trauma require urgent imaging"}, {"question": "What medications has the patient started, stopped or had dose changes on recently?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Sedatives, opioids, anticholinergics, steroids or other deliriogenic drugs; also withdrawal from stopped medications"}, {"question": "How much alcohol or sedative medication does the patient use, and when was the last drink or dose?", "category": "social", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Heavy chronic use with recent cessation 1–5 days ago suggests alcohol or sedative withdrawal and delirium tremens"}, {"question": "Has the patient been eating and drinking normally, or is there vomiting, diarrhea or poor intake?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Dehydration and electrolyte imbalance as reversible causes"}, {"question": "Does the patient have kidney failure, liver disease, diabetes, or other chronic illnesses?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Uremia, hepatic encephalopathy, hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia as triggers"}, {"question": "Has the patient had memory problems or trouble thinking before this episode?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Pre-existing dementia or mild cognitive impairment, which is the strongest risk factor and may coexist"}, {"question": "Has the patient had recent surgery, anesthesia, or been in the hospital or ICU?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Hospitalization, surgery and ICU stay as precipitants"}, {"question": "Is the patient seeing or hearing things that are not there, or acting suspicious or fearful?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Visual hallucinations and paranoid delusions, which support delirium over dementia"}, {"question": "Has the patient had a seizure, loss of consciousness, or stiff neck with headache?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Meningitis, encephalitis or nonconvulsive status epilepticus; these are life-threatening and must not be missed"}, {"question": "Does the patient wear glasses or hearing aids, and are they available?", "category": "ros", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Sensory impairment that worsens confusion and is easily corrected"}], "accepted_names": ["Delirium", "Acute confusional state", "Acute encephalopathy", "Toxic-metabolic encephalopathy", "Altered mental status due to delirium"], "reasoning_points": ["Acute onset over hours to days with a fluctuating course during the day, often worse at night", "Inattention is the core deficit, with disorientation and disorganized thinking", "Either hypoactive (withdrawn, sleepy) or hyperactive (agitated, hallucinating) presentation, or a mix of both", "Evidence of an underlying trigger such as infection, dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, new or changed medication, alcohol or sedative withdrawal, surgery, pain or sleep deprivation", "Pre-existing dementia or cognitive impairment is the strongest risk factor and may coexist with delirium", "Bedside glucose checked immediately and reversible causes addressed before attributing symptoms to dementia or a psychiatric disorder", "Structured bedside assessment such as the Confusion Assessment Method confirms the diagnosis", "Red flags excluded: fever with neck stiffness, focal neurologic deficit, head trauma, hypoxia, hypotension or persistent unresponsiveness", "Dementia, depression, primary psychosis, nonconvulsive status epilepticus, CNS infection, alcohol withdrawal, hepatic encephalopathy and hypoglycemia considered and distinguished"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Older hospitalized patient with sudden confusion and disorientation after admission", "Clear fluctuation with worse confusion at night and clearer periods in the morning", "Obvious inattention on bedside testing", "Identifiable trigger such as urinary tract infection, new sedative or recent surgery", "Hyperactive presentation with agitation and visual hallucinations"], "medium": ["Hypoactive delirium mistaken for depression, fatigue or dementia", "Patient with known dementia who becomes more confused, requiring recognition of delirium superimposed on dementia", "Multiple potential triggers present at once, such as infection plus electrolyte disturbance plus new medication", "Older adult with a normal white count despite infection", "Delirium as the only sign of sepsis or hypoxia"], "hard": ["Fluctuating confusion with no obvious cause, requiring broad workup including imaging and EEG", "Persistent unresponsiveness that does not improve after correcting obvious causes, suggesting nonconvulsive status epilepticus", "Alcohol or sedative withdrawal with autonomic overactivity and risk of seizures", "Hepatic encephalopathy presenting with fluctuating confusion, sleep inversion and asterixis", "Younger patient with delirium from drug intoxication or poisoning", "Patient with focal deficit or head trauma requiring urgent imaging before lumbar puncture"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
