ities (chronic microvascular disease)", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Lacunar infarcts or old cortical infarcts", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Cerebral microbleeds on susceptibility-weighted imaging", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Normal-appearing brain for age (imaging does not exclude dementia)", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "CT head without contrast", "modality": "ct", "preferred_when": "When MRI is unavailable, contraindicated, or the patient cannot tolerate it", "findings": [{"text": "Generalized cerebral atrophy with widened sulci and ventricles", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "White matter hypodensities from chronic small vessel disease", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Old infarcts or lacunes", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Chronic subdural hematoma", "frequency": "rare"}, {"text": "Normal study", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "FDG-PET brain", "modality": "nuclear", "preferred_when": "Diagnostically uncertain cases, especially early-onset or atypical presentations", "findings": [{"text": "Temporoparietal and posterior cingulate hypometabolism (Alzheimer pattern)", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Occipital hypometabolism (Lewy body pattern)", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Frontal or anterior temporal hypometabolism (frontotemporal pattern)", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Patchy, asymmetric hypometabolism (vascular pattern)", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "Amyloid PET brain", "modality": "nuclear", "preferred_when": "Selected cases where Alzheimer disease is suspected but the diagnosis remains uncertain", "findings": [{"text": "Cortical amyloid uptake indicating amyloid plaques", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "No cortical amyloid uptake, arguing against Alzheimer disease as the cause", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "Carotid duplex ultrasound", "modality": "ultrasound", "preferred_when": "When vascular dementia is suspected and carotid revascularization is being considered", "findings": [{"text": "Carotid stenosis or plaque", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "No hemodynamically significant stenosis", "frequency": "common"}]}], "workup": {"initial": [{"test": "cbc", "why": "Screens for anemia, infection or a hematologic cause of cognitive symptoms"}, {"test": "bmp", "why": "Detects hyponatremia, hypercalcemia, uremia and glucose abnormalities, all reversible causes of confusion"}, {"test": "tsh", "why": "Hypothyroidism is a common and reversible cause of cognitive slowing"}, {"test": "b12", "why": "Deficiency is a reversible cause of cognitive decline and neuropathy"}, {"test": "a1c", "why": "Identifies diabetes, a vascular risk factor and a cause of glucose-related cognitive fluctuation"}, {"test": "MRI brain without contrast", "why": "Structural study to look for atrophy patterns, vascular disease and treatable lesions such as subdural hematoma or hydrocephalus"}, {"test": "CT head without contrast", "why": "Alternative structural study when MRI is unavailable or contraindicated", "only_if": "MRI cannot be obtained or the patient is unstable"}], "confirmatory": [{"test": "Neuropsychological testing", "why": "Formal testing documents the pattern and severity of deficits and helps distinguish dementia from depression or normal aging"}], "supporting": [{"test": "folate", "why": "Reversible deficiency when the history suggests poor nutrition or alcohol use", "only_if": "poor nutrition, alcohol use or macrocytic anemia"}, {"test": "HIV", "why": "HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder can mimic dementia", "only_if": "risk factors or systemic features suggest HIV"}, {"test": "VDRL", "why": "Neurosyphilis is a treatable cause of cognitive decline", "only_if": "compatible history or risk factors"}, {"test": "ammonia", "why": "Evaluates for hepatic encephalopathy", "only_if": "liver disease or fluctuating attention with asterixis"}, {"test": "FDG-PET brain", "why": "Helps distinguish dementia subtypes when the diagnosis is uncertain", "only_if": "early-onset or atypical presentation with an uncertain diagnosis"}, {"test": "Amyloid PET brain", "why": "Can support or argue against Alzheimer disease when the diagnosis is uncertain", "only_if": "Alzheimer disease is suspected but the clinical picture is unclear"}, {"test": "Carotid duplex ultrasound", "why": "Assesses for carotid stenosis when vascular dementia is suspected and revascularization is being considered", "only_if": "vascular dementia is suspected and the patient is a candidate for revascularization"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "CT head without contrast", "why": "Not needed if MRI is obtained and tolerated"}, {"test": "FDG-PET brain", "why": "Not part of the routine workup; reserved for diagnostically uncertain cases"}, {"test": "Amyloid PET brain", "why": "Not routine; does not change management in most patients"}, {"test": "Carotid duplex ultrasound", "why": "Not routine unless vascular dementia is suspected and revascularization is being considered"}, {"test": "Ammonia", "why": "Not routine; correlates poorly with encephalopathy and is only useful when liver disease is suspected"}, {"test": "HIV", "why": "Not routine in the absence of risk factors"}, {"test": "VDRL", "why": "Not routine in the absence of risk factors or a compatible history"}, {"test": "Lumbar puncture", "why": "Not routine for typical dementia; reserved for suspected infection, inflammation or rapidly progressive disease"}], "decision_points": ["Delirium must be excluded first: acute onset, fluctuating attention and an identifiable trigger point to delirium rather than dementia", "Depression can mimic dementia and should be screened for, since it is treatable", "Reversible causes (hypothyroidism, B12 deficiency, hyponatremia, hypercalcemia, medication effects) should be excluded before attributing decline to a neurodegenerative disease", "Structural imaging is indicated once to exclude subdural hematoma, tumor, hydrocephalus and stroke", "Rapidly progressive dementia over weeks to months should prompt evaluation for Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, autoimmune encephalitis or central nervous system infection"], "criteria": [{"name": "Dementia syndrome", "summary": "Acquired cognitive decline in one or more domains (memory, language, executive function, visuospatial ability, attention) that is severe enough to interfere with independence in daily activities and represents a decline from a prior level of function. The decline is not explained by delirium or a major psychiatric disorder, and is established by history and objective cognitive testing.", "items": ["Cognitive decline from a previous level of performance", "Impairment in at least one cognitive domain", "Interference with independence in everyday activities", "Not occurring exclusively during delirium", "Not better explained by another mental disorder"]}], "scores": ["mmse", "moca", "cdr", "gds"]}, "differential": [{"name": "Delirium", "slug": "delirium", "why_considered": "Acute confusion in an older adult is far more often delirium than new dementia, and the two can coexist.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Acute onset over hours to days with fluctuating attention", "discriminators": {"history": ["Onset over hours to days rather than months to years", "Attention waxes and wanes through the day", "Recent infection, surgery, new medication, hospitalization or withdrawal", "Family reports the patient is 'not themselves' since a specific date"], "exam": ["Fluctuating level of alertness during the visit", "Marked inattention on digit span or serial sevens", "May be hyperactive, hypoactive or mixed", "Tremor, myoclonus or asterixis when metabolic or withdrawal-related"], "labs": ["CBC and urinalysis showing infection", "BMP showing hyponatremia, hypercalcemia, uremia or glucose abnormality", "Drug levels or toxicology when overdose or withdrawal is suspected"], "imaging": ["Head CT or MRI to exclude acute stroke, hemorrhage or mass when focal findings are present"]}}, {"name": "Major depressive disorder with cognitive symptoms", "slug": "major-depressive-disorder", "why_considered": "Depression in older adults can present with memory complaints, poor concentration and apathy that look like dementia.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Prominent depressed mood, anhedonia or hopelessness alongside cognitive complaints", "discriminators": {"history": ["Depressed mood, tearfulness or loss of pleasure most of the day", "Patient complains loudly about memory while family may not notice as much", "Sleep and appetite disturbance, early morning waking", "Prior episodes of depression or recent bereavement", "Symptoms began after a life stressor or loss"], "exam": ["Psychomotor retardation or agitation", "Poor effort on cognitive testing with 'I don't know' answers", "Cognitive scores improve with encouragement and cueing", "Flat affect and hopelessness"], "labs": ["TSH to exclude hypothyroidism, which can cause both mood and cognitive symptoms"]}}, {"name": "Normal aging with mild cognitive impairment", "slug": "mild-cognitive-impairment", "why_considered": "Many older adults and families worry about dementia when the changes are within the range of normal aging or mild cognitive impairment.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Occasional word-finding pauses and misplacing items but able to retrace steps", "No interference with work, finances, driving or medications", "Family does not report functional decline", "Complaints stable over years rather than clearly progressive"], "exam": ["Cognitive testing shows mild deficits or is normal for age and education", "Independent in all instrumental activities of daily living", "No disorientation to date, place or person"]}}, {"name": "Medication-induced cognitive impairment", "slug": "medication-induced-cognitive-impairment", "why_considered": "Sedatives, anticholinergics and opioids commonly cause or worsen confusion in older adults.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "New or increased sedating or anticholinergic medication before the cognitive change", "discriminators": {"history": ["Recent addition or dose increase of a benzodiazepine, opioid, antihistamine, anticholinergic or antipsychotic", "Symptoms began within days to weeks of the change", "Multiple psychoactive medications (polypharmacy)", "Improvement after stopping the offending drug"], "exam": ["Sedation, slowed speech or gait instability", "Dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention or constipation from anticholinergics", "Attention and alertness fluctuate with dosing"], "labs": ["Medication review is the key test; drug levels when available"]}}, {"name": "Hypothyroidism", "slug": "hypothyroidism", "why_considered": "Hypothyroidism is a reversible cause of cognitive slowing, fatigue and depressed mood in older adults.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Fatigue, cold intolerance, constipation and weight gain with cognitive slowing", "discriminators": {"history": ["Fatigue, cold intolerance, constipation, dry skin", "Weight gain despite unchanged diet", "Hoarse voice and slowed thinking", "Prior thyroid disease, neck radiation or amiodarone use"], "exam": ["Bradycardia, delayed relaxation phase of reflexes", "Dry coarse skin, thinning hair, periorbital puffiness", "Goiter or thyroidectomy scar"], "labs": ["Elevated TSH with low free T4", "Anemia and hyperlipidemia may accompany it"]}}, {"name": "Vitamin B12 deficiency", "slug": "vitamin-b12-deficiency", "why_considered": "B12 deficiency causes reversible cognitive decline and can occur with a normal CBC.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Cognitive decline with paresthesias, imbalance or macrocytic anemia", "discriminators": {"history": ["Vegan or strict vegetarian diet, or prior gastric or ileal surgery", "Metformin or long-term proton pump inhibitor use", "Numbness and tingling in the feet, unsteady gait", "Sore tongue or glossitis"], "exam": ["Decreased vibration and position sense in the feet", "Positive Romberg sign, ataxia", "Hyperreflexia or extensor plantar response", "Pallor or glossitis"], "labs": ["Low serum B12; elevated methylmalonic acid and homocysteine are more sensitive", "Macrocytosis with hypersegmented neutrophils"]}}, {"name": "Chronic subdural hematoma", "slug": "chronic-subdural-hematoma", "why_considered": "A treatable structural cause of progressive confusion, especially in older adults and those on anticoagulants.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Progressive confusion with headache, gait change or a fall weeks earlier", "discriminators": {"history": ["Fall or head injury weeks to months earlier, sometimes unwitnessed", "Anticoagulant or antiplatelet use", "Headache, drowsiness or fluctuating alertness", "Stepwise rather than smooth decline"], "exam": ["Focal weakness, pronator drift or asymmetric reflexes", "Papilledema or decreased level of consciousness", "Gait apraxia or unsteadiness"], "imaging": ["Crescent-shaped extra-axial collection on head CT", "Midline shift or effacement of sulci"]}}, {"name": "Normal pressure hydrocephalus", "slug": "normal-pressure-hydrocephalus", "why_considered": "A potentially reversible triad of gait disturbance, urinary incontinence and cognitive decline in older adults.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Magnetic gait with urinary incontinence and cognitive slowing", "discriminators": {"history": ["Gait difficulty is the earliest and most prominent symptom", "Urinary urgency and incontinence", "Cognitive slowing with relatively preserved memory early", "Symptoms may fluctuate"], "exam": ["Wide-based, shuffling, 'magnetic' gait with difficulty turning", "Impaired postural reflexes", "Frontal release signs"], "imaging": ["Ventriculomegaly out of proportion to cortical atrophy", "Periventricular T2/FLAIR hyperintensity from transependymal flow", "Tight high-convexity sulci"]}}, {"name": "Vascular cognitive impairment", "slug": "vascular-dementia", "why_considered": "Cerebrovascular disease is a common contributor to cognitive decline, often mixed with Alzheimer pathology.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Stepwise decline with plateaus, often after strokes", "Vascular risk factors: hypertension, diabetes, smoking, atrial fibrillation", "Focal neurologic symptoms or transient ischemic attacks", "Early gait disturbance and urinary symptoms"], "exam": ["Focal deficits such as weakness, sensory loss or visual field cut", "Carotid bruits", "Pseudobulbar affect or dysarthria"], "imaging": ["Multiple lacunar infarcts, cortical infarcts or extensive white matter hyperintensities", "Microbleeds on susceptibility-weighted imaging"]}}, {"name": "Lewy body dementia", "slug": "lewy-body-dementia", "why_considered": "A common neurodegenerative dementia with features that change management and medication safety.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Visual hallucinations, often detailed and recurrent", "Fluctuating cognition and alertness", "REM sleep behavior disorder with dream enactment", "Parkinsonism with falls early in the course", "Severe sensitivity to antipsychotic medications"], "exam": ["Resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia", "Postural instability with falls", "Fluctuating attention during the visit"], "imaging": ["Occipital hypometabolism on FDG-PET", "Preserved medial temporal volume on MRI"]}}, {"name": "Frontotemporal dementia", "slug": "frontotemporal-dementia", "why_considered": "A cause of early-onset dementia with prominent behavioral or language changes rather than memory loss.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Onset often before age 65", "Personality change, disinhibition, apathy or loss of empathy", "Compulsive or repetitive behaviors, dietary changes", "Progressive language difficulty with preserved memory early", "Family history of early-onset dementia or motor neuron disease"], "exam": ["Impaired executive function and social conduct", "Preserved orientation and memory early", "Motor neuron disease signs in some patients"], "imaging": ["Frontal and anterior temporal atrophy on MRI", "Frontal or anterior temporal hypometabolism on FDG-PET"]}}, {"name": "Neurosyphilis", "slug": "neurosyphilis", "why_considered": "A treatable infection that can present with cognitive decline, personality change and psychiatric symptoms.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Cognitive decline with risk factors for syphilis, Argyll Robertson pupils or a rash history", "discriminators": {"history": ["History of syphilis or genital ulcer, or multiple sexual partners", "Psychiatric symptoms, personality change or memory loss", "Tremor, unsteady gait or lightning pains"], "exam": ["Argyll Robertson pupils (small, irregular, accommodate but do not react)", "Tremor, dysarthria, ataxia or hyperreflexia", "Optic atrophy or cranial nerve deficits"], "labs": ["Positive serum treponemal and nontreponemal tests", "Reactive CSF VDRL with lymphocytic pleocytosis and elevated protein"]}}, {"name": "HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder", "slug": "hiv-associated-neurocognitive-disorder", "why_considered": "HIV can cause cognitive decline directly or through opportunistic central nervous system infection.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Cognitive decline with HIV risk factors, weight loss or systemic symptoms", "discriminators": {"history": ["Known HIV infection or risk factors", "Weight loss, night sweats, recurrent infections", "Progressive cognitive slowing and apathy"], "exam": ["Impaired attention and processing speed", "Tremor, hyperreflexia or ataxia", "Oral thrush or lymphadenopathy"], "labs": ["Positive HIV antibody or antigen test", "Low CD4 count and elevated viral load"], "imaging": ["Diffuse white matter hyperintensities on MRI", "Cerebral atrophy out of proportion to age"]}}, {"name": "Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease", "slug": "creutzfeldt-jakob-disease", "why_considered": "A rapidly progressive dementia that must be recognized because of its speed and prognosis.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Dementia progressing over weeks to months with myoclonus and ataxia", "discriminators": {"history": ["Rapid progression over weeks to months", "Myoclonic jerks, especially startle-induced", "Unsteady gait and coordination problems", "Behavioral change and insomnia early"], "exam": ["Myoclonus, ataxia, extrapyramidal signs", "Cognitive decline with preserved awareness until late", "Visual disturbances or akinetic mutism late"], "labs": ["CSF 14-3-3 protein and RT-QuIC", "EEG showing periodic sharp wave complexes"], "imaging": ["Cortical ribboning and basal ganglia hyperintensity on diffusion-weighted MRI"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Cognitive decline is detectable on testing and interferes with instrumental activities such as finances, medications and driving, but the patient remains independent in basic self-care and can hold a conversation.", "moderate": "Memory and orientation are clearly impaired; the patient needs help with medications, appointments and some activities of daily living such as dressing or bathing; behavioral symptoms such as apathy, irritability or wandering may appear.", "severe": "The patient is disoriented to person, place and time, is dependent for most activities of daily living, may be incontinent, and has limited or incoherent speech; behavioral and motor complications are common.", "critical": "End-stage disease with immobility, dysphagia, recurrent aspiration and infections; death usually results from aspiration pneumonia, sepsis or another intercurrent illness rather than from the dementia itself."}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Acute onset over hours to days with fluctuating attention", "meaning": "Delirium superimposed on dementia or a non-dementia cause; look for infection, medication, metabolic derangement or withdrawal"}, {"finding": "New focal weakness, sensory loss, aphasia or visual field cut", "meaning": "Stroke or structural lesion; obtain urgent imaging"}, {"finding": "New severe headache, neck stiffness or fever", "meaning": "Central nervous system infection or hemorrhage; urgent evaluation"}, {"finding": "Rapid progression over weeks to months with myoclonus or ataxia", "meaning": "Consider Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, autoimmune encephalitis or central nervous system infection"}, {"finding": "Recent fall or head injury, especially on an anticoagulant", "meaning": "Chronic subdural hematoma; obtain head CT"}, {"finding": "Weight loss, night sweats or focal neurologic signs in a younger patient", "meaning": "Consider HIV, malignancy or opportunistic infection"}, {"finding": "New urinary incontinence with gait deterioration", "meaning": "Consider normal pressure hydrocephalus, which may be reversible"}, {"finding": "Suicidal ideation or severe agitation", "meaning": "Psychiatric emergency requiring immediate safety planning"}], "complications": [{"name": "Delirium superimposed on dementia", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Acute worsening of confusion", "Fluctuating attention", "New infection, medication or metabolic change"]}, {"name": "Falls and fractures", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Gait instability", "Wandering", "Use of sedating medications"]}, {"name": "Malnutrition and weight loss", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Forgotten meals", "Difficulty shopping or preparing food", "Poor appetite"]}, {"name": "Aspiration pneumonia", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Coughing or choking with meals", "Dysphagia", "Fever and hypoxia"]}, {"name": "Urinary tract infection and sepsis", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Incontinence", "Acute confusion", "Fever and dysuria"]}, {"name": "Pressure ulcers", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Immobility", "Incontinence", "Poor nutrition"]}, {"name": "Medication adverse effects", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["New sedating or anticholinergic drug", "Bradycardia or syncope with cholinesterase inhibitors", "Worsening confusion after a medication change"]}, {"name": "Caregiver burnout", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Caregiver reports exhaustion, depression or resentment", "Patient safety lapses at home", "Missed appointments"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "older_adults", "differences": "The largest group affected; presentation may be dominated by falls, weight loss, incontinence or delirium rather than memory complaints, and polypharmacy and sensory impairment worsen function."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "Dementia is very rare in children and usually reflects a metabolic, genetic or neurodegenerative disorder with developmental regression, seizures or loss of previously acquired skills."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Dementia is uncommon in pregnancy; cognitive complaints are more often related to thyroid disease, anemia, mood disorders or eclampsia-related neurologic complications."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Cognitive decline may be due to opportunistic central nervous system infection or HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder and can progress faster; maintain a lower threshold for imaging and CSF studies."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Uremic encephalopathy and dialysis-related fluctuations can mimic dementia; symptoms may wax and wane with dialysis and improve with treatment."}, {"population": "hepatic_impairment", "differences": "Hepatic encephalopathy causes fluctuating attention and asterixis that can look like dementia but reverses with treatment of the liver disease."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Recurrent hypoglycemia and severe hyperglycemia can cause cognitive symptoms that fluctuate with glucose levels and improve with better control."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Midlife obesity is a modifiable vascular risk factor and is associated with a higher risk of later cognitive decline."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "What changes have you or your family noticed, and when did they start?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Insidious onset over months to years of memory and thinking problems, not an abrupt change over hours to days"}, {"question": "Is this getting steadily worse over time?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Slowly progressive decline; stepwise decline suggests vascular disease, rapid decline over weeks suggests Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease or another rapidly progressive cause"}, {"question": "Are these problems interfering with your day-to-day life, like managing money, medications or driving?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Loss of independence in instrumental activities of daily living, which separates dementia from normal aging and mild cognitive impairment"}, {"question": "Do you repeat the same question or story within minutes? Do you misplace items in odd places?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Short-term memory loss with repetition and misplacing items, typical of Alzheimer disease"}, {"question": "Have you gotten lost in places you know well, like your own neighborhood?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Getting lost in familiar places, which supports functional impairment and visuospatial involvement"}, {"question": "Do you have trouble finding the right words or naming familiar objects?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Word-finding difficulty and circumlocution; prominent early language change suggests a frontotemporal disorder"}, {"question": "Have you seen or heard things that others do not see or hear?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recurrent detailed visual hallucinations, which point to Lewy body dementia"}, {"question": "Do you act out your dreams, such as kicking or shouting while asleep?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "REM sleep behavior disorder, which supports Lewy body dementia and often precedes it"}, {"question": "Has your personality or behavior changed, such as losing interest in things or saying inappropriate things?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Apathy, disinhibition or loss of empathy, which suggests frontotemporal dementia"}, {"question": "How is your mood? Have you felt down, hopeless or lost interest in things you enjoy?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Depression, which can mimic dementia and is treatable; also a common comorbidity"}, {"question": "Have you had any sudden confusion, or times when you were less alert and then improved?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Fluctuating attention and acute onset, which point to delirium rather than dementia"}, {"question": "Have you had a recent fall or hit your head, especially if you take a blood thinner?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Chronic subdural hematoma, a treatable structural cause of progressive confusion"}, {"question": "Have you had any new weakness, numbness, trouble speaking or vision loss?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Focal neurologic deficits suggesting stroke, mass lesion or another structural cause"}, {"question": "Have you had a fever, night sweats or weight loss?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Infection such as HIV or another systemic illness causing cognitive decline"}, {"question": "Do you have trouble with your balance or walking, or any new urinary accidents?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Gait disturbance with urinary incontinence, which suggests normal pressure hydrocephalus, a potentially reversible cause"}, {"question": "What medical conditions do you have, such as high blood pressure, diabetes, high cholesterol, atrial fibrillation or prior stroke?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Vascular risk factors that raise the likelihood of vascular contributions to cognitive impairment"}, {"question": "Have you ever had a stroke, transient ischemic attack or head injury?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prior cerebrovascular events or traumatic brain injury, which increase dementia risk"}, {"question": "What medications do you take, including over-the-counter sleep aids, allergy medicines and pain medicines?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Sedating or anticholinergic drugs such as diphenhydramine, oxybutynin, benzodiazepines or opioids that can impair cognition"}, {"question": "Have you started any new medication or had a dose change recently?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "A temporal link between a new drug and cognitive change, suggesting medication-induced impairment"}, {"question": "Does anyone in your family have dementia, especially a parent or sibling, or did anyone develop it before age 65?", "category": "family", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Family history of dementia, which raises risk and may suggest a familial or early-onset form"}, {"question": "How much alcohol do you drink, and have you ever had withdrawal symptoms?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Heavy alcohol use or withdrawal, which can cause cognitive impairment and mimic dementia"}, {"question": "Do you smoke, and have you had exposure to solvents or pesticides at work?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Modifiable vascular risk factors and occupational exposures associated with cognitive decline"}, {"question": "Who do you live with, and who helps you with things like bills, medications and appointments?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Level of support at home and safety concerns such as living alone or driving with getting lost"}, {"question": "Do you still drive, and have you had any near-misses or gotten lost while driving?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Driving safety risk, which affects independence and public safety"}, {"question": "Have you had any thoughts of hurting yourself or others?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Suicidal ideation or severe agitation, which is a psychiatric emergency"}], "accepted_names": ["Dementia", "Major neurocognitive disorder", "Neurocognitive disorder", "Senility"], "reasoning_points": ["Insidious onset over months to years with slowly progressive decline, rather than the acute fluctuating course of delirium", "Cognitive impairment in at least one domain (memory, language, executive function, visuospatial ability or attention) documented by history and objective testing", "The decline interferes with independence in everyday activities such as finances, medications, driving or appointments", "The impairment is not explained by delirium or a major psychiatric disorder such as depression", "Reversible causes have been excluded: hypothyroidism, vitamin B12 deficiency, hyponatremia, hypercalcemia, uremia, glucose abnormalities and medication effects", "Structural imaging has excluded treatable lesions such as chronic subdural hematoma, tumor, normal pressure hydrocephalus and stroke", "The pattern of deficits and any associated features (visual hallucinations, REM sleep behavior disorder, parkinsonism, stepwise decline, prominent behavioral change) help identify the subtype", "Red flags such as rapid progression over weeks, focal neurologic deficits, fever, head trauma or new incontinence with gait change have been considered and addressed"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Older adult with insidious, progressive short-term memory loss over years", "Family reports repeating questions and getting lost in familiar places", "Clear interference with finances and medications", "Cognitive testing shows impaired recall that does not improve with cueing", "Reversible causes excluded and MRI shows typical atrophy"], "medium": ["Older adult with apathy and withdrawal, raising the question of depression versus dementia", "Patient with vascular risk factors and a stepwise decline after a stroke", "Patient with visual hallucinations, fluctuating alertness and dream enactment suggesting Lewy body dementia", "Patient on multiple sedating or anticholinergic medications with cognitive slowing", "Cognitive complaints with a normal neurologic exam and only subtle deficits on testing"], "hard": ["Very old patient presenting with falls, weight loss or incontinence rather than memory complaints", "Delirium superimposed on dementia, with acute worsening and fluctuating attention", "Rapidly progressive dementia over weeks with myoclonus and ataxia suggesting Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease", "Early-onset dementia before age 65 with prominent behavioral or language changes suggesting frontotemporal dementia", "Gait-predominant presentation with urinary incontinence suggesting normal pressure hydrocephalus", "Patient with HIV or another immunocompromising condition and cognitive decline", "Cognitive decline in a patient with renal or hepatic impairment where encephalopathy can mimic dementia"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
