rs to lie still, voice may be hoarse", "severe": "Toxic-appearing, diaphoretic, tachypneic, uncomfortable at rest", "critical": "Severely ill, lethargic or confused, labored breathing, signs of shock or respiratory failure"}, "findings": [{"id": "fever-measured", "region": "general", "maneuver": "Measure the temperature (oral or temporal)", "finding": "Fever, often 100.4–103°F", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "chills-rigors", "region": "general", "maneuver": "Observe the patient and ask about chills", "finding": "Chills or shivering, sometimes rigors", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "ill-appearance", "region": "general", "maneuver": "Observe the patient's general appearance and work of breathing", "finding": "Ill-appearing, flushed, fatigued, prefers to lie still", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "tachycardia", "region": "cardiac", "maneuver": "Measure the heart rate and palpate the radial pulse", "finding": "Tachycardia, often out of proportion to fever", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "tachypnea", "region": "lung", "maneuver": "Count the respiratory rate over a full minute", "finding": "Tachypnea", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "pharyngeal-erythema", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the oropharynx with a light and tongue blade", "finding": "Diffuse pharyngeal erythema, sometimes with enlarged tonsils; no exudate", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "nasal-congestion", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the nasal mucosa", "finding": "Erythematous, congested nasal mucosa with clear discharge", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "conjunctival-injection", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the conjunctivae", "finding": "Conjunctival injection or watery eyes", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "supports", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "cervical-lymphadenopathy", "region": "lymph", "maneuver": "Palpate the cervical lymph node chains", "finding": "Tender, mildly enlarged anterior cervical lymph nodes", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "supports", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "lung-clear", "region": "lung", "maneuver": "Auscultate all lung fields", "finding": "Clear breath sounds without crackles or wheezing", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "no-tachypnea-rest", "region": "lung", "maneuver": "Observe the patient at rest and ask them to speak a full sentence", "finding": "No respiratory distress or accessory muscle use", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-meningismus", "region": "neuro", "maneuver": "Assess neck stiffness and perform a brief neurologic screen", "finding": "No neck stiffness, normal mental status, no focal deficits", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-rash", "region": "skin", "maneuver": "Inspect the skin, including the trunk and extremities", "finding": "No rash or petechiae", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}, {"id": "no-exudate", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the tonsils and posterior pharynx", "finding": "No tonsillar exudate or asymmetry", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "no-cervical-adenopathy-massive", "region": "neck", "maneuver": "Palpate the neck for mass or asymmetry", "finding": "No unilateral neck swelling or mass", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "optional"}]}, "vitals": {"temp": {"mild": {"min": 98.6, "max": 100.4}, "moderate": {"min": 100.4, "max": 102.2}, "severe": {"min": 101.0, "max": 103.5}, "critical": {"min": 102.0, "max": 104.5}}, "hr": {"mild": {"min": 70, "max": 100}, "moderate": {"min": 90, "max": 115}, "severe": {"min": 100, "max": 130}, "critical": {"min": 110, "max": 150}}, "rr": {"moderate": {"min": 16, "max": 22}, "severe": {"min": 20, "max": 28}, "critical": {"min": 24, "max": 36}}, "sbp": {"critical": {"min": 80, "max": 100}}, "dbp": {"critical": {"min": 45, "max": 65}}, "spo2": {"severe": {"min": 90, "max": 95}, "critical": {"min": 82, "max": 92}}}, "labs": [{"test_id": "wbc", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 3.0, "max": 4.5}, "moderate": {"min": 3.5, "max": 11.0}, "severe": {"min": 11.0, "max": 16.0}, "critical": {"min": 14.0, "max": 22.0}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Often normal or mildly low from viral suppression; leukocytosis with a left shift suggests secondary bacterial pneumonia."}, {"test_id": "lymph_pct", "ranges": {"mild": {"min": 40, "max": 55}, "moderate": {"min": 40, "max": 60}, "severe": {"min": 35, "max": 60}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Relative lymphocytosis is common in viral illness; not specific for influenza."}, {"test_id": "crp", "ranges": {"moderate": {"min": 1.0, "max": 4.0}, "severe": {"min": 3.0, "max": 10.0}, "critical": {"min": 8.0, "max": 20.0}}, "frequency": "sometimes", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Nonspecific; marked elevation should raise concern for bacterial superinfection."}, {"test_id": "procalcitonin", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 0.1, "max": 0.5}, "critical": {"min": 0.5, "max": 2.0}}, "frequency": "rare", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Low values favor viral illness; higher values suggest bacterial coinfection."}, {"test_id": "ck", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 308, "max": 1000}, "critical": {"min": 1000, "max": 5000}}, "frequency": "rare", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Check when there is severe myalgia, dark urine, or weakness; elevation suggests influenza myositis or rhabdomyolysis."}, {"test_id": "lactate", "ranges": {"critical": {"min": 2.0, "max": 5.0}}, "frequency": "rare", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Elevated only with severe illness, shock, or secondary bacterial sepsis."}, {"test_id": "hs_tnt", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 22, "max": 100}, "critical": {"min": 50, "max": 500}}, "frequency": "rare", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Order when chest pain, dyspnea, or hemodynamic instability suggests myocarditis."}, {"test_id": "bnp", "ranges": {"severe": {"min": 100, "max": 400}, "critical": {"min": 300, "max": 1000}}, "frequency": "rare", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "May rise with influenza-related myocarditis or heart failure exacerbation."}], "ecg": {"findings": [{"feature": "rhythm", "finding": "Sinus tachycardia", "frequency": "common"}, {"feature": "rate", "finding": "Tachycardia out of proportion to fever", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"feature": "st", "finding": "Diffuse ST-segment elevation or depression suggesting myocarditis", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "severe"}, {"feature": "t_wave", "finding": "T-wave inversions", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "severe"}, {"feature": "conduction", "finding": "New bundle branch block or AV block", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "severe"}]}, "imaging": [{"study": "Chest x-ray", "modality": "xray", "preferred_when": "Suspected pneumonia or hypoxia", "findings": [{"text": "Normal lungs in uncomplicated influenza", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Patchy or lobar consolidation suggesting secondary bacterial pneumonia", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Diffuse bilateral interstitial or alveolar opacities suggesting viral pneumonitis", "frequency": "rare"}, {"text": "Pleural effusion", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "CT chest without contrast", "modality": "ct", "preferred_when": "Chest x-ray nondiagnostic but pneumonia or pneumonitis strongly suspected", "findings": [{"text": "Ground-glass opacities or consolidation", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Multifocal or diffuse involvement", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "Echocardiogram", "modality": "echo", "preferred_when": "Suspected myocarditis, pericarditis, or new heart failure", "findings": [{"text": "Global or regional wall motion abnormality", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Reduced ejection fraction", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Pericardial effusion", "frequency": "rare"}]}], "workup": {"initial": [{"test": "Rapid influenza test", "why": "Rapid antigen or molecular test on a nasopharyngeal swab; confirms influenza and guides antiviral treatment"}, {"test": "cbc", "why": "Assesses for leukocytosis suggesting bacterial superinfection or leukopenia from viral illness", "only_if": "moderate to severe illness or concern for bacterial complication"}, {"test": "bmp", "why": "Assesses hydration and electrolytes in patients who are vomiting or dehydrated", "only_if": "dehydration, vomiting, or severe illness"}, {"test": "Chest x-ray", "why": "Looks for pneumonia when hypoxia, abnormal lung exam, or persistent fever is present", "only_if": "pneumonia is suspected (hypoxia, crackles, or worsening after initial improvement)"}], "confirmatory": [{"test": "Respiratory viral panel (multiplex PCR)", "why": "More sensitive than rapid antigen testing and distinguishes influenza A from B and other viruses", "only_if": "rapid test is negative but influenza is still suspected, or results will change management"}], "supporting": [{"test": "crp", "why": "Supports inflammation; marked elevation raises concern for bacterial coinfection", "only_if": "concern for bacterial superinfection"}, {"test": "procalcitonin", "why": "Low values favor viral illness and argue against bacterial pneumonia", "only_if": "uncertainty about bacterial coinfection"}, {"test": "ck", "why": "Evaluates severe myalgia or dark urine for myositis or rhabdomyolysis", "only_if": "severe muscle pain, weakness, or dark urine"}, {"test": "hs_tnt", "why": "Evaluates for myocarditis when chest pain or dyspnea is present", "only_if": "chest pain, dyspnea, or hemodynamic instability"}, {"test": "Echocardiogram", "why": "Assesses cardiac function and pericardial effusion when myocarditis is suspected", "only_if": "suspected myocarditis or new heart failure"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "Chest x-ray", "why": "Not routine in uncomplicated influenza with clear lungs and no hypoxia"}, {"test": "CT chest without contrast", "why": "Not needed unless complications are suspected and chest x-ray is nondiagnostic"}, {"test": "d_dimer", "why": "No role in diagnosing influenza or its typical complications"}, {"test": "lipase", "why": "Not indicated unless abdominal pain suggests pancreatitis"}, {"test": "ua", "why": "Not indicated unless urinary symptoms or dehydration are present"}], "decision_points": ["Antiviral treatment should start as soon as possible, ideally within 48 hours of symptom onset, and should not be delayed while waiting for test results in high-risk or severely ill patients", "A negative rapid influenza test does not rule out influenza when clinical suspicion is high; consider molecular testing", "Consider bacterial superinfection if symptoms improve then worsen with fever, or if there is hypoxia, purulent sputum, or focal lung findings", "Hospitalize for hypoxia, respiratory distress, altered mental status, inability to tolerate oral intake, or hemodynamic instability"], "criteria": [], "scores": []}, "differential": [{"name": "COVID-19", "slug": "covid-19", "why_considered": "Overlapping febrile respiratory illness with cough, sore throat, myalgias, and fatigue, especially during respiratory virus season.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Respiratory symptoms with fever during community circulation of SARS-CoV-2, or new loss of taste or smell.", "discriminators": {"history": ["Gradual or variable onset rather than the abrupt 'hit by a truck' onset of influenza", "New loss of taste or smell", "Known exposure to a person with COVID-19"], "exam": ["May have hypoxia with relatively mild lung findings", "No specific distinguishing physical finding"], "labs": ["Positive SARS-CoV-2 nucleic acid amplification or antigen test", "Lymphopenia is common"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray often normal early", "CT chest may show peripheral ground-glass opacities"]}}, {"name": "Bacterial pneumonia", "slug": "community-acquired-pneumonia", "why_considered": "Fever, cough, and malaise can mimic influenza, and bacterial pneumonia is a common complication of influenza.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Focal lung findings, hypoxia, purulent sputum, or worsening after initial improvement.", "discriminators": {"history": ["Productive cough with purulent or blood-tinged sputum", "Pleuritic chest pain", "Symptoms that improve then worsen with recurrent fever"], "exam": ["Focal crackles, bronchial breath sounds, dullness to percussion, or egophony", "Tachypnea and hypoxemia"], "labs": ["Leukocytosis with left shift", "Elevated procalcitonin and CRP"], "imaging": ["Lobar or segmental consolidation on chest x-ray", "Air bronchograms or pleural effusion"]}}, {"name": "Common cold (rhinovirus and other seasonal coronaviruses)", "slug": "common-cold", "why_considered": "Nasal congestion, sore throat, and cough are shared with mild influenza.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Gradual onset over one to three days", "Predominantly nasal congestion, sneezing, and sore throat", "Fever is uncommon in adults", "Mild fatigue without prominent body aches"], "exam": ["Low-grade or no fever", "Nasal mucosal swelling with clear discharge", "Lungs clear"], "labs": ["Influenza testing negative", "Respiratory viral panel may identify rhinovirus or seasonal coronavirus"]}}, {"name": "Streptococcal pharyngitis", "slug": "streptococcal-pharyngitis", "why_considered": "Sore throat and fever are prominent in both, and influenza can present with severe throat pain.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Sore throat is the dominant symptom", "Cough and nasal congestion are typically absent", "Abrupt onset of throat pain without prominent myalgias"], "exam": ["Tonsillar exudate, palatal petechiae, or tender anterior cervical adenopathy", "Scarlet fever rash may be present"], "labs": ["Positive rapid antigen detection test or throat culture for group A Streptococcus", "Influenza testing negative"]}}, {"name": "Infectious mononucleosis", "slug": "infectious-mononucleosis", "why_considered": "Fever, sore throat, fatigue, and malaise in adolescents and young adults.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Gradual onset over days to weeks", "Severe sore throat with difficulty swallowing", "Profound fatigue lasting weeks"], "exam": ["Exudative pharyngitis with palatal petechiae", "Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy", "Splenomegaly"], "labs": ["Lymphocytosis with atypical lymphocytes", "Positive heterophile antibody or EBV serology", "Elevated transaminases"]}}, {"name": "Acute bronchitis", "slug": "acute-bronchitis", "why_considered": "Cough with chest discomfort and low-grade fever can resemble mild influenza.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Cough is the predominant symptom, often productive", "Fever is low-grade or absent", "Body aches and chills are minimal"], "exam": ["Diffuse wheezing or coarse breath sounds", "No focal consolidation"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray typically normal without consolidation"]}}, {"name": "Viral gastroenteritis", "slug": "viral-gastroenteritis", "why_considered": "Influenza can cause nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea, especially in children, and patients may call this 'stomach flu.'", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Vomiting and diarrhea are the primary symptoms", "Respiratory complaints such as cough and sore throat are absent or minimal", "Fever may be low-grade"], "exam": ["Diffuse abdominal tenderness without focal findings", "Signs of dehydration may be present"], "labs": ["Influenza testing negative", "Stool testing may identify norovirus or other enteric pathogens"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Afebrile or low-grade fever, mild cough and congestion, able to carry out usual activities with some difficulty, no risk factors for complications.", "moderate": "Fever 100.4–102.2°F, prominent myalgias, headache, and cough, feels too unwell to work or attend school, but no respiratory distress or dehydration.", "severe": "High fever, marked prostration, tachypnea, hypoxemia, or inability to tolerate oral intake; may require hospitalization, especially with underlying chronic disease.", "critical": "Respiratory failure, shock, encephalopathy, or myocarditis requiring intensive care; most often in older adults, infants, pregnant people, or the immunocompromised."}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Shortness of breath at rest or oxygen saturation below 92%", "meaning": "Suggests viral pneumonitis or secondary bacterial pneumonia; needs urgent evaluation and imaging."}, {"finding": "Symptoms improve for a few days then worsen with high fever and productive cough", "meaning": "Classic pattern of secondary bacterial pneumonia."}, {"finding": "Confusion, lethargy, or difficulty waking", "meaning": "Possible influenza encephalopathy, sepsis, or severe dehydration; requires emergent workup."}, {"finding": "Chest pain, palpitations, or syncope", "meaning": "Raises concern for myocarditis or pericarditis."}, {"finding": "Severe muscle pain with dark urine or weakness", "meaning": "Suggests rhabdomyolysis or influenza myositis."}, {"finding": "Inability to keep fluids down or signs of dehydration", "meaning": "May require intravenous fluids and hospitalization."}, {"finding": "Hypotension or tachycardia out of proportion to fever", "meaning": "Suggests sepsis or hemodynamic compromise."}], "complications": [{"name": "Secondary bacterial pneumonia", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Improvement followed by worsening fever and cough", "Purulent sputum", "Focal crackles or consolidation on chest x-ray", "Leukocytosis with left shift"]}, {"name": "Primary influenza viral pneumonia", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Rapid progression of dyspnea and hypoxemia within 1–2 days of onset", "Diffuse bilateral opacities on chest imaging", "Cyanosis or respiratory failure"]}, {"name": "Myocarditis or pericarditis", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Chest pain, dyspnea, or palpitations", "New ECG changes or elevated troponin", "Wall motion abnormality on echocardiogram"]}, {"name": "Encephalitis or encephalopathy", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Altered mental status, seizures, or focal neurologic deficits", "Fever with confusion"]}, {"name": "Myositis or rhabdomyolysis", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Severe calf or thigh pain", "Dark urine", "Elevated creatine kinase"]}, {"name": "Otitis media", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Ear pain or pressure", "Bulging, erythematous tympanic membrane", "More common in children"]}, {"name": "Sinusitis", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Facial pain or pressure", "Persistent nasal congestion and purulent discharge"]}, {"name": "Exacerbation of chronic disease (asthma, COPD, heart failure, diabetes)", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Wheezing or increased inhaler use", "Worsening dyspnea or edema", "Hyperglycemia or diabetic ketoacidosis"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Vomiting and diarrhea are more common; febrile seizures can occur; otitis media and croup are more frequent complications. Children under 5, especially under 2, are at higher risk of severe disease."}, {"population": "infants", "differences": "May present with fever, poor feeding, lethargy, or apnea rather than classic cough and myalgias; sepsis evaluation is often considered."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "May lack fever and present with weakness, confusion, or decompensation of chronic illness; higher risk of pneumonia, hospitalization, and death."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Higher risk of severe illness, hospitalization, preterm labor, and fetal complications; antiviral treatment should be started promptly and is recommended in pregnancy."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Prolonged viral shedding, atypical or more severe presentation, higher risk of viral pneumonia and bacterial superinfection; may require extended antiviral therapy."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Higher risk of severe influenza and of hyperglycemia or diabetic ketoacidosis during illness; monitor glucose closely."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Higher risk of severe influenza, hospitalization, and respiratory failure."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "How did your symptoms start — suddenly or gradually?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Abrupt onset over a few hours; patients often can name the hour they started feeling sick"}, {"question": "When did the symptoms start?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Within the last 1–2 days; determines whether antiviral therapy can still be started within 48 hours"}, {"question": "Have you had a fever or chills?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Fever or feeling feverish with chills or shivering; not everyone with influenza has a fever"}, {"question": "Do your muscles or body ache?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prominent diffuse myalgias, often described as feeling like being hit by a truck"}, {"question": "How severe is your fatigue?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Marked prostration and weakness, worse than a typical cold"}, {"question": "Do you have a cough, sore throat, or runny nose?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Dry or minimally productive cough, sore throat, and nasal congestion accompanying the systemic symptoms"}, {"question": "Have you had any nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Sometimes present, more common in children; vomiting and diarrhea as the only symptoms suggest gastroenteritis rather than influenza"}, {"question": "Has anyone around you been sick with fever, cough, and body aches?", "category": "social", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Sick contacts at home, school, or work, or influenza circulating in the community during flu season"}, {"question": "Have you been vaccinated against influenza this season?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Not vaccinated increases likelihood; vaccination reduces risk and severity but does not rule out influenza"}, {"question": "Do you have any chronic conditions such as asthma, diabetes, heart disease, or kidney disease?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Conditions that increase the risk of complications and lower the threshold for antiviral treatment"}, {"question": "Are you pregnant, or could you be pregnant?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Pregnancy increases the risk of severe illness; antiviral treatment is recommended and should be started promptly"}, {"question": "Are you taking any medications that weaken your immune system, such as steroids or chemotherapy?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Immunosuppression, which raises the risk of severe or prolonged illness"}, {"question": "Are you short of breath at rest, or does it hurt to breathe?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Dyspnea or pleuritic chest pain suggesting pneumonia, viral pneumonitis, or myocarditis"}, {"question": "Did your symptoms improve and then get worse again with a new fever?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Classic pattern of secondary bacterial pneumonia"}, {"question": "Have you been confused, unusually sleepy, or unable to keep fluids down?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Encephalopathy, severe dehydration, or sepsis requiring urgent evaluation"}], "accepted_names": ["Influenza", "Flu", "Seasonal influenza", "Influenza A", "Influenza B", "Human influenza"], "reasoning_points": ["Abrupt onset of fever, chills, myalgias, and fatigue over a few hours", "Prominent systemic symptoms with dry cough, sore throat, and nasal congestion", "Sick contact or community circulation of influenza during fall or winter", "Absence of gradual-onset, predominantly nasal symptoms typical of a common cold", "No focal lung findings, hypoxia, or purulent sputum to suggest bacterial pneumonia", "Rapid influenza test or molecular testing confirms influenza when obtained", "Risk factors for complications (age, pregnancy, chronic disease, immunosuppression) identified to guide antiviral treatment"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Classic abrupt onset of fever, chills, myalgias, and cough during flu season", "Clear sick contact at home, school, or work", "Ill-appearing but lungs clear and no hypoxia", "Rapid influenza test positive"], "medium": ["Older adult without fever presenting with weakness or confusion", "Patient with asthma or COPD whose respiratory symptoms are worsening", "Symptoms overlap with COVID-19 or another respiratory virus", "Negative rapid antigen test but high clinical suspicion"], "hard": ["Infant with fever, poor feeding, and lethargy rather than classic cough and myalgias", "Pregnant patient with severe illness and concern for complications", "Immunocompromised patient with prolonged or atypical presentation", "Symptoms improve then worsen with high fever and productive cough suggesting secondary bacterial pneumonia", "Chest pain or dyspnea raising concern for myocarditis"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
