ider angiomata, palmar erythema, jaundice"], "labs": ["Thrombocytopenia", "Elevated INR", "Low albumin and elevated bilirubin"], "imaging": ["Esophageal or gastric varices on EGD", "Signs of portal hypertension on ultrasound or CT"]}}, {"name": "Mallory-Weiss tear", "slug": "mallory-weiss-tear", "why_considered": "Vomiting blood after forceful retching or vomiting, often after alcohol use, mimics other upper GI bleeding sources.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Forceful vomiting or retching before hematemesis", "Heavy alcohol use or bulimia"], "exam": ["Usually no epigastric tenderness or chronic liver stigmata"], "labs": ["Anemia proportional to blood loss", "No thrombocytopenia or coagulopathy"], "imaging": ["Linear mucosal tear at the gastroesophageal junction on EGD"]}}, {"name": "Diverticular bleeding", "slug": "diverticular-bleeding", "why_considered": "The most common cause of lower GI bleeding in older adults; presents with sudden, painless hematochezia.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden, painless passage of large amounts of red or maroon blood", "Age over 60", "Known diverticulosis"], "exam": ["Usually benign abdomen without tenderness", "Bright red blood on digital rectal exam"], "labs": ["Anemia from acute blood loss", "No coagulopathy unless on anticoagulants"], "imaging": ["Diverticula with stigmata of recent bleeding on colonoscopy", "Active contrast extravasation on CT angiography if bleeding is ongoing"]}}, {"name": "Colorectal cancer", "slug": "colorectal-cancer", "why_considered": "Can present with occult or overt lower GI bleeding, especially in older adults with iron deficiency anemia or changed bowel habits.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Iron deficiency anemia, weight loss, or a change in bowel habits in an older adult", "discriminators": {"history": ["Change in bowel habits", "Unintentional weight loss", "Family history of colorectal cancer or polyps"], "exam": ["Palpable abdominal mass", "Occult or visible blood on digital rectal exam"], "labs": ["Iron deficiency anemia with low MCV and ferritin", "Positive fecal occult blood test"], "imaging": ["Colonic mass or stricture on colonoscopy", "Mass or lymphadenopathy on CT"]}}, {"name": "Ischemic colitis", "slug": "ischemic-colitis", "why_considered": "Presents with lower abdominal pain and hematochezia in older adults, often with vascular risk factors.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Abrupt crampy lower abdominal pain followed by urgency to defecate", "Older age with atherosclerosis, atrial fibrillation, or hypotension"], "exam": ["Mild to moderate abdominal tenderness", "Blood on rectal exam"], "labs": ["Leukocytosis", "Elevated lactate in severe cases"], "imaging": ["Segmental colonic wall thickening on CT", "Mucosal edema, erythema, or ulceration on colonoscopy"]}}, {"name": "Inflammatory bowel disease", "slug": "inflammatory-bowel-disease", "why_considered": "Ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease can cause chronic or acute lower GI bleeding with diarrhea and abdominal pain.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Chronic diarrhea, often bloody", "Weight loss", "Younger age at onset"], "exam": ["Abdominal tenderness", "Perianal disease or fistulas in Crohn disease"], "labs": ["Anemia and leukocytosis", "Elevated inflammatory markers"], "imaging": ["Continuous mucosal inflammation from the rectum in ulcerative colitis", "Skip lesions and transmural inflammation in Crohn disease"]}}, {"name": "Hemorrhoidal bleeding", "slug": "hemorrhoids", "why_considered": "Bright red blood per rectum, often after defecation, is a common benign cause of lower GI bleeding.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Bright red blood on toilet paper or coating the stool", "Painless bleeding after bowel movements", "Constipation or straining"], "exam": ["Visible or palpable hemorrhoids on rectal exam", "No abdominal tenderness or mass"], "labs": ["Usually normal hemoglobin unless chronic bleeding"], "imaging": ["Hemorrhoids on anoscopy or colonoscopy", "No proximal source of bleeding"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Occult or small-volume bleeding with normal vital signs and minimal or no anemia; patient is alert and comfortable.", "moderate": "Visible bleeding with mild tachycardia or orthostatic changes, mild pallor, and moderate anemia; patient is hemodynamically stable but symptomatic.", "severe": "Ongoing or large-volume bleeding with resting tachycardia, hypotension, marked pallor, and significant anemia; patient may be anxious or restless.", "critical": "Massive hemorrhage with shock, altered mental status, cool clammy extremities, and severe anemia; requires immediate resuscitation and urgent intervention."}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Hematemesis or coffee-ground emesis", "meaning": "Upper GI bleeding; requires urgent endoscopy and resuscitation"}, {"finding": "Melena or hematochezia with hemodynamic instability", "meaning": "Significant ongoing blood loss; resuscitate and localize source urgently"}, {"finding": "Systolic blood pressure below 90 mmHg or heart rate above 120", "meaning": "Shock from blood loss; immediate fluid and blood resuscitation"}, {"finding": "Altered mental status or syncope", "meaning": "Severe hypoperfusion; indicates critical bleeding"}, {"finding": "Rigid abdomen or rebound tenderness", "meaning": "Perforation with peritonitis; surgical emergency"}, {"finding": "Known cirrhosis with hematemesis", "meaning": "Possible variceal bleed; high mortality, needs urgent endoscopic therapy"}], "complications": [{"name": "Hemorrhagic shock", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Hypotension", "Tachycardia", "Cool extremities", "Altered mental status"]}, {"name": "Severe anemia requiring transfusion", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Pallor", "Fatigue", "Shortness of breath", "Low hemoglobin"]}, {"name": "Acute kidney injury from hypovolemia", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Rising creatinine", "Oliguria", "Elevated BUN/creatinine ratio"]}, {"name": "Recurrent or rebleeding", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Return of hematemesis or melena", "Falling hemoglobin", "Hemodynamic changes"]}, {"name": "Aspiration pneumonia", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Vomiting with altered consciousness", "Cough, fever, hypoxia after endoscopy"]}, {"name": "Death", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Massive uncontrolled bleeding", "Multi-organ failure", "Failure to respond to resuscitation"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "older_adults", "differences": "May present with confusion, falls, or weakness without obvious bleeding signs; less tachycardia and pain; higher mortality and more comorbid conditions."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "Often presents with vomiting blood or blood in stool due to swallowed maternal blood, esophagitis, or intussusception; may have irritability and poor feeding; NSAID use and cirrhosis are uncommon."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "May present with hyperemesis causing Mallory-Weiss tear or with bleeding from esophageal varices due to pregnancy-related liver changes; symptoms may be attributed to morning sickness; pregnancy test required before imaging."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "May have bleeding from CMV or other opportunistic infections, or from medication-related ulcers; fever and systemic symptoms may be present; consider infectious causes."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Uremia can cause platelet dysfunction and bleeding; also at higher risk from anticoagulants and NSAIDs; careful medication dosing and reversal."}, {"population": "hepatic_impairment", "differences": "Cirrhosis with portal hypertension increases risk of variceal bleeding and coagulopathy; requires aggressive reversal and specific therapy."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "What did the blood look like — did you vomit red blood or coffee-ground material?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Hematemesis or coffee-ground emesis localizes the source to the upper GI tract"}, {"question": "What color are your stools — black and tarry, maroon, or bright red?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Melena suggests an upper source; hematochezia suggests a lower source (or a brisk upper bleed)"}, {"question": "When did the bleeding start, and is it still going on?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Ongoing bleeding with instability requires urgent resuscitation and endoscopy"}, {"question": "How much blood have you seen — a few drops, a cupful, or more?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Estimates volume lost and guides triage and transfusion needs"}, {"question": "Do you have abdominal pain, and where is it?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Epigastric pain suggests ulcer; painless bleeding suggests diverticular or variceal source"}, {"question": "Have you felt lightheaded, dizzy on standing, or fainted?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Hypoperfusion from significant blood loss"}, {"question": "Have you had any forceful vomiting or retching before the blood appeared?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Suggests a Mallory-Weiss tear at the gastroesophageal junction"}, {"question": "Have you had black stools or a positive stool test before?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prior GI bleeding or chronic occult blood loss"}, {"question": "Do you have a history of ulcers, liver disease, or diverticulosis?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Peptic ulcer disease, cirrhosis with varices, or diverticular disease as the source"}, {"question": "Do you take NSAIDs, aspirin, or blood thinners?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "NSAIDs and antithrombotics cause ulcers and worsen bleeding; anticoagulants require reversal"}, {"question": "How much alcohol do you drink?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Heavy use raises risk of varices, gastritis, and Mallory-Weiss tear"}, {"question": "Have you had unintentional weight loss or a change in bowel habits?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Suggests colorectal cancer as the bleeding source"}, {"question": "Could you be pregnant? When was your last menstrual period?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Excludes pregnancy-related bleeding and is required before imaging or endoscopy"}, {"question": "Any blood in your urine or vaginal bleeding?", "category": "ros", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Excludes a urinary or gynecologic source of the blood"}], "accepted_names": ["Gastrointestinal bleeding", "GI bleeding", "Gastrointestinal hemorrhage", "GI hemorrhage", "Upper GI bleed", "Lower GI bleed"], "reasoning_points": ["Localizes the source as upper or lower GI based on hematemesis, melena, or hematochezia", "Identifies risk factors such as NSAID use, alcohol, cirrhosis, or anticoagulation", "Assesses hemodynamic stability and severity of blood loss", "Recognizes an elevated BUN/creatinine ratio as suggesting an upper GI source", "Excludes pregnancy before imaging or endoscopy", "Selects EGD for suspected upper bleeding and colonoscopy for suspected lower bleeding", "Considers must-not-miss causes: peptic ulcer, varices, and colorectal cancer", "Plans resuscitation, transfusion, and reversal of coagulopathy when indicated"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Classic hematemesis or coffee-ground emesis with melena", "Known peptic ulcer disease or NSAID use", "Epigastric tenderness on exam", "Anemia with an elevated BUN/creatinine ratio"], "medium": ["Melena without overt hematemesis", "Older adult with painless hematochezia from diverticular bleeding", "Patient on warfarin or a direct oral anticoagulant", "Normal hemoglobin early in acute bleeding"], "hard": ["Older adult presenting with confusion, falls, or weakness without obvious bleeding", "Occult bleeding with iron deficiency anemia and no visible blood", "Brisk upper GI bleed presenting as hematochezia", "Cirrhotic patient with variceal bleeding and coagulopathy", "Pregnant patient with bleeding attributed to morning sickness"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
