e time (pH below 4 for more than about 4–6% of the study)", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Symptom episodes correlate with acid reflux events", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Normal acid exposure (functional heartburn)", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "Esophageal manometry", "modality": "other", "preferred_when": "Before antireflux surgery or when a motility disorder is suspected", "findings": [{"text": "Low lower esophageal sphincter resting pressure", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Normal esophageal peristalsis", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Weak or absent peristalsis suggesting a motility disorder", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "Chest x-ray", "modality": "xray", "findings": [{"text": "Usually normal; no role in diagnosing GERD", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "Retrocardiac air-fluid level from a large hiatal hernia", "frequency": "rare"}]}], "workup": {"initial": [{"test": "cbc", "why": "Baseline blood count; anemia would suggest erosive disease or another bleeding source", "only_if": "alarm features such as dysphagia, weight loss, or suspected GI bleeding"}, {"test": "hs_tnt", "why": "Excludes acute coronary syndrome when chest pain could be cardiac", "only_if": "chest pain is not clearly reflux, or there are cardiac risk factors"}, {"test": "hcg", "why": "Rules out pregnancy before endoscopy, sedation, or medication changes", "only_if": "patient could be pregnant"}, {"test": "Upper GI endoscopy (esophagogastroduodenoscopy)", "why": "Directly assesses for esophagitis, stricture, and Barrett's esophagus", "only_if": "alarm features, age over 60 with new symptoms, or failure of empiric PPI therapy"}], "confirmatory": [{"test": "Ambulatory esophageal pH monitoring", "why": "Documents abnormal acid exposure when symptoms persist despite therapy and endoscopy is normal"}], "supporting": [{"test": "Barium swallow (upper GI series)", "why": "Evaluates anatomy and stricture when endoscopy is unavailable or not tolerated", "only_if": "dysphagia or suspected stricture"}, {"test": "Esophageal manometry", "why": "Assesses peristalsis and sphincter function before antireflux surgery", "only_if": "antireflux surgery is being considered or a motility disorder is suspected"}, {"test": "lipase", "why": "Excludes pancreatitis when epigastric pain is prominent", "only_if": "epigastric pain suggests pancreatitis"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "Chest x-ray", "why": "Does not diagnose GERD and rarely changes management"}, {"test": "d_dimer", "why": "No role in typical reflux symptoms"}, {"test": "Abdominal ultrasound", "why": "Not useful for reflux; consider only if biliary or pancreatic disease is suspected"}], "decision_points": ["Trial of lifestyle changes plus a proton pump inhibitor is appropriate first-line therapy for classic symptoms without alarm features", "Endoscopy is indicated for alarm features (dysphagia, odynophagia, weight loss, GI bleeding, anemia), age over 60 with new-onset symptoms, or failure of empiric therapy", "Exclude cardiac causes of chest pain before attributing symptoms to GERD when the presentation is atypical", "pH monitoring is reserved for persistent symptoms with normal endoscopy or before antireflux surgery"], "criteria": [], "scores": []}, "differential": [{"name": "Acute coronary syndrome", "slug": "acute-coronary-syndrome", "why_considered": "Burning mid-chest discomfort that can be mistaken for heartburn; both are common and can occur after meals or at rest", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Chest pain with exertional onset, diaphoresis, shortness of breath, or pain radiating to the jaw, neck, or arm", "discriminators": {"history": ["Pressure or squeezing quality rather than burning", "Provoked by exertion and relieved by rest", "Radiation to jaw, neck, or left arm", "Associated dyspnea, diaphoresis, or nausea", "Cardiac risk factors such as diabetes, smoking, hypertension, or hyperlipidemia"], "exam": ["Diaphoresis, pallor, or distress", "New S3, S4, or murmur", "Hypotension or tachycardia"], "labs": ["Elevated high-sensitivity troponin", "Rising troponin on serial testing"], "imaging": ["Regional wall motion abnormality on echocardiography", "Coronary stenosis on angiography"], "ecg": ["ST elevation or depression", "T-wave inversion", "New Q waves or conduction abnormality"]}}, {"name": "Peptic ulcer disease", "slug": "peptic-ulcer-disease", "why_considered": "Epigastric burning pain that may be relieved or worsened by food and overlaps with reflux symptoms", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Epigastric pain that wakes the patient at night, or melena, hematemesis, or anemia", "discriminators": {"history": ["Epigastric rather than retrosternal pain", "Pain relieved by food (duodenal) or worsened by food (gastric)", "NSAID use or prior H. pylori infection", "Melena or hematemesis"], "exam": ["Focal epigastric tenderness", "Signs of anemia such as pallor", "Peritoneal signs if perforated"], "labs": ["Anemia with low ferritin", "Positive H. pylori stool antigen or urea breath test"], "imaging": ["Gastric or duodenal ulcer on endoscopy", "Free air on upright chest x-ray if perforated"]}}, {"name": "Esophageal cancer", "slug": "esophageal-cancer", "why_considered": "Reflux is a risk factor and early disease can present with heartburn or regurgitation before dysphagia develops", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Progressive solid-food dysphagia, unintentional weight loss, or iron-deficiency anemia in a patient with long-standing reflux", "discriminators": {"history": ["Progressive dysphagia, first to solids then liquids", "Unintentional weight loss", "Odynophagia", "Long-standing GERD or Barrett's esophagus", "Tobacco or heavy alcohol use"], "exam": ["Cachexia or temporal wasting", "Supraclavicular or cervical lymphadenopathy", "Hepatomegaly if metastatic"], "labs": ["Iron-deficiency anemia", "Elevated liver enzymes if hepatic metastases"], "imaging": ["Irregular mass or stricture with mucosal destruction on endoscopy", "Malignant-appearing stricture on barium swallow", "Mass or lymphadenopathy on CT or endoscopic ultrasound"]}}, {"name": "Achalasia", "slug": "achalasia", "why_considered": "Regurgitation and chest discomfort that can be mistaken for reflux", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Dysphagia to both solids and liquids from the outset", "Regurgitation of undigested food hours after eating", "Nocturnal regurgitation and aspiration", "Heartburn less prominent or absent"], "exam": ["Usually normal abdominal exam", "Signs of weight loss"], "imaging": ["Dilated esophagus with a smooth bird-beak narrowing at the gastroesophageal junction on barium swallow", "Absent peristalsis and failure of lower esophageal sphincter relaxation on manometry"]}}, {"name": "Functional heartburn", "slug": "functional-heartburn", "why_considered": "Burning retrosternal discomfort with normal endoscopy and no response to acid suppression", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Symptoms persist despite adequate proton pump inhibitor therapy", "No relationship to meals or lying down", "Associated anxiety or somatization"], "exam": ["Normal examination"], "labs": ["Normal blood count and metabolic panel"], "imaging": ["Normal endoscopy", "Normal esophageal acid exposure on pH monitoring", "No symptom correlation with reflux events"]}}, {"name": "Eosinophilic esophagitis", "slug": "eosinophilic-esophagitis", "why_considered": "Reflux-like symptoms with dysphagia, more common in younger patients with atopy", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Dysphagia or food impaction", "History of asthma, eczema, or allergic rhinitis", "Poor response to proton pump inhibitors"], "exam": ["Usually normal", "Oral allergy findings such as lip or tongue swelling"], "labs": ["Peripheral eosinophilia sometimes present"], "imaging": ["Rings, furrows, or white exudates on endoscopy", "Esophageal eosinophils on biopsy"]}}, {"name": "Biliary colic or cholecystitis", "slug": "cholecystitis", "why_considered": "Right upper quadrant or epigastric pain after fatty meals that can be confused with reflux", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Pain in the right upper quadrant or epigastrium", "Colicky pain after fatty meals", "Radiation to the right shoulder or back", "Fever and vomiting if cholecystitis"], "exam": ["Positive Murphy sign", "Right upper quadrant tenderness or palpable gallbladder"], "labs": ["Leukocytosis", "Elevated alkaline phosphatase, GGT, or bilirubin"], "imaging": ["Gallstones, gallbladder wall thickening, or pericholecystic fluid on ultrasound", "Biliary dilation on ultrasound or MRCP"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Intermittent heartburn less than twice a week, no nocturnal symptoms, no esophagitis on endoscopy, symptoms controlled with lifestyle changes or occasional antacids", "moderate": "Heartburn two or more times a week, nocturnal symptoms, mild erosive esophagitis (Los Angeles grade A or B), symptoms controlled with daily proton pump inhibitor", "severe": "Daily symptoms despite proton pump inhibitor, severe erosive esophagitis (grade C or D), peptic stricture, or Barrett's esophagus", "critical": "Complicated disease with GI bleeding, perforation, or severe aspiration; life-threatening but uncommon"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Progressive dysphagia or odynophagia", "meaning": "Suggests stricture, severe esophagitis, or malignancy; endoscopy is indicated"}, {"finding": "Unintentional weight loss", "meaning": "Raises concern for malignancy or severe dysphagia; endoscopy is indicated"}, {"finding": "Hematemesis, melena, or iron-deficiency anemia", "meaning": "Suggests erosive esophagitis, ulcer, or malignancy; urgent endoscopy is indicated"}, {"finding": "Chest pain with dyspnea, diaphoresis, or radiation to jaw or arm", "meaning": "Must exclude acute coronary syndrome before attributing to reflux"}, {"finding": "Choking, coughing, or nocturnal aspiration", "meaning": "Suggests severe reflux with aspiration risk; further evaluation and aggressive therapy needed"}, {"finding": "Failure of an adequate proton pump inhibitor trial", "meaning": "Suggests an alternative diagnosis or complicated disease; endoscopy and pH monitoring should be considered"}], "complications": [{"name": "Erosive esophagitis", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Persistent symptoms despite therapy", "Odynophagia", "Endoscopic mucosal breaks"]}, {"name": "Peptic stricture", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Progressive solid-food dysphagia", "Food impaction", "Smooth tapering narrowing on barium swallow"]}, {"name": "Barrett's esophagus", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Long-standing GERD symptoms", "Salmon-colored mucosa above the gastroesophageal junction on endoscopy", "Intestinal metaplasia on biopsy"]}, {"name": "Esophageal adenocarcinoma", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Progressive dysphagia", "Weight loss", "Irregular mass or malignant stricture on endoscopy"]}, {"name": "GI bleeding from esophagitis", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Hematemesis or melena", "Anemia", "Hemodynamic instability"]}, {"name": "Aspiration pneumonia", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Nocturnal cough or choking", "Fever and productive cough", "Infiltrate on chest x-ray"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Older children may report heartburn, but younger children often present with recurrent vomiting, poor feeding, irritability, or respiratory symptoms rather than classic burning chest pain."}, {"population": "infants", "differences": "Infants typically present with spitting up, feeding refusal, arching of the back, irritability, or poor weight gain; classic heartburn cannot be reported."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Symptoms may be milder or atypical, with more dysphagia, respiratory symptoms, or silent reflux; they are also more likely to have severe esophagitis or Barrett's esophagus."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Heartburn is very common in the third trimester due to increased abdominal pressure and hormonal effects; symptoms usually improve after delivery. Treatment options are limited to pregnancy-safe medications."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Symptoms tend to be more frequent and severe, and are more likely to persist; weight loss often improves symptoms."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "What does the burning feel like, and where exactly is it?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Burning behind the breastbone (mid-chest), sometimes rising into the throat"}, {"question": "When does it happen in relation to meals and lying down?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "30–60 minutes after meals, worse when lying down or bending over"}, {"question": "How often do you get it, and how long has this been going on?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Two or more times a week for months to years supports GERD rather than occasional reflux"}, {"question": "Does anything come up into your throat or mouth?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Regurgitation of sour or bitter fluid, or a sudden excess of saliva (water brash)"}, {"question": "What makes it better or worse?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Worse with large, fatty, or spicy meals, alcohol, caffeine, chocolate, and tight clothing; better with antacids and sitting upright"}, {"question": "Do you have any trouble swallowing, painful swallowing, or food getting stuck?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Progressive solid-food dysphagia or odynophagia suggests stricture, severe esophagitis, or malignancy"}, {"question": "Have you vomited blood or coffee-ground material, or had black tarry stools?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Hematemesis or melena suggests erosive esophagitis, ulcer, or malignancy"}, {"question": "Have you lost weight without trying?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Unintentional weight loss raises concern for malignancy or severe dysphagia"}, {"question": "Does the chest pain ever come with shortness of breath, sweating, or pain in your jaw or arm?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "These features suggest acute coronary syndrome and must be excluded before attributing pain to reflux"}, {"question": "Do you have a chronic cough, hoarse voice, or a lump-in-the-throat feeling?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Extraesophageal reflux symptoms such as dry cough, morning hoarseness, or globus"}, {"question": "Do you ever wake at night with reflux, cough, or choking?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Nocturnal symptoms suggest more severe disease and aspiration risk"}, {"question": "What medicines do you take, including over-the-counter pain relievers?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "NSAIDs, aspirin, alendronate, calcium channel blockers, nitrates, or prednisone can worsen reflux"}, {"question": "Do you smoke or drink alcohol, and how much?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Tobacco and alcohol use are modifiable risk factors"}, {"question": "Have you gained weight recently, or do you eat large or late meals?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Obesity and late evening meals are strong contributors to reflux"}, {"question": "Has anyone in your family had frequent heartburn or GERD?", "category": "family", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Family history of GERD or frequent heartburn"}, {"question": "Could you be pregnant? When was your last period?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Pregnancy is a common cause of reflux and affects medication and testing choices"}], "accepted_names": ["GERD", "Gastroesophageal reflux disease", "Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease", "GORD", "Gastroesophageal reflux"], "reasoning_points": ["Classic burning retrosternal discomfort occurring 30–60 minutes after meals and worse when lying down or bending over", "Regurgitation of sour or bitter fluid, sometimes with water brash", "Symptoms occurring two or more times a week over months to years, distinguishing GERD from occasional physiologic reflux", "Relief with antacids and with sitting upright, and improvement with smaller meals and head-of-bed elevation", "Risk factors such as obesity, hiatal hernia, smoking, pregnancy, and large or late meals", "No alarm features: no dysphagia, odynophagia, GI bleeding, anemia, or unintentional weight loss", "Cardiac causes of chest pain excluded when the presentation is atypical", "Extraesophageal symptoms such as chronic dry cough, hoarseness, or globus sensation support the diagnosis", "Diagnosis is clinical in typical cases; endoscopy is reserved for alarm features, older age with new symptoms, or failure of empiric therapy"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Middle-aged adult with classic postprandial burning behind the breastbone", "Symptoms worse lying down and relieved by antacids", "Obesity or known hiatal hernia as a risk factor", "No alarm features and a normal abdominal and cardiac exam"], "medium": ["Predominant extraesophageal symptoms such as chronic dry cough or morning hoarseness without much heartburn", "Chest pain that requires excluding acute coronary syndrome before attributing it to reflux", "Pregnancy with third-trimester heartburn and limited medication options", "Symptoms that persist despite lifestyle changes and require a proton pump inhibitor trial"], "hard": ["Older adult with new-onset symptoms and mild or atypical complaints, raising concern for complicated disease", "Long-standing reflux with progressive dysphagia suggesting a peptic stricture or malignancy", "Normal endoscopy with persistent symptoms, requiring pH monitoring to distinguish GERD from functional heartburn", "Overlap with peptic ulcer disease, biliary colic, or eosinophilic esophagitis in a younger patient with atopy"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
