ct with mild heart failure or ongoing pain; troponin moderately elevated, ECG shows ST elevation or significant ST depression, may need urgent reperfusion", "severe": "Large infarct with pulmonary edema, hypotension or significant arrhythmia; troponin markedly elevated, ECG shows extensive ST elevation or new bundle branch block", "critical": "Cardiogenic shock, ventricular fibrillation or mechanical complication such as papillary muscle rupture, ventricular septal defect or free wall rupture; troponin very high, patient in extremis"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "ST-segment elevation on ECG", "meaning": "Acute STEMI; needs immediate reperfusion therapy"}, {"finding": "Hypotension or cardiogenic shock", "meaning": "Large infarct or mechanical complication; needs urgent intervention"}, {"finding": "Ventricular tachycardia or ventricular fibrillation", "meaning": "Life-threatening arrhythmia; needs immediate defibrillation and advanced cardiac life support"}, {"finding": "New murmur or acute pulmonary edema", "meaning": "Mechanical complication such as papillary muscle rupture or ventricular septal defect"}, {"finding": "Persistent chest pain despite nitroglycerin", "meaning": "Ongoing ischemia; needs urgent reperfusion"}, {"finding": "Sudden severe tearing pain radiating to the back", "meaning": "Aortic dissection until proven otherwise"}], "complications": [{"name": "Arrhythmias", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Palpitations", "Syncope", "ECG shows ventricular tachycardia, ventricular fibrillation or heart block"]}, {"name": "Heart failure", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Dyspnea", "Crackles at lung bases", "Elevated jugular venous pressure", "S3 gallop"]}, {"name": "Cardiogenic shock", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Hypotension", "Cool extremities", "Oliguria", "Altered mental status"]}, {"name": "Papillary muscle rupture", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["New loud systolic murmur", "Acute pulmonary edema", "Hypotension"]}, {"name": "Ventricular septal defect", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["New harsh holosystolic murmur", "Thrill at the left sternal border", "Acute heart failure"]}, {"name": "Free wall rupture", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Sudden hypotension", "Distended neck veins", "Pulseless electrical activity", "Pericardial effusion with tamponade on echocardiogram"]}, {"name": "Left ventricular aneurysm", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Persistent ST elevation on ECG", "Heart failure", "Ventricular arrhythmias", "Echocardiogram shows dyskinetic segment"]}, {"name": "Left ventricular thrombus", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Anterior infarct", "Echocardiogram shows thrombus", "Risk of systemic embolism"]}, {"name": "Pericarditis", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Pleuritic chest pain", "Pericardial friction rub", "Diffuse ST elevation on ECG"]}, {"name": "Dressler syndrome", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Fever", "Pleuritic chest pain", "Pericardial effusion", "Elevated ESR and CRP"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "older_adults", "differences": "May present without chest pain, with sudden shortness of breath, weakness, confusion, fainting or a fall; higher mortality and more atypical symptoms."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Neuropathy can blunt pain, so the first sign may be dyspnea, nausea, sweating or hyperglycemia; higher risk of silent infarction."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Rare, but when it occurs it is often caused by coronary artery dissection or spasm rather than plaque rupture; symptoms may be attributed to normal pregnancy discomfort; radiation-based imaging should be avoided when possible."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Higher risk of contrast nephropathy; need careful hydration and consideration of alternative imaging or treatment strategies."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Chest discomfort may be vague and attributed to reflux or muscle strain; shortness of breath on exertion may be dismissed as being out of shape."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "What does the discomfort feel like, and where is it?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Pressure, squeezing, tightness or heaviness in the center of the chest behind the breastbone"}, {"question": "When did it start, and is it still there now?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Sudden onset more than 20 minutes ago and still present; ongoing pain means ongoing ischemia"}, {"question": "Does it spread anywhere?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Radiation to the left or both arms, neck, jaw, shoulder, back or upper abdomen"}, {"question": "Did it come on at rest or with exertion?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Often at rest or with mild activity; many wake up with it"}, {"question": "Does anything make it better or worse, like rest, nitroglycerin, position or breathing?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Rest and nitroglycerin help only partially or briefly; no change with position or breathing"}, {"question": "Are you short of breath, sweaty, nauseated or lightheaded?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Shortness of breath, cold clammy sweat, nausea or vomiting, dizziness"}, {"question": "Have you ever had chest pain, a heart attack, angina, a stent or bypass surgery?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Known coronary artery disease or prior myocardial infarction raises the probability sharply"}, {"question": "Do you have high blood pressure, high cholesterol, diabetes or kidney disease?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Major modifiable risk factors that support the diagnosis"}, {"question": "Do you smoke, or have you smoked?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Current or past smoking, a strong risk factor"}, {"question": "Has anyone in your family had a heart attack or sudden cardiac death, and at what age?", "category": "family", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Father or brother before 55, mother or sister before 65"}, {"question": "Do you use cocaine, methamphetamine or other stimulants?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Stimulant use, which can cause coronary spasm or thrombosis"}, {"question": "Is the pain sharp and worse when you take a deep breath or lie flat, or does pressing on your chest reproduce it?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Absent; these features point away from myocardial infarction toward pericarditis or chest wall pain"}, {"question": "Did the pain start suddenly and tear or rip through to your back?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Absent; tearing pain radiating to the back suggests aortic dissection, a must-not-miss look-alike"}], "accepted_names": ["Heart attack", "Myocardial infarction", "MI", "Acute myocardial infarction", "AMI", "STEMI", "NSTEMI", "Acute coronary syndrome"], "reasoning_points": ["Chest pressure lasting more than 20 minutes, often at rest, with radiation to the arm, neck or jaw", "Associated shortness of breath, cold diaphoresis, nausea or a sense of doom", "Cardiac risk factors such as age, smoking, hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, prior coronary disease or early family history", "ECG showing territorial ST elevation, ST depression or T-wave inversion, with reciprocal changes supporting infarction", "Rise and fall of serial high-sensitivity troponin above the 99th percentile confirming myocardial injury", "Echocardiogram showing a new regional wall motion abnormality in the territory of the infarct-related artery", "Alternative life-threatening causes excluded: aortic dissection, pulmonary embolism and pericarditis", "Recognition that ST elevation with symptoms is a STEMI needing immediate reperfusion without waiting for troponin"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Older man with classic pressure-like chest pain radiating to the left arm", "Cold diaphoresis and nausea with the pain", "Known coronary artery disease or prior myocardial infarction", "ECG shows clear territorial ST elevation with reciprocal depression", "Troponin markedly elevated on the first draw"], "medium": ["NSTEMI with ST depression or T-wave inversion rather than ST elevation", "Diabetic patient with blunted pain presenting with dyspnea, nausea and sweating", "Pain described as heartburn or indigestion rather than pressure", "Normal or nondiagnostic initial ECG requiring serial tracings and repeat troponin", "Woman or older adult with fatigue and shortness of breath as the main complaint"], "hard": ["Older adult presenting with confusion, weakness or a fall and no chest pain", "New left bundle branch block masking the ST changes", "Inferior infarct with vagal symptoms, bradycardia and hypotension", "Cocaine-associated coronary spasm or thrombosis in a younger patient", "Chest pain with a competing diagnosis such as aortic dissection, pulmonary embolism or pericarditis", "Troponin elevated from another cause such as kidney disease, heart failure or sepsis, requiring careful interpretation"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
