", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Vomiting", "Abdominal distension", "Inability to pass gas or stool", "High-pitched or absent bowel sounds"]}, {"name": "Perforation and peritonitis", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Rigid, tender abdomen", "Fever and hypotension", "Free air on imaging"]}, {"name": "Recurrence after repair", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Bulge reappears at the same site months to years after surgery", "Often associated with chronic straining or wound infection"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Umbilical hernias are common in infants and often close spontaneously by age 4–5; inguinal hernias in children are usually congenital and have a high risk of incarceration, so they are repaired promptly. Ultrasound is preferred over CT."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Higher risk of incarceration and strangulation; symptoms may be vague and the bulge may be hidden by obesity. Comorbidities increase operative risk."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Increased intra-abdominal pressure can worsen or reveal umbilical and inguinal hernias; the gravid uterus can mask the bulge. Ultrasound is the preferred imaging; surgery is generally deferred until after delivery unless complications occur."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "A thick abdominal wall can hide the bulge, so patients may report only pain or pressure. Imaging is often needed to confirm the diagnosis, and weight loss is recommended before elective repair."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "Where exactly do you feel the bulge or discomfort?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Groin or scrotum (inguinal), around the umbilicus (umbilical), or at a prior surgical scar (incisional)"}, {"question": "Does the lump come and go, and does it change when you stand up 