g, sharing utensils)", "strength": "moderate"}, {"text": "Immunosuppression (HIV, transplant, chemotherapy)", "strength": "moderate"}, {"text": "Occupational exposure (healthcare workers, contact sports such as wrestling)", "strength": "weak"}], "comorbidities": ["HIV infection or other immunosuppression", "Atopic dermatitis (eczema herpeticum)", "Prior history of genital or orolabial herpes"], "family_history": [], "social_history": ["Sexually active", "May have a partner with a history of cold sores or genital herpes"], "exposures": ["Direct skin-to-skin or mucosal contact with an infected person", "Sexual contact with an infected partner", "Kissing or sharing utensils with someone who has an active cold sore"], "risk_medications": ["prednisone", "tacrolimus", "cyclosporine", "methotrexate", "azathioprine", "mycophenolate"]}, "presentation": {"chief_complaints": [{"text": "I have painful blisters on my genitals", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "I have a tingling or burning sore on my lip", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "It hurts when I urinate", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "I feel like I have the flu", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "hpi": {"onset": "Primary infection: gradual over 1–2 days after an incubation period of about 2–12 days. Recurrent infection: abrupt, often preceded by tingling, itching or burning at the site for hours to a day.", "duration": "Lesions last 7–10 days in primary infection; recurrences are shorter, typically 3–7 days.", "progression": "Starts with localized tingling, burning or itching, followed by small grouped blisters that rupture and form painful ulcers, then crust and heal without scarring. Primary episodes are more severe and may be accompanied by systemic symptoms; recurrences are milder and shorter.", "location": "Orolabial: lips, mouth, face. Genital: vulva, vagina, cervix, penis, scrotum, buttocks, anus or thighs. Other sites: fingers (herpetic whitlow), eyes (keratoconjunctivitis), skin (eczema herpeticum).", "radiation": "Pain may radiate to the groin or legs with genital lesions; oral lesions may cause referred ear pain.", "quality": "Painful, burning, tingling, itchy; ulcers are tender to touch.", "severity": "Mild to severe; primary genital herpes can be very painful (7–10/10), while recurrences are usually mild to moderate.", "timing": "Continuous until healed; recurrences can be triggered by stress, illness, fever, sunlight, menstruation or trauma.", "aggravating": ["Urination (genital lesions)", "Touching or friction", "Eating or drinking acidic foods (oral lesions)", "Sun exposure (oral lesions)"], "relieving": ["Topical or oral analgesics", "Cool compresses", "Sitting in a warm bath", "Antiviral medication"], "associated_symptoms": [{"text": "Painful urination", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Swollen, tender lymph nodes in the groin or neck", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Fever", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Headache", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Muscle aches", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Malaise or fatigue", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Vaginal or urethral discharge", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "pertinent_negatives": ["No new medications or other trigger for the rash", "No history of similar lesions before (for primary episode)", "No joint pain or swelling", "No visual changes or eye pain (unless ocular involvement)", "No confusion or neck stiffness", "No recent travel or tick exposure", "No lesions elsewhere on the body"]}, "ros": [{"system": "constitutional", "positives": [{"text": "Fever", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Fatigue", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Muscle aches", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Weight loss", "Night sweats"]}, {"system": "skin", "positives": [{"text": "Painful blisters or sores", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "Tingling or itching before the sores appear", "frequency": "common"}], "negatives": ["Rash on the palms and soles", "Target lesions", "Honey-colored crusts"], "argues_against": ["Secondary syphilis", "Erythema multiforme", "Impetigo"]}, {"system": "genitourinary", "positives": [{"text": "Painful urination", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Vaginal or urethral discharge", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Blood in urine", "Flank pain", "Testicular pain"], "argues_against": ["Urinary tract infection", "Kidney stones", "Epididymitis"]}, {"system": "ent", "positives": [{"text": "Sores on the lips or inside the mouth", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Sore throat", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Difficulty swallowing", "Drooling"], "argues_against": ["Streptococcal pharyngitis", "Epiglottitis"]}, {"system": "neurologic", "positives": [{"text": "Headache", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Confusion", "Neck stiffness", "Seizures", "Focal weakness"], "argues_against": ["Herpes encephalitis", "Meningitis"]}, {"system": "eyes", "positives": [{"text": "Eye redness or pain", "frequency": "rare"}], "negatives": ["Blurred vision", "Photophobia"], "argues_against": ["Herpes keratitis"]}], "atypical": [{"population": "immunocompromised", "how_it_differs": "Lesions are more extensive, persist longer, may become chronic and can disseminate to multiple sites; systemic symptoms are more prominent.", "missing_classic_features": ["Self-limited course", "Localized lesions only"]}, {"population": "children", "how_it_differs": "Primary oral infection (gingivostomatitis) is common in young children and presents with fever, irritability, refusal to eat, and multiple vesicles and ulcers in the mouth and on the lips.", "missing_classic_features": ["Genital lesions", "Clear trigger for recurrence"]}, {"population": "pregnancy", "how_it_differs": "Primary infection near delivery carries a high risk of neonatal transmission; recurrent lesions are usually milder but still require careful management.", "missing_classic_features": ["Mild, self-limited course"]}, {"population": "older_adults", "how_it_differs": "May present with atypical orofacial pain or neuralgia without obvious vesicles (e.g., postherpetic neuralgia-like symptoms), and lesions may be less vesicular.", "missing_classic_features": ["Classic grouped vesicles"]}]}, "exam": {"general": {"mild": "Well-appearing, comfortable, no distress", "moderate": "Mild discomfort, may grimace when the affected area is touched or during urination", "severe": "Ill-appearing, visibly uncomfortable, reluctant to sit or walk due to genital pain", "critical": "Toxic-appearing, febrile, lethargic; consider disseminated disease or CNS involvement"}, "findings": [{"id": "grouped-vesicles", "region": "skin", "maneuver": "Inspect the affected skin and mucosa in good light", "finding": "Small grouped fluid-filled vesicles on an erythematous base", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "painful-ulcers", "region": "skin", "maneuver": "Inspect and gently touch any ruptured lesions", "finding": "Shallow, tender ulcers with a red base after vesicles rupture", "frequency": "common", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "orolabial-lesions", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Inspect the lips, oral mucosa, tongue and gingiva", "finding": "Vesicles or ulcers on the lips, gingiva, tongue or buccal mucosa; may be grouped", "frequency": "common", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "genital-lesions", "region": "genitourinary", "maneuver": "Inspect the vulva, vagina, penis, scrotum, perineum and perianal area", "finding": "Vesicles or ulcers on the external genitalia, perineum or perianal skin", "frequency": "common", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "inguinal-lymphadenopathy", "region": "lymph", "maneuver": "Palpate the inguinal lymph node chains bilaterally", "finding": "Tender, enlarged inguinal lymph nodes", "frequency": "common", "significance": "supports", "importance": "import