very little urine", "Flank pain"], "argues_against": ["Renal artery stenosis", "Glomerulonephritis", "Kidney stones"]}, {"system": "endocrine", "positives": [{"text": "Snoring or witnessed pauses in breathing at night", "frequency": "sometimes"}], "negatives": ["Episodes of pounding headache, sweating and racing heart", "Muscle cramps or weakness", "Unexplained weight gain with purple stretch marks"], "argues_against": ["Pheochromocytoma", "Primary aldosteronism", "Cushing syndrome"]}], "atypical": [{"population": "children", "how_it_differs": "Hypertension is uncommon in children and usually secondary to kidney disease, coarctation of the aorta or an endocrine cause; it is detected on routine screening or during workup of another problem, and the child is typically symptom-free.", "missing_classic_features": ["Adult pattern of essential hypertension", "Long-standing asymptomatic course"]}, {"population": "older_adults", "how_it_differs": "Isolated systolic hypertension with a wide pulse pressure is typical because arteries stiffen with age; orthostatic drops in pressure and postprandial dizziness are more common, and symptoms may be attributed to aging rather than to blood pressure.", "missing_classic_features": ["Elevated diastolic pressure", "Clear symptom attribution"]}, {"population": "pregnancy", "how_it_differs": "Elevated readings after 20 weeks with proteinuria, swelling or headaches suggest preeclampsia rather than chronic hypertension; blood pressure can fall in early pregnancy and rise again later, so a previously normal reading does not exclude the diagnosis.", "missing_classic_features": ["Stable chronic readings", "Absence of proteinuria or edema"]}, {"population": "diabetes", "how_it_differs": "The threshold for treatment is lower and target pressures are tighter; hypertension often coexists with diabetic kidney disease, so rising creatinine or protein in the urine may be the first clue.", "missing_classic_features": ["Isolated elevation of blood pressure without kidney involvement"]}, {"population": "obesity", "how_it_differs": "Often accompanied by obstructive sleep apnea, so daytime sleepiness, loud snoring and morning headaches are more prominent; weight loss alone can lower pressure substantially.", "missing_classic_features": ["Normal sleep pattern", "Absence of metabolic risk factors"]}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "how_it_differs": "Hypertension is frequently secondary to chronic kidney disease and is harder to control; patients may have swelling, fatigue and abnormal kidney labs rather than an isolated high reading.", "missing_classic_features": ["Normal kidney function", "Easy blood pressure control with one medicine"]}]}, "exam": {"general": {"mild": "Well-appearing, no distress; often overweight; comfortable at rest", "moderate": "Well-appearing but may be overweight with central adiposity; no acute distress", "severe": "May appear anxious or mildly uncomfortable; possible signs of chronic end-organ strain (mild dyspnea on exertion, ankle edema)", "critical": "Ill-appearing with signs of acute end-organ injury — severe headache, confusion, chest pain, dyspnea, focal neurologic deficits, or visual disturbance"}, "findings": [{"id": "elevated-bp-both-arms", "region": "extremities", "maneuver": "Measure blood pressure in both arms with the patient seated quietly for 5 minutes, using an appropriately sized cuff at heart level", "finding": "Elevated systolic and/or diastolic pressure, confirmed on repeat measurement", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "elevated-bp-repeat", "region": "extremities", "maneuver": "Repeat the blood pressure after 5 minutes of quiet rest and again at a separate visit", "finding": "Persistently elevated readings on separate occasions", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "normal-contralateral-bp", "region": "extremities", "maneuver": "Compare blood pressure in both arms", "finding": "Interarm difference less than 10 mm Hg", "frequency": "almost_always", "significance": "neutral", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "interarm-difference", "region": "extremities", "maneuver": "Compare blood pressure in both arms", "finding": "Interarm systolic difference of 10 mm Hg or more, suggesting peripheral arterial disease or aortic coarctation", "frequency": "sometimes", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "fundoscopic-changes", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Perform a fundoscopic exam", "finding": "Arteriolar narrowing, arteriovenous nicking, or flame hemorrhages and cotton-wool spots in severe cases", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "papilledema", "region": "heent", "maneuver": "Perform a fundoscopic exam", "finding": "Blurring of the optic disc margins (papilledema), indicating malignant hypertension", "frequency": "rare", "min_severity": "critical", "significance": "strongly_supports", "importance": "essential"}, {"id": "s4-gallop", "region": "cardiac", "maneuver": "Auscultate the heart at the apex with the bell", "finding": "Fourth heart sound (S4), reflecting a stiff, hypertrophied left ventricle", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "displaced-pmi", "region": "cardiac", "maneuver": "Palpate the apical impulse", "finding": "Laterally displaced and sustained apical impulse from left ventricular hypertrophy", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "carotid-bruit", "region": "neck", "maneuver": "Auscultate the carotid arteries", "finding": "Carotid bruit, suggesting atherosclerotic narrowing", "frequency": "sometimes", "min_severity": "moderate", "significance": "supports", "importance": "important"}, {"id": "jugular-venous-distention", "regi