ng and tachycardia, or marked blood pressure variability", "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden spells of severe headache, palpitations and drenching sweats", "Spells triggered by stress, exercise or abdominal pressure", "Family history of endocrine tumors"], "exam": ["Hypertension during a spell", "Pallor during the episode"], "labs": ["Elevated plasma free metanephrines or 24-hour urine metanephrines"], "imaging": ["Adrenal mass on CT or MRI", "Increased uptake on MIBG or DOTATATE imaging"]}}, {"name": "Cushing syndrome", "slug": "cushing-syndrome", "why_considered": "Hypertension with central obesity, purple striae and proximal weakness", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Weight gain around the trunk with thin arms and legs", "Easy bruising and proximal muscle weakness", "Use of glucocorticoid medications"], "exam": ["Violaceous abdominal striae", "Moon facies and dorsocervical fat pad", "Proximal muscle weakness"], "labs": ["Elevated late-night salivary cortisol or 24-hour urine free cortisol", "Failure to suppress cortisol on overnight dexamethasone suppression test", "Hyperglycemia and hypokalemia"], "imaging": ["Pituitary microadenoma on MRI", "Adrenal nodule or hyperplasia on CT"]}}, {"name": "Coarctation of the aorta", "slug": "coarctation-of-the-aorta", "why_considered": "Hypertension in a young patient with upper-limb hypertension and weak leg pulses", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Arm-to-leg blood pressure gradient or diminished femoral pulses in a young patient", "discriminators": {"history": ["Hypertension diagnosed at a young age", "Leg fatigue or cramping with exercise", "Known congenital heart disease"], "exam": ["Diminished or delayed femoral pulses", "Arm-to-leg systolic gradient", "Systolic murmur over the back or left infraclavicular area"], "labs": ["No specific laboratory abnormality"], "imaging": ["Narrowing of the aortic isthmus on CT or MR angiography", "Rib notching on chest x-ray from collateral vessels"]}}, {"name": "Medication-induced hypertension", "slug": "medication-induced-hypertension", "why_considered": "Elevated readings that began or worsened after starting a new medication", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Recent use of NSAIDs, decongestants, oral contraceptives, corticosteroids, stimulants or calcineurin inhibitors", "Temporal relationship between the drug and rising readings", "Alcohol or licorice use"], "exam": ["Elevated blood pressure without other specific findings"], "labs": ["Normal basic metabolic panel and urinalysis"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Stage 1 hypertension: systolic 130–139 or diastolic 80–89 mm Hg, no symptoms, no target-organ damage", "moderate": "Stage 2 hypertension: systolic 140–159 or diastolic 90–99 mm Hg, possible early target-organ changes such as left ventricular hypertrophy", "severe": "Systolic 160–179 or diastolic 100–119 mm Hg, often with target-organ strain such as retinopathy, diastolic dysfunction or proteinuria", "critical": "Hypertensive crisis: systolic above 180 and/or diastolic above 120 mm Hg; with acute end-organ injury this is a hypertensive emergency requiring immediate treatment"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Blood pressure above 180/120 mm Hg with chest pain, dyspnea, neurologic deficit or visual change", "meaning": "Hypertensive emergency with acute end-organ injury; needs immediate treatment"}, {"finding": "Sudden severe headache unlike any before", "meaning": "Possible intracerebral hemorrhage or hypertensive encephalopathy"}, {"finding": "Focal weakness, numbness, slurred speech or vision loss", "meaning": "Possible stroke or transient ischemic attack"}, {"finding": "Tearing chest or back pain", "meaning": "Possible aortic dissection"}, {"finding": "Papilledema on fundoscopy", "meaning": "Malignant hypertension; requires urgent blood pressure control"}, {"finding": "New confusion or reduced level of consciousness", "meaning": "Hypertensive encephalopathy or another acute neurologic emergency"}, {"finding": "Pulmonary edema with crackles and hypoxemia", "meaning": "Hypertensive heart failure; needs urgent treatment"}, {"finding": "Spontaneous hypokalemia with resistant hypertension", "meaning": "Suggests primary aldosteronism or another secondary cause"}], "complications": [{"name": "Left ventricular hypertrophy", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["S4 gallop", "Displaced apical impulse", "Voltage criteria on ECG", "Increased wall thickness on echocardiogram"]}, {"name": "Heart failure with preserved or reduced ejection fraction", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Exertional dyspnea", "Pedal edema", "Elevated jugular venous pressure", "Bibasilar crackles"]}, {"name": "Ischemic stroke", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Sudden focal neurologic deficit", "Slurred speech", "Vision loss"]}, {"name": "Intracerebral hemorrhage", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Sudden severe headache", "Vomiting", "Focal deficit with reduced consciousness"]}, {"name": "Coronary artery disease and myocardial infarction", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Exertional chest pressure", "Ischemic ECG changes", "Elevated troponin"]}, {"name": "Chronic kidney disease", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Rising creatinine", "Proteinuria", "Small echogenic kidneys"]}, {"name": "Hypertensive retinopathy", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Arteriolar narrowing", "Arteriovenous nicking", "Flame hemorrhages and cotton-wool spots", "Papilledema in malignant hypertension"]}, {"name": "Aortic dissection", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Tearing chest or back pain", "Interarm blood pressure difference", "Widened mediastinum on chest x-ray"]}, {"name": "Peripheral arterial disease", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Claudication", "Diminished distal pulses", "Carotid or femoral bruits"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Hypertension is uncommon and usually secondary to kidney disease, coarctation of the aorta or an endocrine cause; diagnosis uses age-, height- and sex-specific percentiles, and the child is typically symptom-free."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Isolated systolic hypertension with a wide pulse pressure is typical because arteries stiffen with age; orthostatic and postprandial drops in pressure are more common, and treatment must avoid excessive lowering that causes falls."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Elevated readings after 20 weeks with proteinuria, swelling or headaches suggest preeclampsia rather than chronic hypertension; ACE inhibitors, ARBs and thiazides are avoided, and blood pressure can fall in early pregnancy and rise again later."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "The threshold for treatment is lower and target pressures are tighter; hypertension often coexists with diabetic kidney disease, so rising creatinine or protein in the urine may be the first clue."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Often accompanied by obstructive sleep apnea, so daytime sleepiness, loud snoring and morning headaches are more prominent; weight loss alone can lower pressure substantially."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Hypertension is frequently secondary to chronic kidney disease and is harder to control; patients may have swelling, fatigue and abnormal kidney labs rather than an isolated high reading, and creatinine must be monitored after starting an ACE inhibitor or ARB."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "What were your blood pressure readings, and where were they taken?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Repeated office readings of 130/80 mm Hg or higher, or home readings that are consistently elevated; a single high reading is not enough"}, {"question": "How long have your pressures been high, and when were they first noted?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Months to years of elevated readings; a recent abrupt rise suggests a secondary cause"}, {"question": "Do you have any symptoms at all — headaches, dizziness, chest pain, shortness of breath, vision changes?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Usually none; the presence of chest pain, dyspnea, focal neurologic symptoms or visual loss suggests end-organ injury"}, {"question": "Do you check your blood pressure at home, and what do those readings show?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Home readings that confirm or refute office values; normal home readings suggest white-coat hypertension"}, {"question": "Are you taking any blood pressure medicine, and have you been able to take it as prescribed?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Current regimen, doses, adherence, and whether the patient has missed doses"}, {"question": "Do you take any over-the-counter pain relievers, decongestants, or other medicines?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "NSAIDs, pseudoephedrine or phenylephrine, oral contraceptives, corticosteroids, stimulants, or other agents that raise blood pressure"}, {"question": "Have you ever been told you have kidney disease, diabetes, or sleep apnea?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Conditions that are causes or consequences of hypertension and that change treatment targets"}, {"question": "Does anyone in your family have high blood pressure, early heart disease, or kidney disease?", "category": "family", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "A strong family history supports primary hypertension; early cardiovascular disease or polycystic kidney disease suggests other causes"}, {"question": "How much alcohol do you drink, and do you use tobacco or any stimulants?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Heavy or regular alcohol use, tobacco, or stimulant use that contributes to elevated pressure"}, {"question": "How much salt do you eat, and how often do you exercise?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "High dietary sodium and physical inactivity — modifiable contributors"}, {"question": "Do you snore loudly or has anyone seen you stop breathing at night?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Obstructive sleep apnea, a common secondary contributor"}, {"question": "Have you had spells of pounding headache with sweating and a racing heart?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Paroxysmal spells suggesting pheochromocytoma"}, {"question": "Have you had sudden weakness, numbness, trouble speaking, or loss of vision?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Stroke or transient ischemic attack — an emergency"}, {"question": "Have you had tearing chest or back pain, or chest pressure with exertion?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Aortic dissection or acute coronary syndrome"}, {"question": "Could you be pregnant? When was your last period?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Pregnancy must be excluded before starting an ACE inhibitor, ARB or thiazide, and before imaging that uses radiation"}], "accepted_names": ["High blood pressure", "Hypertension", "HTN", "HBP", "Essential hypertension", "Primary hypertension", "Benign essential hypertension"], "reasoning_points": ["Elevated blood pressure confirmed on repeated readings at separate visits, or by home or ambulatory monitoring", "The patient is usually asymptomatic, so the diagnosis rests on the numbers rather than on symptoms", "Office readings are interpreted against the ACC/AHA categories: normal below 120/80, elevated 120–129 systolic, stage 1 130–139 or 80–89, stage 2 140 or higher or 90 or higher", "A search for target-organ damage: left ventricular hypertrophy, retinopathy, kidney impairment, proteinuria, vascular disease", "A search for secondary causes when the presentation is atypical: young age, resistant hypertension, hypokalemia, abdominal bruit, spells, or a creatinine rise after starting an ACE inhibitor or ARB", "Medications and substances that raise blood pressure are reviewed and, where possible, stopped", "Global cardiovascular risk is estimated from lipids, glucose or A1c, smoking and kidney function", "Pregnancy is excluded before prescribing an ACE inhibitor, ARB or thiazide", "A blood pressure above 180/120 mm Hg with signs of acute end-organ injury is a hypertensive emergency, not an outpatient workup"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Middle-aged or older adult with repeated office readings of 150/95 mm Hg", "No symptoms and no target-organ damage", "Overweight, high-sodium diet, family history of hypertension", "Basic metabolic panel, urinalysis and lipids are unremarkable"], "medium": ["Borderline office readings with normal home readings — white-coat hypertension must be excluded", "Patient on an NSAID or decongestant that is raising the pressure", "Coexisting diabetes or chronic kidney disease that changes the treatment target", "Mild left ventricular hypertrophy on ECG or echocardiogram"], "hard": ["Young patient with severe hypertension and hypokalemia suggesting primary aldosteronism", "Resistant hypertension on three drugs including a diuretic", "Abdominal bruit or a creatinine rise after starting an ACE inhibitor suggesting renal artery stenosis", "Paroxysmal spells of headache, sweating and tachycardia suggesting pheochromocytoma", "Young patient with weak femoral pulses and an arm-