kin-to-skin genital contact with an infected partner"], "risk_medications": ["immunosuppressants", "corticosteroids"]}, "presentation": {"chief_complaints": [{"text": "I noticed some bumps down there", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "My doctor said my Pap test was abnormal", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "I have a lump or growth in my genital area", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "I have no symptoms, but my partner told me they have HPV", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "I have a sore or bump in my mouth or throat", "frequency": "rare"}], "hpi": {"onset": "Gradual; warts appear weeks to months after exposure, while high-risk HPV is usually detected only on screening and may have been present for years", "duration": "Warts may be present for weeks to months before evaluation; high-risk HPV infection is often longstanding and asymptomatic", "progression": "Warts may stay the same, grow in size or number, or resolve on their own; high-risk HPV usually clears without symptoms, but persistent infection can cause precancerous cell changes over years", "location": "Genital area (vulva, vagina, cervix, penis, scrotum), anus or perianal skin, and sometimes the mouth or throat", "radiation": "Usually none", "quality": "Small bumps or clusters that may be raised or flat, smooth or cauliflower-like; usually painless", "severity": "Mild; most people have no pain or systemic symptoms", "timing": "Warts are persistent and may slowly change over weeks to months; high-risk HPV has no symptoms", "aggravating": ["Friction or irritation of the area"], "relieving": ["None specific"], "associated_symptoms": [{"text": "No symptoms at all", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "Visible bumps or warts in the genital or anal area", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Itching or irritation around the warts", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Mild bleeding or discomfort with intercourse if warts are irritated", "frequency": "rare"}, {"text": "Abnormal cervical screening re