arachnoid hemorrhage or another vascular emergency and requires urgent imaging"}, {"finding": "Fever with neck stiffness or altered mental status", "meaning": "Suggests meningitis or encephalitis and requires urgent lumbar puncture and antibiotics"}, {"finding": "New focal neurologic deficit that does not resolve or does not spread gradually", "meaning": "Suggests stroke or a space-occupying lesion rather than migraine aura"}, {"finding": "New headache after age 50", "meaning": "Raises concern for giant cell arteritis, mass lesion, or other secondary cause"}, {"finding": "Headache that is worse when lying down or wakes the patient from sleep", "meaning": "Suggests raised intracranial pressure or a posterior fossa lesion"}, {"finding": "Progressive headache that worsens every day over weeks", "meaning": "Suggests a mass lesion, infection, or other secondary cause"}, {"finding": "Headache after head injury", "meaning": "Suggests intracranial hemorrhage or post-traumatic complication"}, {"finding": "Papilledema on fundoscopy", "meaning": "Suggests raised intracranial pressure, mass lesion, or venous sinus thrombosis"}, {"finding": "New headache in pregnancy or the postpartum period with aura or neurologic signs", "meaning": "Raises concern for preeclampsia, stroke, or cerebral venous sinus thrombosis"}, {"finding": "History of cancer, HIV, or immunosuppression with new headache", "meaning": "Raises concern for central nervous system infection or metastasis"}], "complications": [{"name": "Medication overuse headache", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Headache on fifteen or more days per month", "Regular use of acute headache medication more than two to three days per week", "Headache improves after withdrawal of the offending medication"]}, {"name": "Chronic migraine", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Headache on fifteen or more days per month for more than three months", "At least eight days per month with migraine features", "Progressive increase in attack frequency over months"]}, {"name": "Migrainous infarction", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Aura symptoms that persist beyond sixty minutes", "Focal deficit with corresponding ischemic lesion on diffusion-weighted MRI", "Usually occurs in a patient with migraine with aura"]}, {"name": "Status migrainosus", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Debilitating attack lasting more than seventy-two hours", "Persistent vomiting and dehydration", "Failure of outpatient acute treatment"]}, {"name": "Persistent aura without infarction", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Aura symptoms lasting more than one week", "No evidence of infarction on imaging"]}, {"name": "Migraine-associated vertigo", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Recurrent spontaneous vertigo lasting minutes to hours", "Personal or family history of migraine", "No alternative vestibular cause identified"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Attacks are often shorter and bilateral, and children may present with abdominal pain, vomiting, or vertigo rather than a clear headache; the diagnosis is clinical and imaging is reserved for red flags."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "New onset after age 50 is uncommon and should prompt evaluation for giant cell arteritis and structural causes; aura without headache and late-life migraine accompaniments are more frequent, and medication interactions are more likely."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Migraine often improves in the second and third trimesters; new headache with aura or neurologic signs in pregnancy or the postpartum period raises concern for preeclampsia, stroke, or cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, and triptans and NSAIDs should be used with caution."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Obesity is associated with higher attack frequency and progression to chronic migraine; weight loss may reduce attack frequency."}, {"population": "hepatic_impairment", "differences": "Dose adjustment or avoidance of certain preventive and acute medications is needed because of reduced hepatic metabolism."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Dose adjustment or avoidance of certain acute and preventive medications is needed because of reduced renal clearance."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "Tell me about the headache — where is it, and what does it feel like?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Unilateral, throbbing or pulsating pain, often frontotemporal or behind the eye"}, {"question": "How long does an attack last if you don't treat it?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "4 to 72 hours; shorter attacks suggest cluster headache, longer constant pain suggests another cause"}, {"question": "Does the pain get worse when you move around, bend over, or cough?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Aggravation by routine physical activity, which supports migraine"}, {"question": "How bad is the pain at its worst, and does it stop you from working or studying?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Moderate to severe, disabling; mild pain that does not limit activity suggests tension-type headache"}, {"question": "Do light, sound, or smells bother you during an attack?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Both photophobia and phonophobia, which support migraine"}, {"question": "Do you feel sick to your stomach or vomit with the headache?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Nausea or vomiting; their absence makes migraine less likely"}, {"question": "Do you ever see flashing lights, zigzag lines, or blind spots before the headache?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Aura that develops gradually over at least five minutes and lasts five to sixty minutes"}, {"question": "Do you notice anything in the day before an attack, like yawning, food cravings, or mood changes?", "category": "associated", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "A prodrome, which supports migraine"}, {"question": "How often do you get these headaches, and how many days a month do you have any headache?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Attack frequency and total headache days; fifteen or more days a month suggests chronic migraine or medication overuse"}, {"question": "How often do you take something for the headache, and what do you take?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Use of acute medication more than two to three days per week, which suggests medication overuse headache"}, {"question": "What seems to bring an attack on — stress, missed meals, poor sleep, hormonal changes, certain foods or drinks?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recognized triggers such as stress, skipped meals, sleep disruption, menses, alcohol, or aged cheese"}, {"question": "Does your headache pattern follow your menstrual cycle?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Attacks around menses, which supports menstrual migraine and affects treatment choice"}, {"question": "Does anyone in your family get migraines or bad recurring headaches?", "category": "family", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "A first-degree relative with migraine, which is common and supports the diagnosis"}, {"question": "Have you ever had the worst headache of your life, or one that came on like a thunderclap?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Sudden severe onset reaching peak in seconds, which suggests subarachnoid hemorrhage"}, {"question": "Have you had a fever, stiff neck, confusion, or a seizure with this headache?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Signs of meningitis, encephalitis, or raised intracranial pressure"}, {"question": "Have you had new weakness, numbness, trouble speaking, or vision loss that did not go away?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "A fixed focal deficit, which suggests stroke or a structural lesion rather than aura"}, {"question": "Is this a new headache pattern for you, especially if you are over 50?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "New headache after age 50, which raises concern for giant cell arteritis or a mass lesion"}, {"question": "Does the headache wake you from sleep or get worse when you lie down?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Positional worsening, which suggests raised intracranial pressure or a posterior fossa lesion"}, {"question": "Have you had a head injury, cancer, HIV, or treatment that weakens your immune system?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Conditions that raise concern for hemorrhage, infection, or metastasis"}, {"question": "Do you have depression, anxiety, a sleep disorder, or epilepsy?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Comorbid conditions that are common with migraine and affect treatment"}], "accepted_names": ["Migraine", "Migraine headache", "Migraine disorder", "Migraine without aura", "Common migraine", "Migraine with aura", "Classic migraine"], "reasoning_points": ["Recurrent attacks of moderate to severe, often unilateral, throbbing headache lasting 4 to 72 hours", "Aggravation by routine physical activity", "Associated nausea or vomiting, or both photophobia and phonophobia", "Aura, when present, develops gradually over at least five minutes, lasts five to sixty minutes, and is fully reversible", "A personal or family history of similar recurrent headaches", "Normal neurologic examination between and during attacks", "Red flags for a secondary headache are absent, so the diagnosis is clinical and imaging is not routine", "Alternative diagnoses such as tension-type headache, cluster headache, and medication overuse headache are considered"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Young woman with a long history of recurrent unilateral throbbing headaches", "Clear nausea, photophobia, and phonophobia with each attack", "Attacks triggered by stress, missed meals, or menses and relieved by a dark quiet room", "Family history of migraine and a normal neurologic exam"], "medium": ["Bilateral headache in a child or adolescent with abdominal pain and vomiting", "Migraine with aura that must be distinguished from a transient ischemic attack", "Coexisting tension-type headache with a mixed headache pattern", "Frequent attacks with daily analgesic use suggesting medication overuse headache"], "hard": ["New headache after age 50 requiring evaluation for giant cell arteritis", "Aura without headache in an older adult", "Headache in pregnancy or the postpartum period raising concern for preeclampsia, stroke, or cerebral venous sinus thrombosis", "Atypical features such as positional worsening or a progressive daily pattern that require imaging", "Chronic migraine with fifteen or more headache days per month"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
