on or advanced endometriosis."}, {"test_id": "ua_le", "qualitative": {"moderate": "Trace", "severe": "1+"}, "frequency": "rare", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Mild sterile pyuria can occur from contamination or adjacent inflammation; a positive nitrite or heavy leukocyte esterase with symptoms points to urinary infection instead."}, {"test_id": "ua_nitrite", "qualitative": {"moderate": "Positive"}, "frequency": "rare", "importance": "optional", "caveat": "Positive nitrite argues against dysmenorrhea and toward a urinary tract infection."}], "imaging": [{"study": "Transvaginal pelvic ultrasound", "modality": "ultrasound", "preferred_when": "First-line imaging in adults; best resolution for the uterus and adnexa", "findings": [{"text": "Normal uterus and ovaries with no structural abnormality (primary dysmenorrhea)", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Enlarged, heterogeneous, or globular uterus with myometrial cysts or asymmetric wall thickening (adenomyosis)", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Well-circumscribed hypoechoic myometrial masses (uterine fibroids)", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Endometrioma — a homogeneous low-level internal echo ovarian cyst", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Free pelvic fluid", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Thickened or fluid-filled fallopian tube or tubo-ovarian complex (pelvic inflammatory disease)", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "Transabdominal pelvic ultrasound", "modality": "ultrasound", "preferred_when": "Patients who have not been sexually active, or when transvaginal imaging is declined or not possible", "findings": [{"text": "Normal uterus and ovaries", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Large fibroids or adnexal masses may be seen; smaller lesions are often missed", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Distended bladder required as an acoustic window", "frequency": "almost_always"}]}, {"study": "MRI pelvis without contrast", "modality": "mri", "preferred_when": "When ultrasound is nondiagnostic and adenomyosis or deep endometriosis is suspected", "findings": [{"text": "Junctional zone thickening and myometrial cysts (adenomyosis)", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Deep infiltrating endometriosis involving the uterosacral ligaments, rectum, or bladder", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Fibroids characterized by location and size", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "Diagnostic laparoscopy", "modality": "endoscopy", "preferred_when": "When endometriosis is strongly suspected and imaging is unrevealing, or when symptoms persist despite treatment", "findings": [{"text": "Endometrial implants on the peritoneum, ovaries, or pelvic sidewalls", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Adhesions or endometriomas", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Normal-appearing pelvis (primary dysmenorrhea)", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast", "modality": "ct", "findings": [{"text": "Usually normal or nonspecific in dysmenorrhea; not a first-line test", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "May incidentally show fibroids, adnexal masses, or free fluid", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}], "workup": {"initial": [{"test": "hcg", "why": "Rules out pregnancy and ectopic pregnancy in anyone of reproductive age with pelvic pain"}, {"test": "cbc", "why": "Checks for anemia from heavy menstrual bleeding and for leukocytosis suggesting infection"}, {"test": "ua", "why": "Screens for urinary tract infection or stone, a common mimic of pelvic pain"}, {"test": "Transvaginal pelvic ultrasound", "why": "First-line imaging to look for fibroids, adenomyosis, endometriomas, or other structural causes", "only_if": "pain is severe, new after age 25, worsening, or not responding to NSAIDs"}, {"test": "Transabdominal pelvic ultrasound", "why": "Alternative when transvaginal imaging is not possible", "only_if": "patient has not been sexually active or declines transvaginal study"}], "confirmatory": [{"test": "Diagnostic laparoscopy", "why": "Definitive for endometriosis or adhesions when imaging is unrevealing and symptoms persist", "only_if": "secondary dysmenorrhea is strongly suspected and imaging is nondiagnostic"}], "supporting": [{"test": "ferritin", "why": "Confirms iron deficiency from heavy bleeding", "only_if": "heavy menstrual bleeding or anemia is present"}, {"test": "crp", "why": "Supports pelvic inflammatory disease when infection is suspected", "only_if": "fever, purulent discharge, or cervical motion tenderness is present"}, {"test": "esr", "why": "Adds to the inflammatory picture when infection or advanced endometriosis is suspected", "only_if": "infection or deep endometriosis is suspected"}, {"test": "MRI pelvis without contrast", "why": "Characterizes adenomyosis or deep endometriosis when ultrasound is inconclusive", "only_if": "ultrasound is nondiagnostic and adenomyosis or deep endometriosis is suspected"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast", "why": "Not a first-line test for dysmenorrhea; exposes the patient to radiation without adding diagnostic value"}, {"test": "Diagnostic laparoscopy", "why": "Invasive; not needed for typical primary dysmenorrhea that responds to NSAIDs"}, {"test": "d_dimer", "why": "No role in cyclical pelvic pain"}, {"test": "lipase", "why": "Not needed unless pain is epigastric or radiates to the back"}, {"test": "Abdominal x-ray", "why": "Rarely changes management of pelvic pain"}], "decision_points": ["Pregnancy test before any imaging or treatment in anyone who could be pregnant", "Pelvic exam and ultrasound when pain is new after age 25, worsening, or not responding to NSAIDs", "Fever, purulent discharge, or cervical motion tenderness prompts testing for pelvic inflammatory disease", "Sudden severe one-sided pain with a positive or unknown pregnancy status is an emergency — evaluate for ectopic pregnancy"], "criteria": [], "scores": []}, "differential": [{"name": "Endometriosis", "slug": "endometriosis", "why_considered": "Cyclical pelvic pain that is severe, worsens over time, or begins later in life is often caused by endometriosis rather than primary dysmenorrhea.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Pain that is progressively worse each cycle, deep pain with intercourse, or pain that continues after the period ends", "discriminators": {"history": ["Pain that has been getting steadily worse over months to years", "Deep pain with intercourse", "Pain with bowel movements or urination during the period", "Pain that begins before the period and continues after it ends", "Infertility or difficulty conceiving"], "exam": ["Tenderness or nodularity of the uterosacral ligaments or posterior fornix", "Fixed, retroverted uterus", "Adnexal mass or tenderness"], "labs": ["CA-125 may be elevated but is not diagnostic"], "imaging": ["Endometrioma — a homogeneous low-level internal echo ovarian cyst on ultrasound", "Deep infiltrating implants involving the uterosacral ligaments, rectum, or bladder on MRI", "Normal-appearing pelvis on imaging does not exclude the diagnosis"]}}, {"name": "Uterine fibroids", "slug": "uterine-fibroids", "why_considered": "Fibroids are a common cause of secondary dysmenorrhea, especially with heavy or prolonged bleeding.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Heavy or prolonged menstrual bleeding", "Pelvic pressure, fullness, or bloating", "Urinary frequency from mass effect", "Pain that is more constant or pressure-like than purely cramping"], "exam": ["Enlarged, irregular, or nodular uterus", "Palpable pelvic mass"], "labs": ["Anemia or low ferritin from chronic heavy bleeding"], "imaging": ["Well-circumscribed hypoechoic myometrial masses on ultrasound", "Fibroids characterized by location and size on MRI"]}}, {"name": "Adenomyosis", "slug": "adenomyosis", "why_considered": "Adenomyosis causes heavy, painful periods and an enlarged, tender uterus, and is easily mistaken for primary dysmenorrhea.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Heavy menstrual bleeding with progressively worsening cramps", "Pain that persists beyond the period", "Onset later in reproductive life, often after age 30", "History of uterine surgery or childbirth"], "exam": ["Diffusely enlarged, boggy, and tender uterus"], "labs": ["Anemia from heavy bleeding"], "imaging": ["Enlarged, heterogeneous, or globular uterus with myometrial cysts or asymmetric wall thickening on ultrasound", "Junctional zone thickening and myometrial cysts on MRI"]}}, {"name": "Pelvic inflammatory disease", "slug": "pelvic-inflammatory-disease", "why_considered": "Lower abdominal pain in a sexually active patient can be infectious rather than menstrual, and delaying treatment risks infertility.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Fever, purulent vaginal discharge, or cervical motion tenderness with pelvic pain", "discriminators": {"history": ["Vaginal discharge, often purulent or foul-smelling", "New or multiple sexual partners", "Fever and chills", "Pain that is not strictly cyclical and may be worse with movement"], "exam": ["Cervical motion tenderness", "Adnexal tenderness or mass", "Fever"], "labs": ["Leukocytosis", "Elevated CRP or ESR", "Positive nucleic acid testing for gonorrhea or chlamydia"], "imaging": ["Thickened or fluid-filled fallopian tube or tubo-ovarian complex on ultrasound"]}}, {"name": "Ovarian cyst rupture or torsion", "slug": "ovarian-torsion", "why_considered": "Sudden severe one-sided pelvic pain can be a gynecologic emergency rather than ordinary period cramps.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Abrupt onset of severe unilateral pelvic pain, especially with vomiting at the start", "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden onset of severe one-sided pain", "Vomiting at the onset of pain", "Known ovarian cyst", "Pain not relieved by NSAIDs or heat"], "exam": ["Unilateral adnexal tenderness or mass", "Peritoneal signs if rupture has occurred"], "labs": ["Falling hemoglobin if significant hemoperitoneum"], "imaging": ["Enlarged ovary with reduced Doppler flow on ultrasound (torsion)", "Free pelvic fluid or hemoperitoneum (rupture)"]}}, {"name": "Ectopic pregnancy", "slug": "ectopic-pregnancy", "why_considered": "Any reproductive-age patient with pelvic pain could have an ectopic pregnancy, which is life-threatening if missed.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Any patient who could be pregnant with pelvic pain", "discriminators": {"history": ["Missed period", "Vaginal bleeding or spotting", "Unilateral pelvic pain", "Shoulder pain or syncope suggesting intra-abdominal bleeding"], "exam": ["Adnexal tenderness or mass", "Peritoneal signs", "Hypotension or tachycardia"], "labs": ["Positive hCG", "Suboptimal rise in serial hCG"], "imaging": ["No intrauterine pregnancy on transvaginal ultrasound", "Adnexal mass or free fluid"]}}, {"name": "Premenstrual syndrome", "slug": "premenstrual-syndrome", "why_considered": "PMS causes cyclical symptoms that patients may describe as period pain, but the timing and nature of symptoms differ.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Symptoms begin one to two weeks before the period and resolve with bleeding", "Bloating, breast tenderness, irritability, and fatigue predominate", "Pain is not the primary complaint"], "exam": ["Normal pelvic examination"], "labs": ["No specific laboratory abnormality"], "imaging": ["Normal pelvic ultrasound"]}}, {"name": "Urinary tract infection", "slug": "urinary-tract-infection", "why_considered": "Lower abdominal pain and discomfort can be urinary rather than menstrual, especially if symptoms are not strictly cyclical.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Dysuria, urgency, and frequency", "Suprapubic discomfort not tied to the menstrual cycle", "No cyclical pattern"], "exam": ["Suprapubic tenderness", "No cervical motion tenderness"], "labs": ["Positive leukocyte esterase and nitrite on urinalysis", "Pyuria or bacteriuria"], "imaging": ["Not routinely indicated"]}}, {"name": "Irritable bowel syndrome", "slug": "irritable-bowel-syndrome", "why_considered": "IBS can cause cramping lower abdominal pain and changes in bowel habits that overlap with menstrual cramps.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Pain related to bowel movements or changes in stool form", "Bloating and alternating constipation and diarrhea", "Symptoms not strictly limited to the menstrual period"], "exam": ["Mild diffuse abdominal tenderness without peritoneal signs", "Normal pelvic examination"], "labs": ["Normal CBC, CRP, and celiac serology"], "imaging": ["Normal pelvic ultrasound"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Cramping that is noticeable but does not limit activities; responds to heat or a single dose of an NSAID; no missed school or work", "moderate": "Cramping that interferes with daily activities, requires scheduled NSAIDs, and may cause missed school or work; associated nausea, headache, or back pain", "severe": "Incapacitating pain despite NSAIDs, with vomiting, diarrhea, or syncope; unable to function for one or more days each cycle; raises concern for secondary dysmenorrhea", "critical": "Not applicable — primary dysmenorrhea itself is not life-threatening; critical presentations suggest a different diagnosis such as ectopic pregnancy, ovarian torsion, or pelvic infection"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Fever or chills with pelvic pain", "meaning": "Suggests pelvic inflammatory disease or another infection; needs prompt evaluation and antibiotics"}, {"finding": "Sudden severe one-sided pelvic pain", "meaning": "Suggests ovarian torsion, ruptured cyst, or ectopic pregnancy; a surgical emergency"}, {"finding": "Positive or unknown pregnancy test with pelvic pain", "meaning": "Ectopic pregnancy until proven otherwise"}, {"finding": "Pain or bleeding between periods, or pain that continues after the period ends", "meaning": "Suggests secondary dysmenorrhea such as endometriosis, adenomyosis, or fibroids"}, {"finding": "New severe cramps after age 25", "meaning": "Suggests secondary rather than primary dysmenorrhea; warrants pelvic exam and imaging"}, {"finding": "Pain that is progressively worsening over months", "meaning": "Suggests an underlying structural or inflammatory cause"}, {"finding": "Foul-smelling vaginal discharge", "meaning": "Suggests pelvic inflammatory disease or another genital infection"}, {"finding": "Syncope or shoulder pain with pelvic pain", "meaning": "Suggests intra-abdominal bleeding, as in a ruptured ectopic pregnancy"}], "complications": [{"name": "Iron deficiency anemia from heavy menstrual bleeding", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Fatigue and pallor", "Low hemoglobin or ferritin", "Heavy or prolonged periods"]}, {"name": "Missed school or work and reduced quality of life", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Absences during periods", "Avoidance of activities", "Anxiety or low mood related to cycles"]}, {"name": "Progression of unrecognized secondary dysmenorrhea", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Worsening pain over time", "Infertility", "Pelvic mass or abnormal imaging"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Adolescents may describe the pain vaguely as a stomachache and miss school rather than report cramps; primary dysmenorrhea usually starts within 6–12 months of menarche once ovulatory cycles are established."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "New or worsening severe period pain after age 25 suggests secondary dysmenorrhea from endometriosis, fibroids, or adenomyosis rather than primary dysmenorrhea."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Dysmenorrhea does not occur during pregnancy; pelvic pain in pregnancy must be evaluated for ectopic pregnancy, miscarriage, or other obstetric causes."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Obesity is associated with heavier bleeding and more painful periods, and can make pelvic examination and imaging less straightforward."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "When did the pain start in relation to your period, and how long does it last?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Pain starting a day or two before or on the first day of bleeding and lasting 1–3 days, recurring each cycle"}, {"question": "Where is the pain, and does it spread anywhere?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Lower abdominal midline cramping that may radiate to the lower back or inner thighs"}, {"question": "What does the pain feel like, and how bad is it on a 0–10 scale?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Cramping, throbbing, or aching pain that comes in waves, often 5–8/10 at its worst"}, {"question": "What makes the pain better or worse?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Worse on the first day or two of bleeding and with activity; better with heat, NSAIDs, rest, or hormonal birth control"}, {"question": "Does the pain interfere with school, work, or your usual activities?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Missed school or work and reduced daily functioning during the period"}, {"question": "How old were you when your periods started, and when did the painful periods begin?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Pain beginning within 6–12 months of menarche suggests primary dysmenorrhea; new pain after age 25 suggests a secondary cause"}, {"question": "Has the pain been getting worse over time, or is it about the same each cycle?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Stable cyclical pain suggests primary dysmenorrhea; steadily worsening pain suggests endometriosis, adenomyosis, or fibroids"}, {"question": "Do you have pain or bleeding between periods, or pain that continues after your period ends?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Present symptoms suggest secondary dysmenorrhea rather than primary"}, {"question": "Do you have pain with intercourse or with bowel movements during your period?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Deep dyspareunia or dyschezia suggests endometriosis"}, {"question": "How heavy are your periods, and how long do they last?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Heavy or prolonged bleeding suggests fibroids, adenomyosis, or iron deficiency anemia"}, {"question": "Do you have nausea, diarrhea, headache, or back pain with your periods?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Nausea, diarrhea, headache, and lower back pain commonly accompany dysmenorrhea"}, {"question": "Do you have a fever, chills, or foul-smelling vaginal discharge with the pain?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Present symptoms suggest pelvic inflammatory disease or another infection"}, {"question": "Could you be pregnant? When was your last period?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Missed period or possible pregnancy raises concern for ectopic pregnancy"}, {"question": "Have you ever had sudden severe one-sided pelvic pain?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Abrupt severe unilateral pain suggests ovarian torsion, ruptured cyst, or ectopic pregnancy"}, {"question": "What have you tried for the pain, and does it help?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "NSAIDs started at the first sign of cramps, heat, rest, exercise, or hormonal birth control that relieves symptoms"}, {"question": "Have you been pregnant or given birth before?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Pain often lessens after childbirth; nulliparity is a weak risk factor"}, {"question": "Does anyone in your family have painful periods or endometriosis?", "category": "family", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Family history of painful periods or endometriosis in a first-degree relative"}, {"question": "Do you smoke, drink alcohol, or use any drugs?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Smoking is a weak risk factor; alcohol and tobacco may worsen symptoms"}, {"question": "Are you sexually active, and do you use contraception?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Sexual history guides pregnancy testing, pelvic exam, and testing for sexually transmitted infections"}, {"question": "Have you had any abdominal or pelvic surgery?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Prior uterine surgery or childbirth is associated with adenomyosis"}], "accepted_names": ["Primary dysmenorrhea", "Dysmenorrhea", "Period pain", "Menstrual cramps", "Menstrual pain", "Painful periods"], "reasoning_points": ["Cyclical lower abdominal cramping that starts a day or two before or on the first day of bleeding and lasts 1–3 days", "Pain began within 6–12 months of menarche and has a stable, recurring pattern rather than worsening over time", "Associated nausea, diarrhea, headache, and lower back pain are common", "Pain is relieved by heat, NSAIDs started at the first sign of cramps, rest, or hormonal birth control", "No pain or bleeding between periods, no dyspareunia, no fever or purulent discharge, and no pain after the period ends", "Pelvic examination is normal with no cervical motion tenderness, adnexal mass, or enlarged tender uterus", "Pregnancy is excluded in anyone of reproductive age with pelvic pain", "Imaging is normal when obtained, supporting primary rather than secondary dysmenorrhea"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Teenager or young adult within a few years of menarche", "Classic cyclical cramping on the first day or two of the period", "Relieved by ibuprofen or a heating pad", "Normal pelvic examination", "No red-flag features"], "medium": ["Woman of reproductive age with heavy bleeding and anemia", "Pain that interferes with school or work despite NSAIDs", "Nausea, diarrhea, and headache prominent with the cramps", "Symptoms overlap with premenstrual syndrome", "Pelvic ultrasound needed to exclude fibroids or adenomyosis"], "hard": ["New severe cramps after age 25 suggesting secondary dysmenorrhea", "Pain that is progressively worsening over months", "Deep dyspareunia or dyschezia suggesting endometriosis", "Adolescent who describes the pain vaguely as a stomachache and misses school", "Pain that continues after the period ends or occurs between periods"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
