r feeds, which can be mistaken for reflux", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Forceful or projectile non-bilious vomiting in an infant around 3–6 weeks of age, especially with a hungry infant who feeds again after vomiting", "discriminators": {"history": ["Vomiting becomes forceful and projectile, often after every feed", "Infant remains hungry and wants to feed again right after vomiting", "Onset typically 3–6 weeks of age, not present from birth", "Progressive worsening rather than the waxing and waning of reflux"], "exam": ["Palpable olive-shaped mass in the right upper quadrant", "Visible gastric peristaltic waves across the upper abdomen", "Signs of dehydration and weight loss"], "labs": ["Hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis with low chloride", "Elevated bicarbonate on basic metabolic panel"], "imaging": ["Abdominal ultrasound showing thickened and elongated pyloric muscle", "Upper GI series with a string sign or double-track sign if ultrasound is equivocal"]}}, {"name": "Eosinophilic esophagitis", "slug": "eosinophilic-esophagitis", "why_considered": "Older child with reflux-like symptoms, trouble swallowing, or food impaction that does not respond to acid suppression", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Dysphagia, food impaction, or reflux symptoms that persist despite an adequate trial of acid-reducing medicine", "discriminators": {"history": ["Trouble swallowing solid foods or episodes of food getting stuck", "Personal or family history of asthma, eczema, or food allergy", "Symptoms do not improve with acid suppression"], "exam": ["Often normal examination", "May show signs of atopic disease such as eczema or allergic rhinitis"], "labs": ["Peripheral eosinophilia sometimes present", "Allergy testing may identify food triggers"], "imaging": ["Endoscopy shows esophageal rings, furrows, white plaques, or a narrowed caliber", "Esophageal biopsy with 15 or more eosinophils per high-power field"]}}, {"name": "Cow's milk protein allergy", "slug": "cows