heezing, chest tightness, and shortness of breath", "Symptoms triggered by exercise, cold air, or allergens", "Personal or family history of atopy"], "exam": ["Wheezing on auscultation", "Prolonged expiratory phase", "Improved air movement after bronchodilator"], "labs": ["Allergy testing or elevated IgE", "Spirometry showing reversible obstruction"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray usually normal or hyperinflated"]}}, {"name": "Tracheoesophageal fistula or esophageal atresia", "slug": "tracheoesophageal-fistula", "why_considered": "Feeding difficulties and coughing or choking with feeds in a newborn", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Coughing, choking, or cyanosis with the first feeds in a newborn, or inability to pass a nasogastric tube", "discriminators": {"history": ["Symptoms present from the first feed", "Coughing, choking, or cyanosis during feeding", "Polyhydramnios during pregnancy"], "exam": ["Respiratory distress", "Inability to pass a nasogastric tube", "Abdominal distension if a distal fistula is present"], "labs": ["Not diagnostic"], "imaging": ["Chest and abdominal x-ray showing a coiled nasogastric tube or air in the stomach", "Contrast study confirming the anatomy"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Occasional spitting up or mild heartburn fewer than twice a week; child is thriving, feeding well, and growing normally; no red flags.", "moderate": "Symptoms two or more times a week with discomfort, feeding refusal, or night-time cough; growth may be slightly affected but the child is not dehydrated or ill-appearing.", "severe": "Frequent symptoms with poor weight gain, esophagitis, feeding aversion, or respiratory symptoms; may require endoscopy and escalation of therapy.", "critical": "Not typical of reflux itself; consider a different diagnosis such as pyloric stenosis, obstruction, or sepsis if the child is ill-appearing, dehydrated, or has bilious or bloody vomiting."}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Bilious (green) or bloody vomit", "meaning": "Suggests intestinal obstruction, malrotation with volvulus, or GI bleeding; needs urgent evaluation"}, {"finding": "Projectile vomiting in an infant", "meaning": "Suggests pyloric stenosis or increased intracranial pressure"}, {"finding": "Poor weight gain or weight loss", "meaning": "Suggests inadequate intake, malabsorption, or a more serious underlying condition"}, {"finding": "Difficulty or pain with swallowing", "meaning": "Suggests esophagitis, stricture, or eosinophilic esophagitis"}, {"finding": "Respiratory distress, choking, or cyanosis with feeds", "meaning": "Suggests aspiration, tracheoesophageal fistula, or airway compromise"}, {"finding": "Lethargy, fever, or ill appearance", "meaning": "Suggests infection, dehydration, or another systemic illness"}, {"finding": "Onset of symptoms after starting a new medication", "meaning": "Suggests medication-induced reflux or another adverse effect"}], "complications": [{"name": "Reflux esophagitis", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Painful swallowing", "Heartburn that persists despite acid suppression", "Endoscopy showing erosions or erythema"]}, {"name": "Peptic stricture", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Progressive difficulty swallowing solid foods", "Food impaction", "Endoscopy showing narrowing"]}, {"name": "Barrett's esophagus", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Long-standing severe reflux", "Endoscopy with intestinal metaplasia on biopsy"]}, {"name": "Dental enamel erosion", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Recurrent acid exposure to teeth", "Dental examination showing enamel loss"]}, {"name": "Aspiration and worsening respiratory symptoms", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Chronic cough, especially at night", "Recurrent pneumonia", "Wheezing or hoarseness"]}, {"name": "Failure to thrive", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Poor weight gain", "Feeding refusal", "Crossing growth percentiles downward"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "infants", "differences": "Physiologic reflux is nearly universal and usually resolves by 12–18 months; infants present with spitting up, arching, irritability after feeds, and feeding refusal rather than heartburn. Red flags such as projectile or bilious vomiting should prompt evaluation for pyloric stenosis or obstruction."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "Young children may localize discomfort to the upper abdomen rather than the chest and may have recurrent vomiting or poor appetite instead of classic heartburn."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Increased abdominal pressure worsens reflux; symptoms are more frequent and more likely to persist, and weight management is an important part of care."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "What does your child do after feeding — do they spit up, arch their back, or seem uncomfortable?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Frequent spitting up or post-feed arching and irritability in an infant; heartburn or sour taste in an older child"}, {"question": "How often do the symptoms happen, and how long have they been going on?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Symptoms two or more times a week over weeks to months suggests GERD rather than occasional physiologic reflux"}, {"question": "When do the symptoms happen — after meals, when lying down, or at night?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Worse within 30–60 minutes after eating and when lying flat, which supports reflux"}, {"question": "Is the vomiting forceful or projectile, and what color is it?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Projectile or green (bilious) vomit suggests pyloric stenosis or obstruction, not simple reflux"}, {"question": "Is there any blood in the vomit or black or bloody stools?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "GI bleeding suggests erosive esophagitis or another cause and needs prompt evaluation"}, {"question": "How is your child growing — have they been gaining weight normally?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Poor weight gain or weight loss suggests a more serious condition or inadequate intake"}, {"question": "Does your child have trouble swallowing or pain when swallowing?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Dysphagia or odynophagia suggests esophagitis, stricture, or eosinophilic esophagitis"}, {"question": "Does your child have a chronic cough, hoarseness, or breathing problems, especially at night?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Nocturnal cough or hoarseness suggests reflux reaching the airway or an alternative diagnosis such as asthma"}, {"question": "What and how much is your child eating, and are there trigger foods?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Large meals, high-fat or spicy foods, and lying down after eating worsen symptoms"}, {"question": "Does anyone smoke in the home, or is your child exposed to secondhand smoke?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Secondhand smoke exposure is a modifiable risk factor for reflux"}, {"question": "Does your child have any other medical conditions, such as cerebral palsy, cystic fibrosis, or asthma?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Neurologic impairment, cystic fibrosis, or asthma increase the likelihood and severity of GERD"}, {"question": "Is your child taking any medicines, especially for asthma, allergies, depression, or pain?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Medications such as albuterol, prednisone, diphenhydramine, amitriptyline, or ibuprofen can worsen reflux"}, {"question": "Does anyone in the family have reflux or GERD?", "category": "family", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Family history of GERD supports the diagnosis"}], "accepted_names": ["Gastroesophageal reflux in children", "GER in children", "Gastroesophageal reflux disease in children", "GERD in children", "Pediatric GERD", "Reflux in children"], "reasoning_points": ["Age-appropriate presentation: infants with frequent spitting up, arching, and irritability after feeds; older children and teens with heartburn or sour taste", "Symptoms occur two or more times a week and are worse after meals and when lying down", "Symptoms improve with lifestyle changes such as smaller meals, staying upright after eating, and elevating the head of the bed", "Growth and hydration are preserved in uncomplicated reflux", "Red flags are absent: no bilious or projectile vomiting, no GI bleeding, no poor weight gain, no dysphagia, no respiratory distress", "Alternative diagnoses such as pyloric stenosis, eosinophilic esophagitis, cow's milk protein allergy, gastroenteritis, peptic ulcer disease, and asthma are considered and excluded", "Testing is reserved for red flags or failure of lifestyle and acid-suppression therapy"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Infant with daily spitting up after feeds who is otherwise thriving and growing normally", "Older child or teen with classic heartburn after large or fatty meals that improves with antacids", "Symptoms clearly worse when lying down and better when sitting upright", "No red flags on history or examination"], "medium": ["Infant with irritability and feeding refusal but normal growth", "Older child with chronic nocturnal cough attributed to reflux rather than asthma", "Symptoms that persist despite lifestyle changes and require a trial of acid suppression", "Overweight child or adolescent with frequent symptoms"], "hard": ["Infant with forceful vomiting concerning for pyloric stenosis", "Older child with dy