", "only_if": "altered mental status without an obvious cause"}, {"test": "Transthoracic echocardiogram", "why": "Not routine; reserved for suspected endocarditis or unexplained shock", "only_if": "new murmur, suspected endocarditis or unexplained shock"}], "decision_points": ["Draw blood cultures before starting antibiotics, but do not delay antibiotics to obtain them", "Start broad-spectrum antibiotics within the first hour once sepsis is suspected", "Measure lactate and repeat it to follow response to fluids", "Give 30 mL/kg of IV crystalloid for hypotension or lactate of 4 mmol/L or higher, then reassess", "Start vasopressors if the mean arterial pressure remains below 65 mm Hg after fluids", "Pregnancy test before CT in anyone who could be pregnant", "Look for and control the source: drain an abscess, remove an infected line or debride infected tissue", "Reassess antibiotic coverage daily and narrow once the organism and sensitivities are known"], "criteria": [{"name": "Sepsis (Sepsis-3)", "summary": "Suspected or confirmed infection plus an acute increase of 2 points or more on the sequential organ failure assessment score, which reflects new organ dysfunction.", "items": ["Suspected or documented infection", "Acute rise in organ dysfunction score of 2 points or more", "Organ dysfunction assessed by respiration, coagulation, liver, cardiovascular, neurologic and renal parameters"]}, {"name": "Septic shock (Sepsis-3)", "summary": "Sepsis with persistent hypotension requiring vasopressors to keep the mean arterial pressure at 65 mm Hg or higher together with a lactate above 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid resuscitation.", "items": ["Sepsis with a need for vasopressors to maintain mean arterial pressure at 65 mm Hg or higher", "Serum lactate above 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid resuscitation"]}, {"name": "Systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS)", "summary": "An older screening framework based on temperature, heart rate, respiratory rate and white count; it is sensitive but not specific and is no longer required to diagnose sepsis.", "items": ["Temperature above 38°C or below 36°C", "Heart rate above 90 beats per minute", "Respiratory rate above 20 breaths per minute or PaCO2 below 32 mm Hg", "White count above 12,000 or below 4,000 cells/µL, or more than 10% band forms"]}], "scores": ["qsofa", "sofa", "news2", "apache_ii"]}, "differential": [{"name": "Cardiogenic shock", "slug": "cardiogenic-shock", "why_considered": "Hypotension, tachycardia, tachypnea and altered mental status can look identical to septic shock, and both cause elevated lactate and organ dysfunction.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Hypotension with elevated jugular venous pressure, pulmonary edema or a new murmur rather than a fever source", "discriminators": {"history": ["Known heart failure, recent myocardial infarction or chest pain before the collapse", "No fever, chills or infectious symptoms at onset", "Orthopnea or paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea"], "exam": ["Elevated jugular venous pressure", "Cool extremities with pulmonary crackles and an S3 gallop", "New murmur suggesting valve rupture or endocarditis"], "labs": ["Markedly elevated troponin and natriuretic peptide", "Lactate elevated from hypoperfusion but no leukocytosis or left shift", "Blood cultures negative"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray showing pulmonary edema with a normal or enlarged heart", "Echocardiogram showing reduced ejection fraction, regional wall motion abnormality or valvular disruption"], "ecg": ["ST elevation or depression in a coronary territory", "New Q waves or bundle branch block"]}}, {"name": "Hypovolemic shock", "slug": "hypovolemic-shock", "why_considered": "Low blood pressure, tachycardia, cool skin and rising lactate occur with blood or fluid loss and can mimic sepsis when infection is also suspected.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Hypotension that improves rapidly with fluids in a patient with obvious bleeding, vomiting or diarrhea and no fever", "discriminators": {"history": ["Vomiting, diarrhea, poor intake or visible blood loss", "No fever or chills", "Trauma or gastrointestinal bleeding"], "exam": ["Flat neck veins and dry mucous membranes", "No source of infection on skin, lung, abdomen or urinary tract exam", "Rapid improvement in blood pressure with fluid bolus"], "labs": ["Normal or low white count without a left shift", "Elevated blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio", "Lactate falls quickly after fluids"], "imaging": ["Collapsed inferior vena cava on ultrasound", "No consolidations, abscess or hydronephrosis"]}}, {"name": "Pulmonary embolism", "slug": "pulmonary-embolism", "why_considered": "Sudden shortness of breath, tachycardia, hypoxemia and hypotension overlap with sepsis, and both can present with a normal chest x-ray.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Pleuritic chest pain, unilateral leg swelling or hypoxemia out of proportion to the lung exam in a patient without fever", "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden onset of dyspnea, often with pleuritic chest pain", "Recent immobility, surgery, travel or active cancer", "No fever or infectious symptoms"], "exam": ["Unilateral leg swelling or calf tenderness", "Clear lungs despite marked hypoxemia", "Loud P2 or signs of right heart strain"], "labs": ["Elevated D-dimer in low-risk patients", "Normal white count and no left shift", "Elevated troponin or natriuretic peptide from right heart strain"], "imaging": ["Filling defect in the pulmonary arteries on CT angiography", "Right ventricular dilation on echocardiogram"], "ecg": ["Sinus tachycardia", "S1Q3T3 pattern or right axis deviation", "Right bundle branch block"]}}, {"name": "Acute pancreatitis", "slug": "acute-pancreatitis", "why_considered": "Severe upper abdominal pain, vomiting, fever, tachycardia and hypotension can resemble an intra-abdominal source of sepsis.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Pain radiating to the back, relieved by leaning forward", "Heavy alcohol 