gulopathy are reviewed."}, {"name": "Large-vessel occlusion and thrombectomy", "summary": "CT or MR angiography showing occlusion of a proximal intracranial artery identifies candidates for mechanical thrombectomy, generally within 6 hours and up to 24 hours when perfusion imaging shows salvageable tissue."}], "scores": ["nihss", "abcd2", "cha2ds2_vasc", "aspects"]}, "differential": [{"name": "Hypoglycemia", "slug": "hypoglycemia", "why_considered": "Low blood glucose can cause focal weakness, aphasia, confusion or altered consciousness that closely mimics stroke.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Any patient with a focal neurologic deficit, especially with diabetes or on insulin or a sulfonylurea", "discriminators": {"history": ["Diabetes treated with insulin or a sulfonylurea", "Missed meal, recent dose increase, or unusual exertion", "Prior similar episodes relieved by eating", "Symptoms may fluctuate or improve with food"], "exam": ["Focal deficit that resolves after glucose correction", "Diaphoresis, tremor or palpitations", "Global confusion rather than a clean vascular syndrome"], "labs": ["Point-of-care glucose below 60 mg/dL", "Deficit improves within minutes of IV dextrose or oral glucose"], "imaging": ["No acute infarct or hemorrhage on head CT or MRI"]}}, {"name": "Seizure with postictal Todd paralysis", "slug": "seizure", "why_considered": "A focal seizure can leave a temporary one-sided weakness or speech difficulty that looks like a stroke.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Witnessed convulsion, tongue biting, or incontinence followed by focal weakness", "discriminators": {"history": ["Witnessed tonic-clonic or focal motor activity", "Tongue biting, urinary incontinence, or loss of consciousness at onset", "Known epilepsy or prior similar episodes", "Deficit follows the seizure rather than preceding it"], "exam": ["Postictal drowsiness or confusion", "Weakness that improves over minutes to hours", "No clear vascular territory pattern"], "labs": ["Elevated creatine kinase or lactate after a generalized seizure", "Prolactin may be elevated shortly after a generalized seizure"], "imaging": ["Head CT without hemorrhage", "MRI may show an old structural lesion or mesial temporal sclerosis rather than acute infarct"], "ecg": ["No atrial fibrillation"]}}, {"name": "Migraine with aura", "slug": "migraine-with-aura", "why_considered": "Visual, sensory or language aura can resemble a transient ischemic attack or stroke, especially in younger patients.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Recurrent, stereotyped episodes over years", "Gradual spread or march of symptoms over 5–20 minutes", "Positive phenomena such as flashing lights, zigzag lines or tingling", "Headache follows the aura", "Similar prior episodes with full recovery"], "exam": ["Normal neurologic exam between episodes", "No focal deficit at presentation"], "imaging": ["Head CT and MRI without acute infarct"]}}, {"name": "Bell palsy", "slug": "bell-palsy", "why_considered": "Acute one-sided facial weakness is a common stroke mimic.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Weakness isolated to the face", "No arm or leg weakness, numbness or speech difficulty", "May follow a viral illness", "Ear pain or altered taste"], "exam": ["Entire half of the face weak, including the forehead and eye closure", "Inability to raise the eyebrow on the affected side", "Normal strength, sensation and speech otherwise"], "imaging": ["Head CT or MRI without acute infarct"]}}, {"name": "Peripheral vertigo (vestibular neuritis or BPPV)", "slug": "vestibular-neuritis", "why_considered": "Sudden dizziness, nausea and unsteadiness can be mistaken for a posterior circulation stroke.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Severe spinning sensation with nausea and vomiting", "No focal weakness, numbness or speech difficulty", "Symptoms provoked by head movement in BPPV", "Gradual improvement over days in neuritis"], "exam": ["Direction-changing or unidirectional nystagmus that suppresses with fixation", "Normal strength, sensation, speech and coordination", "Negative HINTS exam in peripheral causes (no central pattern)"], "imaging": ["MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging shows no acute posterior circulation infarct"]}}, {"name": "Subdural hematoma", "slug": "subdural-hematoma", "why_considered": "Can present with focal weakness, confusion or headache, especially in older adults or after minor trauma.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Older adult, anticoagulant use, or recent fall with new focal deficit or confusion", "discriminators": {"history": ["Recent or remote head trauma, sometimes minor or unwitnessed", "Anticoagulant or antiplatelet use", "Headache, confusion or fluctuating symptoms over days", "Older age"], "exam": ["Focal deficit or altered mental status", "Signs of trauma such as scalp bruising", "Symptoms may fluctuate"], "labs": ["Coagulopathy or thrombocytopenia if on anticoagulants", "Anemia if significant bleeding"], "imaging": ["Crescent-shaped extra-axial collection on noncontrast head CT", "Midline shift or mass effect", "May be isodense and hard to see on CT if chronic; MRI more sensitive"]}}, {"name": "Brain tumor", "slug": "brain-tumor", "why_considered": "A mass lesion can cause focal deficits, seizures or headache that resemble stroke.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Gradual onset and progressive worsening over weeks", "Headache worse in the morning or with Valsalva", "New seizures", "No sudden onset at a definable moment"], "exam": ["Papilledema", "Focal deficit that does not fit a single vascular territory", "Cognitive or personality change"], "imaging": ["Mass lesion with surrounding edema and mass effect on CT or MRI", "Ring enhancement or heterogeneous enhancement after contrast", "Not confined to a vascular territory"]}}, {"name": "Conversion disorder", "slug": "conversion-disorder", "why_considered": "Functional neurologic symptoms can mimic stroke with weakness, sensory loss or speech changes.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden onset often in a setting of psychosocial stress", "Symptoms may vary or improve with distraction", "Prior similar episodes", "Inconsistent description of symptoms"], "exam": ["Give-way weakness or inconsistent effort", "Hoover sign positive", "Sensory loss that does not follow a dermatomal or vascular pattern", "Normal reflexes and no Babinski"], "imaging": ["Head CT and MRI without acute infarct or hemorrhage"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Small infarct or TIA with minor deficit (for example mild hand clumsiness or slight facial droop), NIHSS low, patient alert and able to walk and swallow safely", "moderate": "Clear focal deficit such as dense hemiparesis or aphasia, NIHSS moderate, patient needs help with some activities but airway and swallowing are intact", "severe": "Large territory infarct with dense hemiplegia, gaze deviation, neglect or severe aphasia, NIHSS high, risk of aspiration and cerebral edema", "critical": "Malignant edema with decreased consciousness, herniation, or hemorrhagic stroke with mass effect; may require intubation, osmotic therapy or decompressive surgery"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Sudden severe headache with neck stiffness or vomiting", "meaning": "Suggests subarachnoid hemorrhage rather than ischemic stroke"}, {"finding": "Decreasing level of consciousness or new coma", "meaning": "Large infarct with cerebral edema, herniation, or hemorrhagic conversion; urgent imaging and neurosurgical evaluation"}, {"finding": "New focal deficit in a patient on anticoagulation", "meaning": "Raises concern for intracranial hemorrhage; reverse anticoagulation and image immediately"}, {"finding": "Coughing or choking with oral intake, or a wet voice", "meaning": "Dysphagia with high aspiration risk; keep NPO until a swallow screen is passed"}, {"finding": "Irregularly irregular pulse or known atrial fibrillation", "meaning": "Cardioembolic source; changes secondary prevention to anticoagulation"}, {"finding": "Blood pressure above 185/110 mm Hg before thrombolysis", "meaning": "Must be controlled before IV thrombolysis; also raises concern for hemorrhagic stroke"}, {"finding": "Sudden painless monocular vision loss", "meaning": "Retinal ischemia or central retinal artery occlusion, a stroke equivalent requiring urgent evaluation"}], "complications": [{"name": "Hemorrhagic transformation of ischemic infarct", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Sudden neurologic worsening after initial stability", "New headache or vomiting", "Decreased level of consciousness", "Follows thrombolysis or large infarct"]}, {"name": "Cerebral edema with mass effect and herniation", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Large middle cerebral artery territory infarct", "Progressive drowsiness over 24–72 hours", "New pupillary asymmetry", "Headache and vomiting"]}, {"name": "Aspiration pneumonia", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Coughing or choking with swallowing", "Wet voice", "Fever and hypoxia in the first days", "New infiltrate on chest imaging"]}, {"name": "Deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Immobility and hemiplegia", "Unilateral leg swelling or calf pain", "Sudden dyspnea or hypoxia"]}, {"name": "Urinary tract infection", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Indwelling catheter", "Fever, cloudy urine or new confusion"]}, {"name": "Seizures", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Cortical involvement or hemorrhagic stroke", "New focal motor activity", "Postictal worsening of deficit"]}, {"name": "Post-stroke depression", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Persistent low mood, anhedonia or withdrawal weeks to months after stroke", "Poor participation in rehabilitation", "Sleep and appetite change"]}, {"name": "Vascular dementia and cognitive impairment", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Stepwise cognitive decline", "Multiple infarcts or strategic infarct location", "Impaired executive function and attention"]}, {"name": "Recurrent stroke", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Uncontrolled hypertension", "Untreated atrial fibrillation", "Carotid stenosis", "New sudden focal deficit during recovery"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "older_adults", "differences": "May present with confusion, falls or generalized weakness rather than clear focal deficits; aphasia can be mistaken for dementia; higher mortality and greater risk of aspiration and delirium."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "Stroke is rare but can present with seizures, altered mental status or isolated hemiparesis; risk factors include sickle cell disease, congenital heart disease and vasculopathy rather than atherosclerosis; imaging and thrombolysis decisions differ."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Risk is increased during pregnancy and the postpartum period; can present with headache, seizures or focal deficits that overlap with preeclampsia or eclampsia; imaging and thrombolysis decisions must account for the fetus."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Hypoglycemia can mimic stroke with focal deficits; glucose must be checked immediately and corrected before attributing deficits to stroke."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Contrast use for CT angiography and perfusion must be weighed against the risk of contrast-induced nephropathy; MRI and MR angiography may be preferred."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "When were you last known to be completely normal?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "A precise last-known-well time; this determines eligibility for IV thrombolysis (within 4.5 hours) and mechanical thrombectomy"}, {"question": "Did the weakness or numbness come on suddenly, or build up over hours to days?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Abrupt onset reaching maximum within seconds to minutes; gradual progression suggests a mimic such as tumor, infection or demyelination"}, {"question": "Which side of your body is affected, and is it the face, arm, leg, or all of them?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "A focal, one-sided pattern fitting a vascular territory (face and arm worse than leg suggests middle cerebral artery)"}, {"question": "Is your speech slurred, or are you having trouble finding words or understanding others?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Dysarthria or aphasia; aphasia strongly localizes to the dominant hemisphere and is a disabling deficit"}, {"question": "Have you had any sudden vision loss, double vision, or loss of part of your visual field?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Monocular vision loss (retinal ischemia) or homonymous hemianopia (posterior circulation or occipital infarct)"}, {"question": "Have you had any dizziness, trouble walking, or loss of balance?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Posterior circulation stroke; isolated vertigo with other brainstem signs is concerning"}, {"question": "Did you have a severe headache, vomiting, or neck stiffness with this?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Thunderclap headache or meningismus suggests subarachnoid or hemorrhagic stroke rather than ischemia"}, {"question": "Did you pass out, have a seizure, or bite your tongue?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Loss of consciousness or witnessed convulsion suggests seizure with postictal Todd paralysis rather than stroke"}, {"question": "Have you ever had a stroke or a transient ischemic attack before?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Prior stroke or TIA markedly raises recurrence risk and changes secondary prevention"}, {"question": "Do you have high blood pressure, diabetes, high cholesterol, or atrial fibrillation?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Major modifiable vascular risk factors; atrial fibrillation points to a cardioembolic source"}, {"question": "Do you take a blood thinner such as warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban, or dabigatran?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Anticoagulant use is a contraindication to thrombolysis and raises concern for hemorrhagic stroke"}, {"question": "Have you had any recent surgery, bleeding, head injury, or a fall?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Recent surgery or active bleeding excludes thrombolysis; head trauma suggests subdural hematoma"}, {"question": "Do you smoke, drink alcohol heavily, or use cocaine or stimulants?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Modifiable risk factors; stimulant use raises risk of hemorrhagic stroke"}, {"question": "Does anyone in your family have a history of stroke or early heart disease?", "category": "family", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Family history of stroke or premature cardiovascular disease"}], "accepted_names": ["Stroke", "Cerebrovascular accident", "CVA", "Ischemic stroke", "Acute ischemic stroke", "Hemorrhagic stroke", "Intracerebral hemorrhage", "Brain attack"], "reasoning_points": ["Abrupt onset of a focal neurologic deficit that reached maximum within seconds to minutes", "Deficit fits a single vascular territory (unilateral face, arm and/or leg, or a visual field)", "Vascular risk factors such as hypertension, atrial fibrillation, diabetes, smoking, or prior stroke or TIA", "Point-of-care glucose checked and hypoglycemia excluded as a mimic", "Noncontrast head CT excludes hemorrhage before any thrombolytic is given", "Last-known-well time established and thrombolysis or thrombectomy eligibility reviewed", "Atrial fibrillation or another cardioembolic source considered on ECG and echocardiography", "Mimics such as seizure with postictal Todd paralysis, migraine with aura, Bell palsy and subdural hematoma considered and excluded"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Older adult with hypertension and atrial fibrillation", "Sudden one-sided facial droop, arm weakness and slurred speech", "Symptoms present on waking or witnessed by a family member", "Noncontrast head CT shows no hemorrhage and an early infarct in a clear vascular territory"], "medium": ["Posterior circulation stroke presenting with vertigo, ataxia and double vision rather than hemiparesis", "Patient with diabetes whose focal deficit could be hypoglycemia until glucose is corrected", "Woman of reproductive age on oral contraceptives with a new focal deficit", "Small or early infarct with a normal noncontrast head CT, requiring MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging"], "hard": ["Older adult presenting with confusion or a fall rather than a clear focal deficit", "Patient on an anticoagulant with a new deficit, raising concern for hemorrhagic stroke", "Symptoms that resolve before arrival, suggesting TIA rather than completed stroke", "Stroke mimic such as seizure with postictal Todd paralysis, migraine with aura, or conversion disorder", "Large middle cerebral artery infarct with early decreased consciousness and risk of malignant edema"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
