d pain suggests peritonsillar abscess"}, {"question": "Have you had a fever or chills?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Fever supports infection; high fever with systemic illness raises concern for a bacterial cause or complication"}, {"question": "Do you have a cough or runny nose?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prominent cough and rhinorrhea point to a viral cause; absence of cough favors group A strep"}, {"question": "Have you noticed any white patches on your tonsils or bad breath?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Tonsillar exudate and halitosis support tonsillitis and are part of the Centor criteria"}, {"question": "Have you had any trouble breathing, drooling, or a muffled voice?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Any of these suggests airway compromise, epiglottitis, or peritonsillar abscess and requires urgent evaluation"}, {"question": "Can you open your mouth fully? Does it feel stiff?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Trismus suggests peritonsillar abscess or deep neck infection"}, {"question": "Has anyone around you had a sore throat or strep throat recently?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Close contact with strep supports a bacterial cause; sick contacts with cold symptoms support viral"}, {"question": "Have you had this before? How often?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Recurrent tonsillitis may prompt referral for tonsillectomy"}, {"question": "Are you taking any antibiotics or pain medicine for this?", "category": "medications", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Recent antibiotics may mask or modify the presentation; acetaminophen or ibuprofen use guides dosing advice"}, {"question": "Do you have any medication allergies, especially to penicillin?", "category": "allergies", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Penicillin allergy changes the antibiotic choice if strep is confirmed"}], "accepted_names": ["Tonsillitis", "Acute tonsillitis", "Bacterial tonsillitis", "Streptococcal tonsillitis", "Strep tonsillitis", "Tonsil inflammation"], "reasoning_points": ["Sore throat with painful swallowing, fever, and red swollen tonsils", "Tonsillar exudate and tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy on exam", "Absence of cough and runny nose favors a bacterial (group A strep) cause", "Rapid strep test or throat culture confirms or excludes group A streptococcal infection", "No drooling, stridor, trismus, muffled voice, or uvular deviation to suggest a deep neck infection or airway emergency", "Viral causes are managed with supportive care; bacterial causes require antibiotics"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Child or adolescent with sudden sore throat, fever, and painful swallowing", "Red swollen tonsils with white exudate and tender neck glands", "No cough or runny nose", "Positive rapid strep test"], "medium": ["Viral tonsillitis with cough and runny nose and a negative rapid strep test", "Adolescent with prolonged fatigue and posterior cervical lymphadenopathy suggesting mononucleosis", "Young child with fever, vomiting, and abdominal pain but no clear complaint of sore throat", "Scarlet fever with rash and strawberry tongue"], "hard": ["Severe one-sided throat pain with trismus and muffled voice suggesting peritonsillar abscess", "Drooling or stridor suggesting epiglottitis or airway compromise", "Immunocompromised patient with severe, slowly resolving tonsillitis", "Older adult with mild sore throat and low-grade fever where the diagnosis is less obvious"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
